window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Axioms, inference rules, and proofs", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-1", "text": "1.1.1 Axioms, inference rules, and proofs § axiom · rules of inference A proof separates two jobs: choosing assumptions and drawing consequences from them. The assumptions are axioms ; the permitted steps are rules of inference . From \\(P\\) and “\\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\),” for example, we may infer \\(Q\\). In the even-integer argument from the introduction, a reader can check each step without discovering the argument again. Make the notation and the permitted steps precise enough, and a machine can do that checking too. This is the connection to computation: a proof becomes a finite object that a procedure can inspect. Checking establishes that the conclusion follows from the chosen axioms. Whether those axioms capture what we intended is a separate question. Each arrow shows a dependency between proof steps. The same inference rule first gives \\(Q\\) from \\(P\\), then \\(R\\) from \\(Q\\). Problems From the axioms \\(P\\), “\\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\),” and “\\(Q\\) implies \\(R\\),” with modus ponens as the only rule of inference, write a proof of \\(R\\). Give the justification of each line. Prove that the sum of an even integer and an odd integer is odd, giving the justification for each step. A checker accepts every line of a proof. Show by an example that the conclusion can still be false, and say exactly what the accepted proof establishes."}, {"title": "Hilbert’s program and consistency", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-2", "text": "1.1.2 Hilbert’s program and consistency § consistent · finitary methods Writing rules explicitly lets us check whether an argument follows them. But are the rules themselves reliable? Could they allow us to prove both a statement and its opposite? A system that never does this is called consistent . Hilbert wanted to put mathematics on a secure foundation. First, its assumptions and proof rules would be made explicit. Then one would show that those rules could never produce a contradiction. He hoped to give this justification using reasoning about finite objects and concrete operations on them. These were called finitary methods . Even when a mathematical statement concerns infinitely many numbers, its written proof is finite. So, proofs and the rules for constructing them could themselves be studied. This was a question about the reliability of mathematical reasoning. A related question concerns its automation: once the rules are explicit, can a procedure always determine whether a statement has a proof? To see the difficulty, we must distinguish checking a proof from searching for one. Hilbert’s aim was to study the finite proofs allowed by a system and show that none could lead to a contradiction. Problems A system has the axioms \\(P\\) and “\\(P\\) implies not \\(P\\),” and the rule modus ponens. Is it consistent? Suppose a system also has the rule “from \\(A\\) and not \\(A\\), infer any statement \\(B\\).” Show that if the system is inconsistent, it proves every statement. Hilbert hoped to prove that systems are consistent. Show that a consistency proof carried out inside the same system \\(S\\) could not settle the doubt, by showing that an inconsistent \\(S\\) of the kind in the previous problem proves the statement “\\(S\\) is consistent.”"}, {"title": "Proof checking, proof search, and decision", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-3", "text": "1.1.3 Proof checking, proof search, and decision § proof search · decision procedure Suppose a proposed proof fails a check. We have rejected that argument, not its conclusion. Another argument may succeed. Now suppose no proof is supplied. If candidate proofs use a fixed finite alphabet and can be checked mechanically, list strings by length and test them. Every finite proof eventually appears. If a proof exists, this search finds one. But if no proof exists, there is no final candidate whose rejection settles the matter. The search simply continues. A decision procedure must do more: finish with a correct yes or no on every input. Could a cleverer procedure succeed? Failure of this search strategy does not rule out all strategies. To ask that larger question, we need a precise account of what a mechanical procedure is. That is why models of computation enter the story. Checking tests one proposed proof. Searching tries candidates until it finds a proof. Deciding must also finish when no proof exists. Problems Strings over the alphabet \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) are listed by length, starting with the empty string, and alphabetically within each length. Write the first seven strings, and give the position of \\(ab\\). A proof search lists candidate strings over a two-symbol alphabet in this order and checks each one. If the shortest proof of a statement has length \\(5\\), at most how many candidates are checked before a proof is found? Why can such a bound not be computed in advance for an arbitrary statement? Suppose a theory is consistent, its axioms can be listed by an algorithm, and it is complete: for every sentence \\(P\\), it proves \\(P\\) or it proves not \\(P\\). Show that searching for a proof of \\(P\\) and a proof of not \\(P\\) in alternation is a decision procedure for provability."}, {"title": "Algorithms and termination", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-1", "text": "1.2.1 Algorithms and termination § Euclid’s algorithm · invariant Take a task we can finish by hand: find the greatest common divisor of \\(48\\) and \\(18\\). Euclid’s algorithm repeatedly replaces a pair by its second entry and the remainder on division: \\[(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12)\\longrightarrow(12,6)\\longrightarrow(6,0).\\] It returns \\(6\\). The example suggests a rule, but two questions remain: why is the answer right, and why must the procedure stop? Write \\(a=qb+r\\), with \\(0\\leq r invariant: it stays fixed while the pair changes. Meanwhile the nonnegative second entry strictly decreases. It cannot do that forever. Eventually the pair is \\((g,0)\\), whose greatest common divisor is \\(g\\), so we return it. These are separate arguments. Repeating the original pair forever would preserve the correct answer but never deliver it. An algorithm needs both a reason to trust its result and a reason to expect that result. The greatest common divisor remains six throughout this run. The second entry decreases through nonnegative integers until it is zero. These are separate reasons for correctness and termination. Problems Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((91,35)\\), writing each division and each pair. Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((21,13)\\). How many divisions does it take, and why does this pair make the second entry decrease slowly? Give a procedure on pairs of positive integers that keeps the greatest common divisor unchanged at every step but never stops, and another that always stops but can return a wrong answer. What does this show about the two arguments for Euclid’s algorithm?"}, {"title": "Configurations and transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-2", "text": "1.2.2 Configurations and transitions § configuration · transition · run Suppose we pause Euclid’s algorithm at the pair \\((18,12)\\), just before the next division. What must we record in order to continue? We need the pair and the instruction that comes next. We do not need the earlier pairs. The information needed to resume a computation is called its configuration . What must we include? That depends on where we allow the computation to pause. At the start of Euclid’s repeated step, we need only the pair: the next instruction is already fixed. Halfway through a division, the pair alone would not be enough. We would also need the temporary values and a record of how far the division had progressed. A transition is one step from a configuration to the next. Starting with a particular input, we obtain a sequence of configurations. This sequence is called a run . The algorithm supplies the transition rules and tells us when to stop. Proof search can be described in the same way. We record the candidate being checked, the line currently under examination, and any other information needed by the checker. Each operation updates this configuration. For a deterministic procedure, the current configuration determines the next step. Does it also determine the previous step? In Euclid’s algorithm, both \\((30,18)\\) and \\((48,18)\\) lead to \\((18,12)\\). So, knowing how to continue does not necessarily tell us how to go backward. We will return to this distinction when we consider reversibility. A configuration includes enough information to resume the computation. A transition updates that information; a run is a sequence of these updates. Problems Euclid’s algorithm divides by repeated subtraction. It is paused in the middle of dividing \\(48\\) by \\(18\\), after one subtraction has given \\(30\\). What must the configuration record so that the computation can resume? Write the run of Euclid’s algorithm from \\((30,18)\\) as a sequence of configurations. Find two other pairs whose runs reach \\((18,12)\\) after one step. Describe all pairs from which Euclid’s algorithm reaches \\((18,12)\\) in one step. What does the answer imply about running the algorithm backward?"}, {"title": "Turing machines and universal computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-3", "text": "1.2.3 Turing machines and universal computation § Turing machine · tape · head · control state · rule table · halting state · universal machine An instruction such as “divide” still hides a computation. A Turing machine gives us elementary steps explicit enough to study what any program can do. Its tape consists of cells holding symbols from a finite alphabet, including a blank. A head reads one cell. A finite control state records the stage of the instructions. For each state and read symbol, the rule table specifies what to write, which way to move, and the next state. A designated halting state ends the run. For example, start at the left end of a finite block of ones. Move right on a one; halt on a blank. The same finite rule table handles blocks of every finite length. There is no fixed tape bound, but any finite run visits only finitely many cells. A configuration records the tape, head position, and control state. The decisive observation is that a rule table is itself finite data. Encode it as a string and another machine can read it and imitate its steps. A universal machine takes a program description together with an input and performs this simulation. Changing the supplied description changes the program being simulated. A program can now receive another program—or its own description—as input. No infinite object or mysterious self-awareness is involved: it receives a finite string. This will let us turn a proposed predictor of program behavior into a program that defeats that prediction. The highlighted cell is under the head. In this example, the machine moves right over each 1 and halts at a blank. A universal machine uses encoded instructions to simulate another machine; it need not halt if the simulated run does not halt. Problems A machine has a running state \\(q\\) and a halting state \\(h\\). In \\(q\\), reading \\(1\\), it writes \\(1\\), moves right, and stays in \\(q\\); reading a blank, it halts. Started on the leftmost \\(1\\) of a tape holding \\(111\\), list its configurations until it halts. Change the machine so that, reading a blank in state \\(q\\), it writes \\(1\\) and halts. What does the new machine compute on a block of \\(n\\) ones, and how many steps does it take? Design a machine that, started on the leftmost symbol of a block of ones, halts in a state marked “even” if the block has an even number of ones and “odd” otherwise. Give its rule table and trace it on \\(111\\)."}, {"title": "The Church–Turing thesis", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-1", "text": "1.3.1 The Church–Turing thesis § lambda calculus · Church–Turing thesis We have defined a model using a tape, a head, and a finite rule table. Why use this model to discuss the limits of effective calculation? Could another reasonable model compute functions that Turing machines cannot? Other mathematical models describe computation in different ways. Lambda calculus uses rules for substituting expressions. Recursive-function models build functions from specified basic functions and construction rules. Despite these differences, they compute the same functions as Turing machines. A function computable in one model is computable in the others. This equivalence is a mathematical result that can be proved. The Church–Turing thesis makes a further claim: any calculation that can be carried out by an effective mechanical method can be carried out by a Turing machine. The phrase “effective mechanical method” is an informal idea. A Turing machine is a precisely defined object. The thesis connects the two. The agreement between different formal models supports it, but does not turn it into a theorem about an already defined class of every possible mechanical method. This distinction matters when we establish a limitation. We can prove that no Turing machine solves a specified problem. Applying that result to effective procedures in general uses the thesis. The thesis concerns whether a function can be computed at all. It does not say how much time or memory the computation needs. This matters for quantum computing. In the standard quantum model, the allowed operations must themselves have effective mathematical descriptions. Under this condition, quantum computers do not compute functions that are uncomputable by Turing machines. They can change the resources needed for some tasks that are already computable. We will return to this difference when studying quantum algorithms. The solid arrows connect formal models that can simulate one another. They compute the same functions, though they may take different amounts of time. The dashed arrow marks the further claim of the Church–Turing thesis: these models capture what we mean informally by an effective method. Problems A programming language \\(L\\) can simulate every Turing machine, and a Turing machine can simulate every program of \\(L\\). Which functions does \\(L\\) compute? Is this conclusion a theorem or an instance of the Church–Turing thesis? Classify each statement as a theorem or as the Church–Turing thesis: (a) lambda calculus and Turing machines compute the same functions; (b) every function computable by an effective mechanical method is Turing-computable; (c) every recursive function is Turing-computable. Show that some functions from nonnegative integers to \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) are computed by no Turing machine. Turing machines can be listed as \\(M_0,M_1,M_2,\\dots\\), since each has a finite description."}, {"title": "The halting problem", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-2", "text": "1.3.2 The halting problem § halting problem Given a program and its input, can we always determine whether the computation will stop? We know that Euclid’s algorithm stops for its permitted inputs. We also know that a program instructed to repeat forever does not stop. Can one procedure settle this question for every program and input? Suppose such a procedure exists. Call it \\(H(P,x)\\), where \\(P\\) is a program description and \\(x\\) is its input. We require \\(H\\) itself to finish and answer correctly on every valid pair. Let us use \\(H\\) to construct another program, \\(D\\). Its input \\(p\\) is a program description. First, \\(D\\) calls \\(H(p,p)\\): it asks what happens when the program described by \\(p\\) receives its own description as input. Then \\(D\\) takes the opposite action. If \\(H\\) answers “halts,” \\(D\\) enters an endless loop. If \\(H\\) answers “does not halt,” \\(D\\) stops. If the input is not a valid program description, we can simply let \\(D\\) stop. Now let \\(d\\) be the description of \\(D\\), and give \\(d\\) to \\(D\\). What should \\(H(d,d)\\) answer? If it answers “halts,” the instructions of \\(D\\) make this run continue forever. If it answers “does not halt,” the instructions make the run stop. In either case, the prediction is wrong. We assumed that \\(H\\) was an algorithm that always finished and answered correctly. That assumption allowed us to use it as part of \\(D\\). But the resulting program contradicts the assumption. Therefore no such general \\(H\\) exists. This result is called the undecidability of the halting problem . This does not prevent us from determining the behavior of particular programs. We already proved that Euclid’s algorithm terminates. It also permits an analyzer that sometimes answers “unknown.” What is excluded is one algorithm that always finishes with a correct definite answer for every case. Assume \\(H\\) always finishes and predicts correctly. Program \\(D\\) follows its prediction and takes the opposite action. Now give \\(D\\) its own description \\(d\\). \\(H(d,d)\\) is predicting this very run, so either answer is contradicted by what \\(D\\) does. Problems Decide, by inspecting each program, whether it halts on the input \\(x=5\\): (a) while \\(x\\gt 0\\), set \\(x:=x-1\\); (b) while \\(x\\neq0\\), set \\(x:=x-2\\); (c) repeat forever: do nothing. A proposed halting test runs a program for a million steps. It answers “halts” if the program stops and “never halts” otherwise. Give a program on which this test is wrong. Would replacing a million by a larger fixed number repair it? Show that no algorithm decides, for every program \\(P\\), whether \\(P\\) halts when given the input \\(0\\). Use the undecidability of the halting problem: from a pair \\((P,x)\\), construct a program that ignores its own input."}, {"title": "Undecidability and incompleteness", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-3", "text": "1.3.3 Undecidability and incompleteness § undecidable problem · incomplete theory · second incompleteness theorem There are two distinct limits here. An undecidable problem has no algorithm that always terminates with the correct yes-or-no answer. An incomplete theory leaves some sentence neither provable nor refutable from its axioms. One concerns procedures; the other concerns what follows from fixed assumptions. The Gödel–Rosser theorem applies to consistent theories whose axioms can be effectively enumerated and which contain enough arithmetic. Peano arithmetic is a familiar example of the required arithmetic strength. Such a theory has a sentence \\(P\\) for which neither \\(P\\) nor \\(\\neg P\\) is provable. We state this theorem rather than attempt its proof here. Adding \\(P\\) as an axiom settles that sentence. But if the enlarged theory still meets the theorem’s conditions, another sentence remains unsettled. The result is not that every theory is incomplete; its conditions are essential. Gödel’s second incompleteness theorem addresses Hilbert’s consistency question. Under its arithmetic and proof-representation conditions, a consistent theory cannot prove its own standard formal statement of consistency. A stronger theory may justify a weaker one, but the stronger theory’s assumptions then become part of what we trust. For this course, retain the distinction: making rules explicit enables mechanical checking without guaranteeing that every mathematical question can be settled. We can now turn to tasks that do admit algorithms and ask what information their implementations preserve. On the left, we ask whether an algorithm can decide a problem for every input. On the right, we ask what a particular theory can prove. The incompleteness result requires the stated conditions on that theory. An unsuccessful search alone establishes neither result. Problems A theory has the single axiom \\(P\\) and the rule modus ponens, and its sentences are built from \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). Is \\(Q\\) provable? Is not \\(Q\\) provable? Is the theory complete? A sentence \\(G\\) is neither provable nor refutable in a consistent theory \\(T\\). Show that both \\(T\\) with \\(G\\) added as an axiom and \\(T\\) with not \\(G\\) added are consistent. You may use the fact that if \\(T\\) with an added axiom \\(A\\) proves a contradiction, then \\(T\\) proves not \\(A\\). Suppose an algorithm extends a consistent, effectively axiomatized theory of arithmetic step by step, each time adding a sentence that the current theory leaves undecided. Show that the combined theory, with all the added axioms together, is still incomplete if it is consistent."}, {"title": "Deterministic and reversible transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-1", "text": "1.4.1 Deterministic and reversible transitions § deterministic · one-to-one · bijection · logical reversibility In a deterministic computation, each configuration has at most one next configuration. A halting configuration may have none. When the computation can continue, the current configuration fixes what happens next. Does knowing the later configuration also tell us which earlier one produced it? Determinism does not require this. For example, Euclid’s algorithm includes the transitions \\[(30,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12),\\qquad(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12).\\] Knowing the pair \\((18,12)\\) lets us continue the calculation, but it does not identify which earlier pair occurred. Different configurations have merged into the same one. To undo a step, we must be able to recover the earlier configuration from the full resulting configuration. This requires different inputs to have different outputs. Such a transition is called one-to-one . Logical reversibility requires this extra condition. Suppose the transition also reaches every configuration in a fixed state space. It is then a bijection : each configuration has exactly one predecessor. We can define the reverse transformation everywhere on that space. So, knowing what happens next does not necessarily tell us what happened before. We now ask whether useful computations can preserve enough information to be reversed. On the left, each input has a unique successor, but \\(C\\) has two possible predecessors. On the right, each output identifies one predecessor. Reversibility imposes a condition on the backward direction as well. Problems A rule on the configurations \\(A,B,C,D\\) sends \\(A\\to B\\), \\(B\\to C\\), \\(C\\to D\\), and \\(D\\to B\\). Is it deterministic? Which configurations have more than one predecessor, and which have none? Run the same rule for six steps from each of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\). Which starting configurations end at the same configuration after six steps? Prove that a deterministic, one-to-one rule on finitely many configurations is onto, and that every run of such a rule eventually returns to its starting configuration."}, {"title": "Information loss in computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-2", "text": "1.4.2 Information loss in computation § many-to-one · unitary transformation AND returns zero on three inputs: \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\). If we keep only the answer, we cannot recover which input occurred. But obtaining an answer need not mean throwing away its input. The pair \\((x,f(x))\\) still contains \\(x\\), even if \\(f\\) is many-to-one. This suggests extra storage, but storage alone is not a construction. We must choose reversible elementary steps, specify the initial values of the extra bits, and clear temporary results without losing information. Chapter 2 supplies those ingredients. The reason to pursue this is physical as well as logical. Closed-system quantum evolution is described by a unitary transformation, which has an inverse. A quantum circuit must therefore obtain useful answers while respecting reversible evolution between measurements. We have moved from “Can this task be computed?” to “How can its computation preserve the information that quantum evolution requires?” On the left, the AND output does not tell us which input produced it. On the right, we keep both input bits and put their AND in an extra target bit, initially zero. The full update uses XOR, or addition modulo two. Applying it twice restores the original three bits. Chapter 2 explains the construction. Problems Which of these operations on two bits lose information: \\((x,y)\\mapsto x\\land y\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,x\\oplus y)\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto(y,x)\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto x\\oplus y\\)? Keep both inputs and store the AND in an extra bit \\(t\\) that starts at \\(0\\): \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\land y))\\). List the outputs for the four inputs with \\(t=0\\), and show that the map can be undone. A computation maps \\(n\\)-bit inputs to a single output bit. Show that at least \\(2^{n-1}\\) inputs share an output. If the computation keeps some bits besides its output, how many must it keep, at least, to become one-to-one, and how many always suffice?"}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-1", "text": "A proof search over a three-symbol alphabet has checked every candidate of length at most \\(10\\), including the empty string, without finding a proof. How many candidates is that, and what does the failure establish? \\(1+3+\\dots+3^{10}=(3^{11}-1)/2=88{,}573\\) candidates. The failure shows only that the statement has no proof of length at most \\(10\\). A longer proof may exist, and no finite search can establish that none does."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-2", "text": "Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((377,233)\\). Count the divisions, record each quotient, and explain separately why the answer is correct and why the run stops. The remainders are \\(144,89,55,34,21,13,8,5,3,2,1,0\\): twelve divisions, with quotient \\(1\\) every time except the last, which is \\(2\\). The greatest common divisor is \\(1\\). It is correct because each step keeps the set of common divisors unchanged, an invariant. The run stops because the second entry strictly decreases through nonnegative integers. Quotients of \\(1\\) make that decrease as slow as possible."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-3", "text": "Make each step of Euclid’s algorithm reversible by recording its quotient. Show that \\((b,r)\\) and \\(q\\) determine the previous pair, and find how many quotients a run on \\((377,233)\\) must keep. From \\((b,r)\\) and \\(q\\), the previous first entry is \\(a=qb+r\\), so the previous pair \\((a,b)\\) is recovered. A run that keeps all its quotients can be undone step by step. The run on \\((377,233)\\) keeps twelve quotients, eleven \\(1\\)s and one \\(2\\). Reversibility costs extra memory that grows with the length of the run."}, {"title": "Binary encodings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-1", "text": "2.1.1 Binary encodings § encoding A computer acts on bit strings. An encoding tells us what those strings mean: \\(00,01,10,11\\) might represent the integers zero through three, while \\(01000001\\) can represent either the integer 65 or the character A in ASCII. The choice matters because computational cost is measured against input length. An integer \\(N\\) needs about \\(\\log_2N\\) binary digits, but \\(N\\) marks in unary. The same numerical task can therefore have very different size measures. For a fixed length \\(n\\), a computation acts on \\(2^n\\) possible bit strings. An algorithm handles all permitted lengths by one systematic procedure; we do not get to hide a separately invented answer table for every size. An encoding supplies meaning. The bits alone do not tell a machine whether to treat \\(01000001\\) as a number or a character. Problems Write \\(13\\) in binary with four bits, and decode \\(1011\\) as an unsigned binary integer. An integer \\(N\\) is written in binary and, alternatively, in unary, as \\(N\\) marks. For \\(N=1000\\), compare the two input lengths. How does a procedure that takes \\(N\\) steps compare with each length? Show that any encoding of the integers \\(0,1,\\dots,M-1\\) by bit strings of one fixed length \\(n\\) needs \\(n\\geq\\log_2M\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean functions and truth tables", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-2", "text": "2.1.2 Boolean functions and truth tables § Boolean function · truth table What exactly do we want to compute? Take a yes/no question about an \\(n\\)-bit input. Its answer is a Boolean function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), which assigns \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) to every input. An answer of several bits is a list of such functions, one for each output bit. The most direct way to specify \\(f\\) is to list every input together with its output. This list is the truth table of \\(f\\). For AND and OR on two bits: Input \\(xy\\) AND OR \\(00\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) \\(01\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(10\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(11\\) \\(1\\) \\(1\\) Does a truth table tell us how to compute \\(f\\)? It tells us what the answer is, not how to obtain it. It also grows quickly: a function of \\(n\\) bits has \\(2^n\\) rows, so the table alone is exponentially large. We want a description built from a few simple operations. The truth table lists four possible inputs. Only the input \\(11\\) produces one; a circuit must implement all four rows. Problems Write the truth table of the majority function on three bits, which outputs \\(1\\) when at least two of its inputs are \\(1\\). How many Boolean functions of two bits are there? List those that output \\(1\\) on exactly one input. Show that there are \\(2^{2^n}\\) Boolean functions of \\(n\\) bits. For \\(n=10\\), show that fewer than one function in a thousand can be described by a string of fewer than \\(1014\\) bits."}, {"title": "Polynomial representation over the binary field", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-3", "text": "2.1.3 Polynomial representation over the binary field § arithmetic modulo two · XOR · detector Can a truth table be turned into a formula? Use arithmetic modulo two, in which \\(1+1=0\\). Addition modulo two is then XOR, and multiplication is AND. The idea is to build, for each row \\(s\\) of the table, a detector that equals \\(1\\) on the input \\(s\\) and \\(0\\) on every other input. The factor \\(x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=1\\), and the factor \\(1+x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=0\\). Multiplying one such factor for each bit gives \\[\\delta_s(x)=\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=1}x_i\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=0}(1+x_i).\\] For two bits \\((x,y)\\), the detector of the row \\(01\\) is \\((1+x)y\\). Now add the detectors of the rows on which \\(f\\) equals \\(1\\): \\[f(x)=\\sum_{s:\\,f(s)=1}\\delta_s(x)\\pmod 2.\\] Why is this \\(f\\)? On any input \\(x\\), exactly one detector, \\(\\delta_x\\), equals \\(1\\). If the row \\(x\\) is among those added, the sum is \\(1\\); otherwise it is \\(0\\). For OR, the rows with output \\(1\\) are \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\): \\[(1+x)y+x(1+y)+xy=x+y+3xy=x+y+xy\\pmod 2.\\] Check the row \\(11\\): \\(1+1+1=1\\) modulo two, as required, whereas ordinary addition would give \\(3\\). Expanding the detectors in this way writes every Boolean function as a polynomial in its input bits. All additions are modulo two. The polynomial \\(x+y+xy\\) reproduces OR on Boolean inputs; its interpretation depends on this arithmetic. Problems Evaluate \\((1+x)(1+y)\\) modulo two for all four inputs. Which function is it? Build the polynomial of XOR from its truth table using detectors, and simplify it. Show that the polynomial of the three-bit majority function is \\(xy+yz+xz\\) modulo two."}, {"title": "Polynomial evaluation by circuits", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-1", "text": "2.2.1 Polynomial evaluation by circuits § circuit · gate · wire How do we evaluate \\(x+y+xy\\) one operation at a time? Compute \\(u=xy\\) with an AND gate, compute \\(x+y\\) with an XOR gate, and combine the two results with a second XOR gate. This arrangement is a circuit . Its gates are elementary operations, and its wires carry bits from one gate to the next; here a wire carries \\(u\\) from the AND gate to the last XOR gate. A wire may also carry a fixed bit, \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). No wire leads back to an earlier gate, so the gates can be evaluated in order. The same method works for every polynomial: multiply factors with AND gates and add terms with XOR gates. So every Boolean function of a fixed number of bits has a circuit. Does it have a small circuit? Not necessarily: the polynomial built from a truth table can have exponentially many terms. That every function can be built is one claim; that it can be built economically is another. To evaluate \\(z=ab+c\\) over bits, compute the product once and pass that bit to XOR. Here \\(+\\) means XOR, so the diagram is also a circuit plan. Problems Evaluate the circuit for \\(x+y+xy\\), built from one AND gate and two XOR gates, on the input \\(10\\), giving the bit on every wire. Build a circuit for majority, \\(xy+yz+xz\\), from AND and XOR gates. How many gates does it use? The parity function \\(x_1+x_2+\\dots+x_n\\pmod2\\) equals \\(1\\) on half of its inputs. How many detectors does the truth-table construction add, and how many XOR gates does the simplified polynomial need?"}, {"title": "NAND universality", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-2", "text": "2.2.2 NAND universality § NAND · universal · fan-out Do we need AND, XOR, and NOT as separate kinds of gate? One kind is enough. NAND outputs the negation of AND, \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)=\\neg(a\\land b)\\); it is \\(0\\) only when \\(a=b=1\\). Tying both inputs to the same bit gives NOT: \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,a)=\\neg a\\). Negating a NAND output gives AND. XOR takes four NAND gates: \\[t=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\quad u=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,t),\\quad v=\\operatorname{NAND}(b,t),\\quad a\\oplus b=\\operatorname{NAND}(u,v).\\] Checking the four input pairs confirms the last identity. Since NAND builds AND, XOR, and NOT, it can evaluate the polynomial of any Boolean function. A gate with this property is universal . In this construction one bit often feeds several gates: \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) each feed two NAND gates, and so does \\(t\\). Copying a bit in this way, called fan-out , is free in classical circuits. Four NAND gates compute XOR. Branch dots indicate shared classical inputs; small output circles indicate negation. Problems Compute \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\) for all four inputs. Use the table to check that \\(\\operatorname{NAND}\\bigl(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\bigr)=a\\land b\\). Build OR from NAND gates, and verify the construction on all four inputs. Show that circuits of AND and XOR gates, with no constant inputs, cannot compute NOT, but that adding the constant \\(1\\) makes the set universal."}, {"title": "Irreversible Boolean operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-3", "text": "2.2.3 Irreversible Boolean operations § An AND output of one identifies its input: it must have been \\(11\\). An output of zero does not: \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) all fit. Reversibility must work for every allowed input, so unrestricted AND has no inverse. Always specify which registers are kept. A circuit can discard an intermediate copy while still retaining enough information elsewhere to recover its original input. Conversely, an output that looks reversible need not have been produced through reversible elementary steps. For a quantum implementation, we need the stronger construction: a sequence of steps that each preserves distinctions in the full state. The next sections show how to obtain the same Boolean answer without using AND as a two-input, one-output erasure. The AND gate maps \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) to the same output. Output zero cannot identify which of these inputs occurred. Problems For OR on two bits, list the inputs that share each output. Can the inputs be recovered from the output? A circuit computes \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x\\land y,\\;x\\lor y)\\). Is this map one-to-one? Which inputs collide? The map \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,\\,x\\oplus y)\\) is computed by copying \\(x\\) and feeding \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) into an XOR gate, which on its own loses information. Show that the map is one-to-one, and explain where the information discarded by the XOR gate survives."}, {"title": "Landauer’s principle", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-1", "text": "2.3.1 Landauer’s principle § Landauer’s principle A reset sends either initial bit value to zero. In the standard isothermal setting, erasing one equally likely bit without retaining its value elsewhere releases at least \\[Q\\geq k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\] of heat into the environment on average. This is Landauer’s principle ; \\(T\\) is absolute temperature and \\(k_{\\mathrm B}\\) is Boltzmann’s constant. The bound is about an entropy reduction under stated physical conditions, not a fee charged whenever a gate runs. A bit already known to be zero can be left untouched. Bias or usable side information changes the uncertainty being removed; if that information lives in another physical memory, its eventual treatment matters too. We will not need a thermodynamic derivation. The computational question is enough for now: can a useful calculation avoid merging distinct logical states? If it can, it avoids forcing erasure at those steps, though its physical implementation may still dissipate energy. Further detail: Bennett: Landauer’s principle and reversible computation . Both possible logical inputs are reset to zero. For erasure of one equally likely bit in the standard isothermal setting, the minimum heat delivered to the environment is kBT ln 2. Problems Compute \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) at \\(T=300\\) K, with \\(k_{\\mathrm B}=1.38\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K. A processor erases \\(10^{18}\\) bits per second at \\(300\\) K. What minimum power does Landauer’s principle require? Compare it with a processor that uses \\(50\\) W. Erasing a bit that is \\(0\\) with probability \\(p\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1-p\\) has the minimum cost \\(H\\,k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\), where \\(H=-p\\log_2p-(1-p)\\log_2(1-p)\\). Compute \\(H\\) for \\(p=0.9\\), and compare the cost with that of an unbiased bit and a known bit."}, {"title": "Logical reversibility and dissipation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-2", "text": "2.3.2 Logical reversibility and dissipation § logically reversible If a computation never erases information, does it produce no heat? Not necessarily. A logically reversible map, one that is one-to-one on its allowed states, avoids the cost that erasure forces. A real device may still dissipate energy through resistance, friction, imperfect control, or the speed at which it runs. Conversely, can a gate that loses information be made reversible by operating it slowly? No. Running AND slowly does not make its output reveal its input. Reversibility is a property of the map, not of how the map is carried out. Two questions must therefore be kept apart: whether the mathematical map can be inverted, and how much energy its physical realization uses. For quantum computation the first question is decisive, because the operations of an isolated quantum system can always be inverted. A reversible truth table settles whether the input is recoverable. It does not specify the energy dissipated by a particular physical device. Problems Which of these are logically reversible: NOT; AND; \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,x\\oplus y)\\); a reset to \\(0\\)? A device implements NOT but dissipates \\(10^{-18}\\) J per operation, while the Landauer bound at \\(300\\) K is about \\(2.9\\times10^{-21}\\) J. Does the dissipation contradict the reversibility of NOT? Does Landauer’s principle require any dissipation for NOT? Show that a reversible map on \\(n\\)-bit strings is a permutation of the \\(2^n\\) strings. For \\(n=2\\), count the reversible maps and compare with the number of all maps from two bits to two bits."}, {"title": "Reversible function embeddings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-3", "text": "2.3.3 Reversible function embeddings § target bit Can every function be computed without erasing information, even one such as AND that merges its inputs? It can, provided we keep the input. Add a target bit \\(t\\) and replace \\(f\\) by the map \\[U_f:(x,t)\\mapsto(x,\\,t\\oplus f(x)).\\] The input \\(x\\) is kept, and \\(f(x)\\) is added to the target modulo two. With \\(t=0\\), the target becomes the answer \\(f(x)\\). Why is this map reversible? Apply it twice: \\(t\\oplus f(x)\\oplus f(x)=t\\), because adding the same bit twice modulo two gives zero. The map is its own inverse. For AND it is \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\): \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\(000\\) \\(000\\) \\(001\\) \\(001\\) \\(010\\) \\(010\\) \\(011\\) \\(011\\) \\(100\\) \\(100\\) \\(101\\) \\(101\\) \\(110\\) \\(111\\) \\(111\\) \\(110\\) Has AND become invertible? No: \\(f\\) itself is unchanged and still merges its inputs. What is invertible is the larger map, whose output keeps \\(x\\). The extra bits are not mere bookkeeping; they are what makes the transformation one-to-one. Configurations are written \\(xt\\), here with \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\). \\(U_f\\) flips the target exactly when \\(f(x)=1\\), and applying it twice returns every configuration. Problems Write the outputs of \\(U_f:(x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\lor y))\\) for the four inputs with \\(t=0\\). For OR, apply \\(U_f\\) to \\((1,0,1)\\), then apply it again. A programmer expected the target to contain \\(f(1,0)=1\\) after the first call. What assumption did they overlook? For a function \\(f\\) from \\(n\\)-bit strings to \\(m\\)-bit strings, define \\(U_f(x,t)=(x,t\\oplus f(x))\\), where \\(t\\) has \\(m\\) bits and \\(\\oplus\\) acts bit by bit. Prove that \\(U_f\\) is its own inverse for every \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Controlled reversible operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-1", "text": "2.4.1 Controlled reversible operations § control · target · CNOT How can a gate act on one bit depending on another without losing anything? Keep the deciding bit unchanged, and let it choose a reversible update of the other bit. The kept bit is the control ; the updated bit is the target . The simplest example is controlled-NOT, or CNOT : \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a,b\\oplus a)\\). When the control \\(a\\) is \\(0\\), the target is unchanged; when \\(a\\) is \\(1\\), the target flips. So \\(00\\to00\\), \\(01\\to01\\), \\(10\\to11\\), and \\(11\\to10\\). Applying CNOT twice restores both bits. Why does this work? The control survives the gate, so we know which update was applied, and each update, doing nothing or flipping, is its own inverse. Would it be enough to make the target update reversible but discard the control? No: without the control we would not know whether to flip the target back. CNOT keeps the control, the first bit, and flips the target when the control is \\(1\\): \\(10\\) and \\(11\\) exchange. Exactly one arrow enters each configuration, so the step can be undone. Problems Apply CNOT with the second bit as control and the first as target to \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\). Apply three CNOT gates in turn to \\((a,b)\\): control the first bit and target the second, then control the second and target the first, then control the first and target the second. Show that the result is \\((b,a)\\). A controlled operation keeps a control bit \\(c\\) and applies a map \\(g_c\\) to the target. Prove that it is reversible when \\(g_0\\) and \\(g_1\\) are both reversible, and give its inverse. Show by an example that it is not reversible if \\(g_1\\) is a reset."}, {"title": "CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-2", "text": "2.4.2 CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates § Toffoli · Fredkin NOT and CNOT only produce XOR combinations of input bits, possibly with a constant added. They cannot produce the product \\(ab\\). To obtain a reversible AND, give the target two controls: \\[\\operatorname{Toffoli}(a,b,c)=(a,b,c\\oplus ab).\\] With \\(c=0\\), the target becomes \\(ab\\), while both inputs survive. Fixing one control to one gives CNOT; fixing both to one gives NOT. Together with clean workspace, these operations let us reversibly implement any Boolean circuit. This is a construction claim, not a promise that every circuit is small. Fredkin uses a different operation: it swaps two targets when its control is one. On \\((a,b,0)\\), the last target becomes \\(ab\\); with targets \\((0,1)\\), its outputs include \\(a\\) and \\(\\neg a\\). These constructions also provide universal Boolean computation with suitable constant bits and workspace. In the drawings, a filled dot is a control on one, a circled plus is an XOR target, and a vertical connector makes them parts of the same gate. Toffoli and Fredkin are both their own inverses. CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin preserve their full inputs in reversible form. Each drawing shows one gate, with time running left to right. Problems Apply the Toffoli gate to all eight inputs \\((a,b,c)\\). Which inputs change? Using Toffoli gates and extra bits set to fixed values, compute \\(a\\land b\\), \\(\\neg a\\), and \\(a\\oplus b\\). Specify the inputs of each gate. Prove that every output bit of a circuit of CNOT and NOT gates is a sum modulo two of input bits, possibly plus \\(1\\). Conclude that such circuits cannot compute \\(a\\land b\\)."}, {"title": "Workspace and uncomputation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-3", "text": "2.4.3 Workspace and uncomputation § workspace · uncomputation · garbage Toffoli gates compute every function reversibly, but a longer computation produces intermediate results along the way. What becomes of them? Compute the AND of three bits \\(x,y,z\\) with Toffoli gates, using two extra bits that start at \\(0\\): a workspace bit \\(w\\) and an output bit \\(t\\). The first Toffoli gate writes \\(w=xy\\); the second, with controls \\(w\\) and \\(z\\), writes \\(t=xyz\\): \\[(x,y,z,0,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,xyz).\\] The answer is in \\(t\\), but \\(w\\) still holds \\(xy\\). What should we do with it? Resetting \\(w\\) to \\(0\\) erases it, with the cost of erasure. Leaving it means that the workspace keeps a record of the computation’s history, and every further computation adds more. There is a third option: apply the first Toffoli gate again. It is its own inverse, so it returns \\(w\\) to \\(xy\\oplus xy=0\\): \\[(x,y,z,xy,xyz)\\to(x,y,z,0,xyz).\\] This is uncomputation . In general, a reversible computation of \\(f\\) produces its answer together with intermediate values \\(g(x)\\), often called garbage. Copy the answer into a fresh register with CNOT gates, then run the computation backward: \\[(x,0,0;0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);f(x))\\to(x,0,0;f(x)).\\] Only the input and the copied answer remain. Why go to this trouble? Classically, it avoids the cost of erasure. Quantum computation adds a stronger reason: leftover records of a computation’s history prevent the interference on which quantum algorithms depend. Compute the answer, copy its value to a fresh output, then apply the inverse computation. \\(A\\) may act on a larger workspace than the two schematic wires shown. Problems In the three-bit AND computation with workspace \\(w\\) and output \\(t\\), list the five bits \\((x,y,z,w,t)\\) after each of the three Toffoli gates for the input \\((1,1,0)\\). Compute the AND of four bits \\(x,y,z,u\\) with Toffoli gates, two workspace bits that start at \\(0\\), and one output bit, and return the workspace to \\(0\\). List the gates in order. A reversible computation \\(A\\) maps \\((x,0,0)\\) to \\((x,f(x),g(x))\\). Show that applying \\(A\\), then copying the answer register into a fresh register with CNOT gates, then applying \\(A^{-1}\\), produces \\((x,0,0;f(x))\\) for every input \\(x\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is the map \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x\\lor y,\\;x\\land y)\\) reversible? No. The inputs \\(01\\) and \\(10\\) both give \\((1,0)\\). Two output bits are not enough when the map merges inputs."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Compute the three-bit majority function reversibly: using three Toffoli gates, turn \\((x,y,z,t)\\) into \\((x,y,z,\\,t\\oplus\\mathrm{maj}(x,y,z))\\). Check the construction on the inputs \\(110\\) and \\(100\\) with \\(t=0\\). Apply Toffoli gates with controls \\((x,y)\\), \\((y,z)\\), and \\((x,z)\\), each targeting \\(t\\). Together they add \\(xy\\oplus yz\\oplus xz\\), the polynomial of majority. On \\(110\\): \\(1\\oplus0\\oplus0=1\\). On \\(100\\): \\(0\\oplus0\\oplus0=0\\). The inputs are kept, so the map is reversible; it is its own inverse."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-3", "text": "Build the reversible embedding of OR, \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\lor y))\\), from CNOT and Toffoli gates, and show that the circuit is its own inverse. CNOT from \\(x\\) to \\(t\\), CNOT from \\(y\\) to \\(t\\), and Toffoli from \\((x,y)\\) to \\(t\\) add \\(x\\oplus y\\oplus xy=x\\lor y\\) to \\(t\\). Since none of the gates changes \\(x\\) or \\(y\\), running the circuit twice adds \\(x\\lor y\\) twice, which cancels. The circuit is its own inverse, and with \\(t=0\\) it leaves the answer in \\(t\\)."}, {"title": "States", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-1", "text": "3.1.1 States § configuration · state · weight · definite Take a register holding one bit. Its possible contents, \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), are its configurations . At any moment the register holds exactly one of them. Do we always know which? We may have set the bit ourselves, and then we know it. We may know only that either configuration is possible. Or the bit may have been set by a fair coin, so that each configuration has probability \\(1/2\\). What we know about the register at a given time is its state . All three kinds of knowledge take the same form: a number \\(v_j\\) for each configuration \\(j\\), its weight in the state. What we know \\(v_0\\) \\(v_1\\) The bit is \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) Either value is possible \\(1\\) \\(1\\) A fair coin set the bit \\(1/2\\) \\(1/2\\) The weights mean different things in different rows. A weight \\(1\\) or \\(0\\) records whether a configuration is possible; a probability records how often it occurs. A state with weight \\(1\\) on one configuration and \\(0\\) on the others is definite , and we name it by that configuration. Each configuration carries a weight. The state is the pair of weights \\(v_0\\) and \\(v_1\\). Problems A two-bit register has configurations \\(00,01,10,11\\). Write the weights of its state when (a) the register is known to hold \\(10\\); (b) the first bit is known to be \\(1\\) and the second could be either; (c) both bits were set by independent fair coins. Someone describes a bit by the weights \\(v_0=v_1=1\\). Can these weights be probabilities? What do they say about the bit, and what do they leave unsaid? Give two different probability states that are consistent with them. A register has \\(k\\) bits. A possibility state gives each configuration weight \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), with at least one weight \\(1\\). How many possibility states are there? How many probability states are there? Which probability states are consistent with the possibility state in which every configuration is possible?"}, {"title": "Transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-2", "text": "3.1.2 Transitions § transition rule · state diagram · weight · run · time-expanded diagram · path A computation changes the register step by step. A transition rule says which configurations may follow each one. As an example, take NOT, which flips the bit. We draw a rule as a state diagram : a circle for each configuration, and an arrow \\(i\\to j\\) whenever the rule lets \\(j\\) follow \\(i\\). Each arrow carries a weight . For NOT every weight is \\(1\\), because the step certainly follows the arrow. Applying a rule step by step produces a run \\(c_0,c_1,c_2\\), where \\(c_t\\) is the configuration after \\(t\\) steps. From \\(0\\), NOT gives \\(0,1,0\\). To see a run in a diagram, copy the configurations once for each time \\(t=0,1,2\\) and join the copies by the arrows of the rule. In this time-expanded diagram , a run is a path from time \\(0\\) to time \\(2\\). Rules differ in how many arrows leave each configuration and in what their weights mean. These two differences separate the three kinds of rule that follow. NOT: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The only path is \\(0\\to1\\to0\\). Problems A rule on the configurations \\(0,1,2\\) sends \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to2\\), and \\(2\\to0\\). Draw its state diagram and write the run from \\(0\\) for four steps. For the same rule, draw the time-expanded diagram for the times \\(t=0,1,2\\). How many arrows does it contain, and which path is the run from \\(2\\)? A rule on \\(m\\) configurations has \\(a\\) arrows in its state diagram. How many arrows does its time-expanded diagram have for the times \\(0,1,\\dots,t\\)? Show that the runs of \\(t\\) steps are exactly the paths from time \\(0\\) to time \\(t\\), and explain why the time-expanded diagram never contains a cycle, even when the state diagram does."}, {"title": "Deterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-3", "text": "3.1.3 Deterministic transitions § deterministic · reversible Compare NOT with RESET, which writes \\(0\\) whatever the bit holds. Configuration Next under NOT Next under RESET \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) From \\(0\\), NOT gives the run \\(0,1,0\\) and RESET gives \\(0,0,0\\). Under both rules, exactly one arrow leaves each configuration, so the start fixes the whole run: both rules are deterministic . From a definite state, the state stays definite at every step. Can a run also be traced backward? Under NOT, exactly one arrow enters each configuration, so the previous bit is always known. Under RESET, two arrows enter \\(0\\), and a run that reaches \\(0\\) may have come from either bit. A deterministic rule with exactly one arrow entering each configuration is reversible . RESET sends both configurations to \\(0\\). A run that reaches \\(0\\) does not record where it started, so RESET is deterministic but not reversible. Problems On two bits, the rule SWAP exchanges the bits: \\(01\\to10\\) and \\(10\\to01\\), while \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) stay as they are. Write the two-step run from \\(01\\). Is SWAP deterministic? Is it reversible? On the configurations \\(0,1,2,3\\), the rule INC adds \\(1\\) modulo \\(4\\), and the rule HALVE replaces \\(n\\) by \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), the integer part of \\(n/2\\). Draw both state diagrams, write the two-step runs from \\(3\\), and decide which rule is reversible. Prove that a deterministic rule on finitely many configurations is reversible exactly when every configuration has at least one incoming arrow. Then show that the statement fails for the rule \\(n\\mapsto\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) on all nonnegative integers."}, {"title": "Nondeterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-4", "text": "3.1.4 Nondeterministic transitions § nondeterministic · reachable Nondeterminism relaxes the requirement of a unique successor. A configuration may have several permitted successors, so specifying the present no longer specifies a unique future. The rule describes a set of possible runs rather than a single run. For a bit, consider KEEP-OR-FLIP: from either configuration, both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are permitted. Its state diagram has all four arrows. Starting from \\(0\\), two steps admit four paths: \\[0\\to0\\to0,\\qquad0\\to1\\to0,\\qquad0\\to0\\to1,\\qquad0\\to1\\to1.\\] A path is possible when every transition along it is permitted. A configuration is reachable when at least one possible path ends there. Both zero and one are therefore reachable after two steps. In a nondeterministic model of computation, acceptance is defined in the same way: an input is accepted if at least one permitted run accepts it. This is a statement about possibility, not a mechanism for choosing among paths. Even if more paths reached one endpoint than another, the rule would not make that endpoint more likely. Counting possibilities and assigning probabilities are different tasks. To predict how often a future occurs, we need more information on the arrows. KEEP-OR-FLIP: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Four paths are possible; both configurations are reachable at time \\(2\\). Problems A rule on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) allows \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to0\\), and \\(1\\to1\\), and forbids \\(0\\to0\\). List the possible two-step runs from \\(0\\), and the configurations reachable at time \\(2\\). For the same rule, list the possible three-step runs from \\(0\\). Which configurations are reachable at time \\(3\\), and by how many paths? On the configurations \\(0,1,\\dots,k\\), a rule allows \\(i\\to i\\) and \\(i\\to i+1\\) for \\(i\\lt k\\), and only \\(k\\to k\\) at \\(k\\). Show that \\(k\\) is reachable from \\(0\\) at time \\(t\\) exactly when \\(t\\geq k\\), and that the number of paths from \\(0\\) to \\(k\\) at time \\(t\\) is \\(\\binom tk\\)."}, {"title": "Probabilistic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-5", "text": "3.1.5 Probabilistic transitions § probabilistic · conditional probability · Markov A probabilistic rule supplies the information that nondeterminism leaves unspecified: the probability of each next configuration, given the present one. We write the probability on an arrow \\(i\\to j\\) as \\(P(j\\mid i)\\), read “the probability of \\(j\\) given \\(i\\).” Since some next configuration must occur, the probabilities leaving each configuration add to one. The current configuration is assumed to contain all the information needed for this prediction. If earlier history matters, that history must be included in the configuration. With this assumption, the process is called Markov . Consider COIN: whatever the bit currently holds, replace it by the result of a fresh fair coin flip. Its permitted arrows are the same as those of KEEP-OR-FLIP, but each now carries probability \\(1/2\\). For the path \\(0\\to1\\to0\\), the first step has probability \\(1/2\\), and the second has conditional probability \\(1/2\\) given the intermediate configuration. The path probability is therefore \\(1/4\\). The other path to zero, \\(0\\to0\\to0\\), also has probability \\(1/4\\). These are mutually exclusive histories, so their probabilities add: \\[P(c_2=0)=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12,\\] where \\(c_2\\) is the configuration after two steps. The probability of ending at one is also \\(1/2\\). Unlike the deterministic rule, COIN does not fix the outcome of a single run. Unlike the nondeterministic rule, it predicts the distribution of outcomes over independently repeated runs. COIN: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Each path has probability \\(1/4\\); two paths end at each configuration. Problems A step keeps the bit with probability \\(2/3\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/3\\). Starting from \\(0\\), compute \\(P(c_2=0)\\) by listing the paths that end at \\(0\\). For the same rule, compute \\(P(c_3=0)\\) from \\(0\\). Check your answer against the formula \\(P(c_t=0)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^t\\). Prove the formula \\(P(c_t=0)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^t\\) for the rule above, and describe what happens to the run’s memory of its starting configuration as \\(t\\) grows."}, {"title": "The product rule along a path", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-1", "text": "3.2.1 The product rule along a path § product rule A path specifies every intermediate configuration, so following it requires every transition along it. Assign a weight \\(w(i\\to j)\\) to each arrow. The weight of a two-step path is the product \\[w(i\\to j\\to k)=w(j\\to k)\\,w(i\\to j).\\] For deterministic and nondeterministic rules, let an allowed arrow have weight one and a missing arrow weight zero. The product is one precisely when the whole path is permitted: a single missing transition makes it zero. For a probabilistic rule, use the transition probabilities as weights. Multiplication then gives the probability of that particular history. The factors are conditional probabilities for successive steps; they need not describe independent events. Their dependence on the past is already accounted for by the current configuration. For COIN, each two-step path has weight \\((1/2)(1/2)=1/4\\). A path of \\(t\\) steps has weight \\(2^{-t}\\). This determines the contribution of one history. An endpoint may be reached through several histories, whose contributions must still be combined. The weight of the path \\(i\\to j\\to k\\) is the product of its two transition weights. Problems Under the rule that keeps the bit with probability \\(2/3\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/3\\), what is the weight of the path \\(0\\to0\\to1\\to1\\)? A nondeterministic rule on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) allows \\(0\\to0\\), \\(0\\to1\\), and \\(1\\to1\\), and forbids \\(1\\to0\\). Using the product rule with weights \\(1\\) and \\(0\\), decide which of the eight three-step paths from \\(0\\) are possible. The product rule for probabilities assumes the Markov property. Find a two-step random process on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) in which the probability of the path \\(0\\to1\\to0\\), given the start \\(0\\), differs from \\(P(c_1=1\\mid c_0=0)\\,P(c_2=0\\mid c_1=1)\\)."}, {"title": "The sum rule across paths", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-2", "text": "3.2.2 The sum rule across paths § sum rule An endpoint specifies where the run finishes without specifying how it got there. To calculate its weight, we therefore sum the contributions of all paths that reach it. For two steps from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\), the paths differ only in the intermediate configuration \\(j\\): \\[w(i\\Rightarrow k)=\\sum_j w(j\\to k)\\,w(i\\to j).\\] The arrow \\(\\Rightarrow\\) here denotes the complete two-step transition. Each term accounts for one route; the sum accounts for all routes to the chosen endpoint. For a deterministic rule, only one path from the start is permitted. The sum is one at its endpoint and zero elsewhere. For a nondeterministic rule, the sum counts permitted paths. A positive count establishes reachability, but is not a probability. For a probabilistic rule, the histories are mutually exclusive, so the sum is the endpoint probability. Distinguishing the two-step probability \\(P_2\\) from the one-step probabilities \\(P\\), we have \\[P_2(k\\mid i)=\\sum_jP(k\\mid j)P(j\\mid i).\\] For COIN, this is \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) at either endpoint. In all three models, every contribution is nonnegative. Adding a permitted path can therefore increase an endpoint’s total weight or leave it unchanged, but cannot reduce it. This restriction will matter when we encounter quantum transitions. Each route passes through an intermediate configuration \\(j\\). Multiply along each route, then sum over all intermediate configurations; the dots stand for any additional routes. Problems Under KEEP-OR-FLIP, how many possible paths lead from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) in three steps, and what does the count tell us? A rule has \\(P(0\\mid0)=2/3\\), \\(P(1\\mid0)=1/3\\), \\(P(0\\mid1)=1/4\\), and \\(P(1\\mid1)=3/4\\). Use the sum rule to compute the four two-step weights \\(w(i\\Rightarrow k)\\), and check that the weights from each start add to \\(1\\). Prove that, for any probabilistic rule, the two-step weights \\(w(i\\Rightarrow k)\\) given by the sum and product rules add to \\(1\\) over \\(k\\). What does this show about composing two probabilistic steps?"}, {"title": "States and steps", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-3", "text": "3.2.3 States and steps § So far each calculation began from a definite state. The same rules apply to any state, with weight \\(v_i\\) on each configuration \\(i\\). During a step, the contribution from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) is \\(w(i\\to j)v_i\\). Summing over all starting configurations gives the new weight at \\(j\\): \\[v^\\prime_j=\\sum_i w(i\\to j)\\,v_i.\\] The state thus collects the information needed to predict the next step, without retaining a separate list of all earlier paths. For COIN, \\(v^\\prime_0=v^\\prime_1=(v_0+v_1)/2=1/2\\). Every initial probability distribution becomes the same fair distribution. Whether a step can be undone depends on what this update preserves. A reversible deterministic rule permutes the configurations; reversing its arrows restores every initial state. CNOT is an example on two bits, with arrows \\(00\\to00\\), \\(01\\to01\\), \\(10\\to11\\), and \\(11\\to10\\). RESET and COIN instead send distinct initial states to the same final state. No rule acting only on that final state can recover which preparation was used. To undo such a process, we would need additional information outside this description. Each initial weight \\(v_i\\) contributes \\(w(i\\to j)v_i\\) to the new weight at \\(j\\). The new state contains one such sum for every output configuration. Problems Apply one step of COIN, and separately one step of NOT, to the state with weights \\(0.9\\) on \\(0\\) and \\(0.1\\) on \\(1\\). For the rule with \\(P(0\\mid0)=2/3\\), \\(P(1\\mid0)=1/3\\), \\(P(0\\mid1)=1/4\\), and \\(P(1\\mid1)=3/4\\), find the distribution that one step leaves unchanged. Prove that a probabilistic rule on finitely many configurations can be undone by another probabilistic rule only if it is deterministic and reversible."}, {"title": "Negative weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-1", "text": "3.3.1 Negative weights § signed contribution · product rule So far, weights have counted paths or described probabilities. In either case they were nonnegative. Let us keep the same sum and product rules, but allow a state or an arrow to carry a negative real weight. This is a mathematical extension of our path description; its physical interpretation will come later. The product rule still assigns a path the product of its arrow weights. For a path \\(i\\to j\\to k\\), \\[W(i\\to j\\to k)=w(i\\to j)\\,w(j\\to k).\\] The sign now matters. A path with an odd number of negative arrow weights has negative weight; a path with an even number has positive weight. For example, \\((1/2)(-1/2)=-1/4\\), while \\((-1/2)(-1/2)=1/4\\). A negative arrow can therefore reverse the sign of a contribution that was already negative. If the starting configuration itself has weight \\(v_i\\), its contribution along this path is \\(v_iw(i\\to j)w(j\\to k)\\). We include the sign of \\(v_i\\) as well as those of the arrows. A negative path weight is not a negative probability or a negative number of paths. It is a signed contribution to a later sum. The path remains present in the diagram; what changes is how its contribution combines with others. Each row is a path through configurations \\(i\\), \\(j\\), and \\(k\\), with transition weights on the arrows. One negative arrow gives path weight \\(-1/4\\); two negative arrows give path weight \\(+1/4\\). The rows are separate examples. Problems A path has arrow weights \\(-1/2\\), \\(1/3\\), and \\(-3/4\\). What is its weight? A starting configuration has weight \\(-2\\). A path from it has arrow weights \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/3\\). What contribution reaches the endpoint? If the sign of one nonzero arrow weight on a path is reversed, how does the path weight change? What if two arrow occurrences change sign?"}, {"title": "Cancellation across paths", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-2", "text": "3.3.2 Cancellation across paths § sum rule · cancellation The sum rule still adds the contributions of all paths with the same start and endpoint. With two intermediate configurations, \\[W(i\\to k)=w(i\\to j_1)w(j_1\\to k)+w(i\\to j_2)w(j_2\\to k).\\] Write the first pair of arrow weights as \\(a,c\\) and the second as \\(b,d\\). The two path contributions are \\(ca\\) and \\(db\\), and the total is \\(ca+db\\). If \\(a=b=c=1/2\\) and \\(d=-1/2\\), then \\[W(i\\to k)=\\frac14-\\frac14=0.\\] Both paths have nonzero weight, yet their combined contribution vanishes. This is cancellation . With nonnegative weights, a sum can vanish only when every term vanishes. Signed weights allow a zero total even when individual terms are nonzero. Cancellation need not be complete: \\(1/4-1/8=1/8\\). Contributions with the same sign reinforce one another: \\(-1/4-1/4=-1/2\\). Adding a path can now decrease the total weight, and removing a negative contribution can increase it. The sum of the weights is no longer required to be one or even positive. Only contributions to the same final configuration are added. A weight \\(+1/4\\) at configuration \\(0\\) and a weight \\(-1/4\\) at configuration \\(1\\) remain two separate entries. Their sum is zero, but neither entry has vanished. For state weights, the update therefore keeps exactly its earlier form: \\[v'_j=\\sum_i v_iw(i\\to j).\\] Multiplication carries each signed contribution forward; addition combines contributions at their common endpoint. These two rules now describe cancellation as well as accumulation. No rule for converting weights to observed probabilities has yet been specified. Two paths to the same configuration \\(k\\) contribute \\(ca\\) and \\(db\\). With \\(a=b=c=1/2\\) and \\(d=-1/2\\), the contributions cancel although both paths are present. Problems Three paths to the same endpoint contribute \\(1/3\\), \\(-1/2\\), and \\(1/6\\). What is the total? Does it imply that no path reaches the endpoint? In the two-path diagram, set \\(a=b=c=1/2\\). Find the total at \\(k\\) for \\(d=1/2\\), \\(d=-1/4\\), and \\(d=-1/2\\). Two paths to \\(0\\) contribute \\(1/4\\) each, and two paths to \\(1\\) contribute \\(-1/4\\) each. What are the final state weights? Can any contributions cancel?"}, {"title": "The Hadamard step", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-3", "text": "3.3.3 The Hadamard step § Hadamard operation We can use cancellation to construct a step that spreads a definite input over two configurations, yet undoes itself when repeated. Call the step \\(H\\). Give three arrows weight \\(c\\) and the remaining arrow weight \\(-c\\): \\[w(0\\to0)=w(0\\to1)=w(1\\to0)=c,\\qquad w(1\\to1)=-c.\\] Start at \\(0\\) with weight one. After one step the weights at \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are \\((c,c)\\). After two steps, the paths \\(0\\to0\\to0\\) and \\(0\\to1\\to0\\) each contribute \\(c^2\\), giving \\(2c^2\\) at \\(0\\). The paths \\(0\\to0\\to1\\) and \\(0\\to1\\to1\\) contribute \\(c^2\\) and \\(-c^2\\), giving zero at \\(1\\). The signed step with weights \\(c\\) and \\(-c\\): the two paths to \\(0\\) add to \\(2c^2\\), while the paths to \\(1\\) cancel. To restore the starting weight one, require \\(2c^2=1\\). To recover the original weight one, choose \\(2c^2=1\\), or \\(c=1/\\sqrt2\\). This gives the Hadamard operation : \\[w(0\\to0)=w(0\\to1)=w(1\\to0)=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\qquad w(1\\to1)=-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}.\\] The factor \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) has been fixed by composition: two applications must return the original weights. Starting at \\(1\\) provides the other check. The paths to \\(0\\) cancel; the paths to \\(1\\) reinforce, including the path with two negative arrows, whose product is positive. Thus \\(H^2=I\\), where \\(I\\) is the identity step. Notice what this choice does to the weights after one step. From either definite input, their squares add to one: \\[c^2+c^2=c^2+(-c)^2=1.\\] The weights themselves may be negative, and their sum need not be one. Their squares, however, are nonnegative and here give \\(1/2\\) for each configuration. This suggests using squared weights as probabilities, while retaining the signed weights for composition and cancellation. It is a motivation for the quantum probability rule, rather than a derivation of it; we state that rule when we connect these transitions to physical outcomes. For general input weights \\((v_0,v_1)\\), the update is \\[(v'_0,v'_1)=\\left(\\frac{v_0+v_1}{\\sqrt2},\\frac{v_0-v_1}{\\sqrt2}\\right).\\] Applying the same update again gives \\((v_0,v_1)\\). The intermediate weights retain enough information to recover the input: starting at \\(0\\) gives \\((1,1)/\\sqrt2\\), while starting at \\(1\\) gives \\((1,-1)/\\sqrt2\\). Their signs determine which endpoint survives the second step. If we replace the negative arrow by a positive one, the two contributions to each endpoint reinforce. Both inputs then produce the same intermediate weights, and the second step cannot recover the starting configuration. Cancellation is what makes the signed step reversible. Negative real weights have given us opposite signs and cancellation. Next we extend weights to complex numbers, where a continuously varying phase takes the place of this two-way choice of sign, and connect the resulting rules to quantum transitions. \\(H\\): state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The paths to \\(0\\)\n                    add to weight \\(1\\); the paths to \\(1\\) cancel. Problems Apply \\(H\\) to the state weights \\((1,-1)/\\sqrt2\\). Draw the two-step paths from \\(1\\) under \\(H\\). Compute the contribution of each path and the final weights. Replace the negative arrow of \\(H\\) by \\(+1/\\sqrt2\\). Starting at \\(0\\), find the weights after two steps. Explain why this modified step cannot be reversible."}, {"title": "Complex weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-1", "text": "3.4.1 Complex weights § amplitude · conjugate · squared magnitude · phase Complex weights associated with states and transitions are called amplitudes . They obey the same sum and product rules as real weights. The Hadamard operation satisfies \\(H^2=I\\). A square root of NOT is an operation \\(S\\) satisfying \\(S^2=X\\), where \\(X\\) denotes NOT. Neither deterministic reversible operation on one bit has this property: both the identity and NOT square to the identity. Let \\(S\\) have stay-weight \\(a\\) and switch-weight \\(b\\), the same from either configuration. In two steps, staying twice or switching twice returns to the initial configuration, with total weight \\(a^2+b^2\\). Staying then switching or switching then staying reaches the opposite configuration, with total weight \\(ab+ba=2ab\\). For the combined operation to be NOT, \\[a^2+b^2=0,\\qquad2ab=1.\\] The step \\(S\\) has stay-weight \\(a\\) and switch-weight \\(b\\). In two steps from \\(0\\),\n                        the paths returning to \\(0\\) contribute \\(a^2+b^2\\); the paths reaching \\(1\\) contribute\n                        \\(ab+ba=2ab\\). To obtain NOT, these totals must be zero and one, respectively. For real \\(a,b\\), the first equation forces both to vanish, contradicting the second. Complex numbers\n                    permit cancellation between the squares themselves. With \\(i^2=-1\\), choose \\[a=\\frac{1+i}{2},\\qquad b=\\frac{1-i}{2}.\\] Then \\(a^2=i/2\\), \\(b^2=-i/2\\), and \\(2ab=1\\). The two-step transformation is exactly NOT. The weights of \\(S\\) now give the desired composition, but we still need to associate them with probabilities. In the real-weight example, squaring gave a nonnegative number. Here the squares \\(a^2=i/2\\) and \\(b^2=-i/2\\) cannot serve that purpose. Multiplying a complex weight by its conjugate offers a natural extension: if \\(z=u+iv\\), its conjugate is \\(z^*=u-iv\\), and \\[zz^*=(u+iv)(u-iv)=u^2+v^2=|z|^2.\\] This is the squared magnitude of \\(z\\). It is real and nonnegative, and becomes the ordinary square when the weight is real. We can therefore propose that a configuration with amplitude \\(v_j\\) has probability \\[p(j)=v_jv_j^*=|v_j|^2.\\] With this assignment, the requirement that probabilities sum to one becomes a condition on the amplitudes: \\[\\sum_jp(j)=1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\sum_j|v_j|^2=1.\\] We call this normalization . For the two outgoing weights of \\(S\\), \\(|a|^2=|b|^2=1/2\\), so their probabilities do sum to one. The same assignment also gives the probabilities \\(1/2,1/2\\) for a Hadamard step. Thus it agrees with the real-weight example and accommodates the complex weights just constructed. A computation involves more than one step, however. The probability assignment must remain consistent as weights multiply along paths and add at their endpoints. Multiplication has a useful property here: \\[|z_1z_2|^2=(z_1z_2)(z_1z_2)^*=|z_1|^2|z_2|^2.\\] The squared magnitude of a path product is the product of the squared magnitudes of its factors. When several paths meet, their amplitudes must still be added before assigning a probability. For two contributions, \\[|A+B|^2=|A|^2+|B|^2+AB^*+A^*B.\\] The last two terms retain the dependence on how the contributions combine. In two steps of \\(S\\), the return contributions are \\(A=i/2\\) and \\(B=-i/2\\); their sum is zero, and so is the return probability. Assigning separate probabilities and adding them would give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\), losing the cancellation. The distinction is therefore between adding amplitudes at a common endpoint and adding probabilities over distinct final configurations. This cancellation changes the probability of a particular configuration. The total over all final configurations must nevertheless remain one. That places a restriction on the transition weights. If the input amplitudes are \\(v_i\\), the sum and product rules give \\[v'_j=\\sum_i v_iw(i\\to j).\\] For a definite input \\(i\\), only \\(v_i=1\\) is nonzero, so the outgoing amplitudes are simply \\(w(i\\to j)\\). Normalization then requires \\[\\sum_j|w(i\\to j)|^2=1\\] for each input configuration. This checks the transition on definite inputs. With several nonzero input amplitudes, contributions from different starting configurations combine at each endpoint, producing the same kind of cross terms as \\(A+B\\) above. Summing over endpoints gives \\[\\sum_j|v'_j|^2=\\sum_i|v_i|^2\\sum_j|w(i\\to j)|^2+\\sum_{i\\ne k}v_i^*v_k\\sum_jw(i\\to j)^*w(k\\to j).\\] The outgoing normalization condition makes the first term equal to the original total \\(\\sum_i|v_i|^2\\). The second term must therefore vanish for every choice of input amplitudes. Since their relative magnitudes and complex directions can vary, this requires \\[\\sum_jw(i\\to j)^*w(k\\to j)=0\\qquad(i\\ne k).\\] This cross-sum condition allows contributions to reinforce or cancel at individual endpoints while ensuring that their cross terms cancel in the total. Together with outgoing normalization, it makes the proposed probability assignment compatible with the amplitude update. Each step takes a normalized input to a normalized output, so a sequence of such steps preserves total probability. The step \\(S\\) satisfies both requirements. Its outgoing squared magnitudes sum to \\(|a|^2+|b|^2=1\\), and the cross sum for its two inputs is \\[a^*b+b^*a=-\\frac i2+\\frac i2=0.\\] It therefore preserves normalization for arbitrary input amplitudes, as well as for the definite inputs used in its construction. After two steps, the return amplitude \\(a^2+b^2=0\\) gives probability zero, and the switch amplitude \\(2ab=1\\) gives probability one. Both the one-step probabilities and the composition into NOT fit the proposed rule. The complex directions appearing in these sums can be expressed by writing a nonzero amplitude as \\(z=re^{i\\phi}\\). Here \\(r=|z|\\) is its magnitude and \\(\\phi\\) its phase . The probability \\(|z|^2=r^2\\) retains the magnitude but discards the phase. Positive and negative real weights correspond to phases zero and \\(\\pi\\); complex weights allow the intervening angles. Multiplication adds phases, and addition depends on their relative values. This is how two amplitude lists with the same probabilities can give different probabilities after a further transition. The amplitude \\(z=re^{i\\phi}\\) is a vector of length \\(r\\) at angle \\(\\phi\\) in the complex\n                    plane. Its real and imaginary components are \\(r\\cos\\phi\\) and \\(r\\sin\\phi\\); its squared magnitude\n                    is \\(r^2\\). Problems A symmetric step has stay-weight \\(a\\) and switch-weight \\(b\\). Write\n                            the two-step weights for returning and switching. Why can real \\(a,b\\) not make two steps\n                            equal NOT? For the operation \\(S\\) with \\(S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=(1+i)/2\\) and\n                            \\(S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=(1-i)/2\\), compute the two-step weights from \\(1\\) to \\(0\\) and to\n                            \\(1\\). Find every operation with \\(S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=a\\) and\n                            \\(S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=b\\) whose two steps give NOT. Show in particular that \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)\n                            cannot both be real."}, {"title": "Interference and phase", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-2", "text": "3.4.2 Interference and\n                phase § interference · coherent · global phase · relative\n                    phase When paths end at the same configuration, we add their amplitudes and then take the squared magnitude. The probability rule therefore gives \\[p(k)=\\Bigl|\\sum_{\\text{paths to }k}\\text{path amplitude}\\Bigr|^2.\\] The contributions can reinforce or cancel. For two Hadamard steps starting at \\(0\\), the two paths to \\(0\\) each contribute \\(1/2\\). They add to one, giving probability one. The two paths to \\(1\\) contribute \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). They cancel, giving probability zero. Taking the squared magnitude of each path separately would give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) at either endpoint. Adding the amplitudes first raises one probability and lowers the other. This effect is called interference . It is constructive when the probability is greater than the sum of the separate squared magnitudes, and destructive when it is smaller. Paths whose amplitudes combine in this way are called coherent . For complex amplitudes, phase determines how the contributions combine. Multiplying every amplitude by the same factor \\(e^{i\\theta}\\) changes all their phases by the same amount. Every later sum acquires that same factor, and its squared magnitude stays unchanged because \\(|e^{i\\theta}|=1\\). Such a common phase is called a global phase . It has no effect on the probabilities, even after further transitions. The phase difference between two amplitudes is their relative phase . Changing it can change their sum. For example, take the state amplitudes \\[(v_0,v_1)=\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\frac{e^{i\\phi}}{\\sqrt2}\\right).\\] Both squared magnitudes are \\(1/2\\), whatever the value of \\(\\phi\\). But the two contributions arriving at \\(0\\) after a Hadamard step are \\(1/2\\) and \\(e^{i\\phi}/2\\). Their sum depends on \\(\\phi\\), so \\[p(0)=\\left|\\frac{1+e^{i\\phi}}2\\right|^2=\\frac{1+\\cos\\phi}{2}.\\] At \\(\\phi=0\\), the contributions have the same sign and add to one. At \\(\\phi=\\pi\\), they have opposite signs and cancel. At \\(\\phi=\\pi/2\\), the probability is \\(1/2\\). The initial probabilities are the same in all three cases; the relative phase changes the probabilities after \\(H\\). A simple way to change this phase is to multiply the amplitude at \\(1\\) by \\(-1\\), leaving the amplitude at \\(0\\) unchanged. This is the operation \\(Z\\), with stay-weights \\(Z(0\\to0)=1\\) and \\(Z(1\\to1)=-1\\). It changes the relative phase by \\(\\pi\\). Starting at \\(0\\), two Hadamard steps return to \\(0\\); inserting \\(Z\\) between them gives \\(1\\) instead. An algorithm can use such phase changes to control which contributions reinforce and which cancel, and so change the final probabilities. Check your understanding What goes wrong if we take the squared magnitude of each path amplitude before adding? Compare your explanation Taking squared magnitudes first removes the signs and phases that allow cancellation. For the two paths to \\(1\\) under two Hadamard steps, it gives \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) instead of zero. Prepare equal-magnitude amplitudes with relative phase \\(\\phi\\), then apply \\(H\\). Only the routes to final configuration zero are shown. The phase changes their sum, even though it leaves the initial probabilities unchanged. Problems Two paths reach an outcome with amplitudes \\(1/2\\) and \\(i/2\\). What is\n                            its probability? Is the interference constructive, destructive, or neither? Two paths contribute \\(0.6\\) and \\(-0.2\\) to an outcome. Compare the\n                            probability obtained by adding first with the result of squaring first. Start with amplitudes \\((1/\\sqrt2,e^{i\\phi}/\\sqrt2)\\) and apply \\(H\\). The probability of \\(0\\) is \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Which values of \\(\\phi\\) give the same probabilities, and what extra operation could distinguish them?"}, {"title": "Kets, measurement, and path information", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-3", "text": "3.4.3 Kets, measurement, and path information § qubit · ket · superposition · measurement · record The amplitude rules now give us a way to describe quantum states and transitions. A two-state quantum system is a qubit ; its configurations are labelled \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). We denote the definite states by \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\), read “ket zero” and “ket one.” A ket is notation for a state vector, not a probability or an outcome. The amplitude pair \\((v_0,v_1)\\) is written \\[\\ket\\psi=v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1,\\qquad |v_0|^2+|v_1|^2=1.\\] This is a superposition of the two definite states. Its coefficients are the amplitudes we have been composing. In this notation the Hadamard step is \\[H\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad H\\ket1=\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Measurement connects this state description to a reported result. For an ideal measurement in the configuration basis, quantum mechanics makes two assertions: the result is \\(j\\) with probability \\(|v_j|^2\\), and, conditional on that result, the state becomes \\(\\ket j\\). The first assertion gives the probability rule a physical meaning; the second specifies a state update. These are quantum mechanical postulates, rather than consequences of path arithmetic. Chapter 4 gives their general formulation. From either definite input, one Hadamard step gives each result with probability \\(1/2\\). Two steps without a measurement between them restore the input by cancellation. If we measure after the first step, the state instead becomes \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\); the next \\(H\\) then gives equal probabilities from either result. Inserting a measurement changes which amplitudes are available to combine. We can also lose interference by leaving a record in another part of the system. For two qubits, \\(\\ket{ij}\\) denotes a joint configuration: the first label belongs to the first qubit and the second to the record. Contributions cancel only when these full joint configurations coincide. Interference requires the contributions to end at the same configuration. If another register\n                    distinguishes them, the configuration of the whole system is no longer the same, even when the qubit\n                    we intend to measure has the same value. This explains why a record can remove interference without\n                    anyone reading it. Let a second bit start at zero and record the intermediate value of the first. Write the first\n                    qubit’s label before the record’s label. Insert CNOT between two applications of \\(H\\), using the\n                    first qubit as control and the record as target. Each \\(H\\) acts only on the first qubit: \\[\\ket{00}\\xrightarrow{H}\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}\\bigr)\\xrightarrow{\\text{CNOT}}\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}\\bigr)\\xrightarrow{H}\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11}\\bigr).\\] The amplitudes contributing to first-qubit outcome one are still \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). They now\n                    belong to different joint configurations, \\(10\\) and \\(11\\). They cannot cancel. Ignoring the record\n                    means adding the probabilities of these distinguishable joint outcomes, giving \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) for\n                    the first qubit. The relevant change is the existence of a distinguishable record, not a reader’s knowledge of it. To\n                    restore the original interference, apply CNOT a second time before the final \\(H\\). This returns the\n                    record to zero on both alternatives. The state becomes \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), and the final\n                    \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket{00}\\). Here uncomputation restores the common endpoint needed for cancellation. Identical records preserve interference; perfectly distinguishable records remove it; partial\n                    distinguishability gives an intermediate result. For a quantum computation, tracking only the\n                    intended output is therefore insufficient. We must also track any workspace that distinguishes\n                    alternatives we want to interfere, and coherently return those records to a common state when\n                    needed. The next chapters provide the vector and composite-system descriptions needed to follow\n                    these dependencies systematically. The second qubit records the intermediate value of the first. The two routes shown both give\n                    first-qubit outcome one, but end in different joint configurations, \\(10\\) and \\(11\\). Their\n                    amplitudes cannot cancel: add their squared magnitudes when ignoring the record. The routes to\n                    first-qubit outcome zero are omitted. Problems In the record experiment, find the probability of each of the four final\n                            configurations, and the probability that the record shows \\(1\\). Check the final state \\(\\tfrac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})\\)\n                            by applying \\(H\\) to the qubit in \\(\\ket{00}\\) and in \\(\\ket{11}\\) separately. Replace the CNOT by an operation that records the path only partly: it\n                            leaves \\(\\ket{00}\\) unchanged and sends \\(\\ket{10}\\) to\n                            \\(\\ket1(\\cos\\alpha\\ket0+\\sin\\alpha\\ket1)\\). Show that the qubit then gives \\(1\\) at the end\n                            with probability \\((1-\\cos\\alpha)/2\\), and interpret the cases \\(\\alpha=0\\) and\n                            \\(\\alpha=\\pi/2\\)."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-1", "text": "Two paths reach an outcome with amplitudes \\(1/2\\) and \\(e^{i\\pi/3}/2\\). What is its probability? \\(|1+e^{i\\pi/3}|^2/4=(2+2\\cos60^\\circ)/4=3/4\\). Squaring each path first would give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\): the phases partly reinforce."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-2", "text": "An operation \\(G\\) on a qubit has the real weights \\(G(0\\to0)=G(1\\to1)=\\cos\\alpha\\), \\(G(0\\to1)=\\sin\\alpha\\), and \\(G(1\\to0)=-\\sin\\alpha\\). Check the two conditions on an operation, compute the two-step weights by path sums, and find the \\(\\alpha\\) for which two steps send \\(0\\) to \\(1\\). The squares leaving each configuration add to \\(\\cos^2\\alpha+\\sin^2\\alpha=1\\), and the cross sum is \\(-\\cos\\alpha\\sin\\alpha+\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha=0\\). From \\(0\\): weight \\(\\cos^2\\alpha-\\sin^2\\alpha=\\cos2\\alpha\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(2\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha=\\sin2\\alpha\\) at \\(1\\). From \\(1\\): \\(-\\sin2\\alpha\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(\\cos2\\alpha\\) at \\(1\\). At \\(\\alpha=\\pi/4\\), two steps send \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket1\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to \\(-\\ket0\\): NOT, except for a relative sign. Real weights reach NOT only up to this sign; exactly NOT needs complex weights."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-3", "text": "Apply \\(H\\), then the phase step with \\(w(0\\to0)=1\\) and \\(w(1\\to1)=e^{i\\phi}\\), then \\(H\\), to \\(\\ket0\\). Using path sums, find the probability of outcome \\(0\\) as a function of \\(\\phi\\), and the values of \\(\\phi\\) that make each outcome certain. To \\(0\\): \\(\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12e^{i\\phi}\\). To \\(1\\): \\(\\tfrac12-\\tfrac12e^{i\\phi}\\). The probability of \\(0\\) is \\(|1+e^{i\\phi}|^2/4=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Outcome \\(0\\) is certain at \\(\\phi=0\\) and outcome \\(1\\) at \\(\\phi=\\pi\\). A phase that no direct measurement sees controls the result completely."}, {"title": "State vectors and ket notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-1", "text": "4.1.1 State vectors and ket notation § state vector · ket A state vector collects the amplitudes into one object. Choose an order for the basis labels, then write the coefficients in that order: \\[\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\ket0=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\\ket1=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] The ket brackets name a vector; they do not perform an operation. The computational-basis probabilities are \\(|\\alpha|^2\\) and \\(|\\beta|^2\\), which add to one. For two qubits ordered \\(00,01,10,11\\), the same convention gives four coordinates. Keep that order beside a calculation until it becomes familiar: swapping two coordinates changes the represented state unless you also change the basis labels. Superposition is basis-dependent. The vector \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) combines two computational-basis vectors, but can itself be one vector of another basis. The physical state stays the same when we change how we describe it. The two coordinates are ordered by the selected basis. The coefficients belong to basis vectors, not to two simultaneous measurement outcomes. Problems Write \\(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}\\ket0+i\\sqrt{\\tfrac23}\\ket1\\) as a column vector, and check that its coordinates satisfy the condition for a state vector. With the two-qubit configurations ordered \\(00,01,10,11\\), write \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\) as a column vector, and give the probability of each configuration. Find numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) with \\(\\ket0=a\\,\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}+b\\,\\tfrac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\). Relative to which basis is \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) a superposition, and relative to which is it not?"}, {"title": "Operations as matrices", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-2", "text": "4.1.2 Operations as matrices § matrix · matrix product · identity matrix · linearity Applying an operation by path sums means writing out every arrow. Is there a more compact way? Collect the weights in a table whose entry in row \\(j\\) and column \\(i\\) is the weight of the arrow \\(i\\to j\\). This table is a matrix \\(U\\), with \\(U_{ji}=w(i\\to j)\\). One step changes the state by \\(v^\\prime_j=\\sum_iw(i\\to j)\\,v_i\\). In matrix language this is \\(v^\\prime_j=\\sum_iU_{ji}v_i\\), the matrix–vector product: \\(\\ket{\\psi^\\prime}=U\\ket\\psi\\). The sum and product rules for one step are exactly matrix multiplication. Column \\(i\\) of \\(U\\) holds the weights of the arrows leaving \\(i\\), so it is the state \\(U\\ket i\\). For the operations we have met, writing \\(X\\) for NOT and \\(\\operatorname{diag}(a,b)\\) for the matrix with diagonal entries \\(a,b\\) and zeros elsewhere, \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1),\\qquad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Two steps, first \\(U\\) and then \\(V\\), sum over the intermediate configuration \\(j\\): \\((VU)_{ki}=\\sum_jV_{kj}U_{ji}\\). This is the matrix product, with the rightmost matrix acting first. For example, \\(HH=I\\), the identity matrix. Why is a matrix determined by what it does to the basis states? Because \\(U(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)=\\alpha\\,U\\ket0+\\beta\\,U\\ket1\\): each configuration’s amplitude is carried along separately and the results add. This property, linearity , is the sum rule in another form. Which matrices are valid operations? The two conditions on the weights of an operation decide, and their compact form needs one more tool. \\(X\\) is fixed by its arrows \\(0\\to1\\) and \\(1\\to0\\). Each amplitude travels along its arrow, so \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) becomes \\(\\beta\\ket0+\\alpha\\ket1\\). Problems An operation has weights \\(w(0\\to0)=0\\), \\(w(0\\to1)=i\\), \\(w(1\\to0)=-i\\), and \\(w(1\\to1)=0\\). Write its matrix, and apply it to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). Compute the matrices \\(XZ\\) and \\(ZX\\), and apply each to \\(\\ket0\\). Does the order of the operations matter? A proposed rule sends \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) to \\(\\alpha\\ket0+2\\beta\\ket1\\), rescaled so that the squared magnitudes add to \\(1\\). Show that the rule is not linear by comparing its action on \\(\\ket0\\), \\(\\ket1\\), and \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Inner products, bras, and adjoints", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-3", "text": "4.1.3 Inner products, bras, and adjoints § inner product · norm · orthogonal · orthonormal · bra · adjoint · unitary The conditions on an operation were \\(\\sum_j|w(i\\to j)|^2=1\\) and \\(\\sum_jw(i\\to j)^*\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\) for \\(i\\neq k\\). Since \\(w(i\\to j)=U_{ji}\\), these are statements about the columns of \\(U\\): each column has squared magnitudes adding to \\(1\\), and for two different columns, the conjugated entries of one times the entries of the other add to \\(0\\). Such sums deserve a name. The inner product of two vectors is \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j.\\] Why the conjugate? So that \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j|\\psi_j|^2\\) is real and nonnegative. Without it, the one-coordinate vector \\((i)\\) would have “squared length” \\(i^2=-1\\). The norm \\(\\|\\psi\\|=\\sqrt{\\braket\\psi\\psi}\\) is the length of \\(\\ket\\psi\\); a state vector has norm \\(1\\). Two vectors are orthogonal when their inner product is \\(0\\), and a basis of orthogonal unit vectors is orthonormal . The bra \\(\\bra\\phi\\) is the row of conjugated coordinates of \\(\\ket\\phi\\), so that the row \\(\\bra\\phi\\) times the column \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is the inner product \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). For example, \\(\\bra0=(1,0)\\), and \\(\\braket0\\psi=\\alpha\\) picks out the first coordinate. The conditions on an operation now say that the columns of \\(U\\) form an orthonormal basis. To write this as one equation, define the adjoint \\(U^\\dagger\\) of a matrix as its conjugate transpose: \\((U^\\dagger)_{ij}=U_{ji}^*\\). Then \\((U^\\dagger U)_{ik}=\\sum_jU_{ji}^*U_{jk}\\) is the inner product of columns \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), and the conditions say exactly \\[U^\\dagger U=I.\\] A matrix with this property is unitary , and \\(U^\\dagger\\) is its inverse: reverse each arrow and conjugate its weight. Is the adjoint always the inverse? No. For the matrix \\(\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\), which is not unitary, the adjoint is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\) itself, while the inverse is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,1)\\). In general the adjoint is characterized by \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\): it moves a matrix from one side of an inner product to the other. For \\(v=(1,i)\\), the conjugate transpose is \\((1,-i)\\). Multiplying gives \\(1+1=2\\). Omitting conjugation would incorrectly give zero. Problems Compute \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\) for \\(\\ket\\phi=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Are the two states orthogonal? Find the adjoints of \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and of \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). Which of the two matrices is unitary? Using \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\), prove that \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). Conclude that the product of two unitaries is unitary."}, {"title": "State and evolution postulates", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-1", "text": "4.2.1 State and evolution postulates § postulate The experiments in Chapter 3 motivated a mathematical description. We now state its rules explicitly for finite-dimensional systems. They are physical postulates, tested through their predictions, rather than deductions from classical probability. Pure states. A pure state is a unit vector in a complex inner-product space. Vectors differing only by a global phase represent the same physical state. Closed-system evolution. Evolution is unitary: \\(\\ket\\psi\\mapsto U\\ket\\psi\\), with \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\). For a qubit , remove a global phase so its first coordinate is real and nonnegative. Then \\[\\ket\\psi=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\ket1,\\qquad0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi.\\] One angle fixes the two magnitudes; the other fixes their relative phase . At a pole, where one component vanishes, that relative phase has no effect. A pure-state vector is not the description of every preparation or every subsystem. Classical uncertainty about preparation and entanglement with another system call for density matrices, introduced when needed and developed in Appendix D. We begin with pure states so the calculations remain visible. The vector \\((3,4)\\) has norm five. Dividing each coordinate by five gives probabilities \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\), which add to one. Problems Normalize \\(\\ket0+2i\\ket1\\), and give the probabilities of a computational-basis measurement. Write the state \\((i\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) in the form \\(\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket1\\), after removing a global phase. Show that \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(e^{i\\gamma}\\ket\\psi\\) give the same probabilities for every sequence of operations followed by a computational-basis measurement. Then show that \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) do not differ by a global phase."}, {"title": "The measurement postulate", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-2", "text": "4.2.2 The measurement postulate § measurement postulate The measurement rule gives the probability of each outcome. What is the state after the measurement? Perform an ideal computational-basis measurement on a qubit and obtain \\(0\\), then measure it again immediately. Experiments find \\(0\\) again, every time. Which state gives \\(0\\) with certainty? Only \\(\\ket0\\), up to a global phase. So after the outcome \\(0\\), the qubit is in the state \\(\\ket0\\). Measurement. Measuring the state \\(\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\ket j\\) in the computational basis gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\alpha_j|^2\\), and leaves the system in the state \\(\\ket j\\). For \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), the outcome is \\(0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\); after the outcome \\(1\\), the state is \\(\\ket1\\). A single run gives a single outcome; the probabilities describe how often each outcome occurs. This also explains why measuring between two applications of \\(H\\) destroys their return to the start: after the measurement the qubit is \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\), and \\(H\\) applied to either gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\). Is a measurement a closed-system unitary evolution? No. When the outcome is \\(0\\), it sends both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) to \\(\\ket0\\). Different states merge, so a measurement cannot be undone and is not unitary. A measurement leaves the observed configuration, so an immediate second measurement repeats the result with certainty. Problems Measure \\((\\ket0+2i\\ket1)/\\sqrt5\\) in the computational basis. Give the probability of each outcome and the state after it. A qubit in \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) is measured twice in a row. Find the probabilities of the four sequences of outcomes \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\). Start from \\(\\ket0\\). Apply \\(H\\), measure, apply \\(H\\), and measure again. Find the probabilities of the four pairs of outcomes, and compare with applying \\(H\\) twice and measuring once."}, {"title": "Repeated measurements on independently prepared states", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-3", "text": "4.2.3 Repeated measurements on independently prepared states § One measurement produces one outcome. To test a predicted probability, prepare the state afresh on each run and measure every copy in the same way. If the outcome probability is \\(p\\), its observed fraction over \\(N\\) independent runs has standard deviation \\[\\sqrt{\\frac{p(1-p)}N}.\\] For \\(p=1/2\\), 100 runs give a standard deviation of 0.05; 10,000 give 0.005. Reducing this typical fluctuation tenfold costs a hundredfold increase in samples. This is an uncertainty scale, not a guarantee that every experiment lands within it. Measuring one qubit repeatedly is a different experiment. Write \\(\\ket+=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Once a computational-basis measurement of \\(\\ket+\\) gives zero, an immediate repeat gives zero again, assuming no intervening change. A freshly prepared \\(\\ket+\\) is still equally likely to give either outcome. Always specify which preparation is being repeated. After observing zero in a computational-basis measurement, the same qubit remains in \\(\\ket0\\). A fresh \\(\\ket+\\) still gives an equal distribution. Problems A preparation gives outcome \\(1\\) with probability \\(0.3\\). In \\(100\\) independent runs, what is the expected number of \\(1\\)s, and its standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}\\)? For \\(p=1/2\\), how many independent runs are needed for the standard deviation of the observed fraction, \\(\\sqrt{p(1-p)/N}\\), to be at most \\(0.01\\)? Someone measures a single qubit, prepared in \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), a hundred times in a row, obtains \\(0\\) every time, and concludes that the state was \\(\\ket0\\). What do the data actually show? Design an experiment that distinguishes \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) from \\(\\ket0\\)."}, {"title": "Changes of basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-1", "text": "4.3.1 Changes of basis § \\(\\pm\\) basis Is the computational basis special? Consider \\[\\ket+=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad\\ket-=\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Both are unit vectors, and \\(\\braket+-=\\tfrac12(1-1)=0\\), so they form another orthonormal basis. Every state can be written in this basis, and its coordinates are inner products: the coefficient of \\(\\ket+\\) in \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is \\(\\braket+\\psi\\). For example, \\(\\braket+0=\\braket-0=1/\\sqrt2\\), so \\[\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket++\\ket-}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Has anything physical changed? No. Writing \\(\\ket0\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis changes its coordinates, not the state. Applying a gate does change the state. The two can look alike on paper, so it matters to say which one is meant. The same holds for any orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\): \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_j\\braket{u_j}\\psi\\,\\ket{u_j}\\). These rows change coordinates without changing the physical state. The plus/minus basis is defined by \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Problems Write \\(\\ket1\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Write \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Let \\(\\ket{\\pm i}=(\\ket0\\pm i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Show that these two vectors form an orthonormal basis, and write \\(\\ket+\\) in it."}, {"title": "Measurement in an orthonormal basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-2", "text": "4.3.2 Measurement in an orthonormal basis § measurement in a basis · projector · projective measurement Apply \\(H\\) to a qubit and then measure it. Which property of the original state does the outcome reveal? \\(H\\) sends \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\). Write the original state as \\(a\\ket++b\\ket-\\); after \\(H\\) it is \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\). The measurement gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(|a|^2=|\\braket+\\psi|^2\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(|\\braket-\\psi|^2\\). “Apply \\(H\\), then measure” therefore measures the state in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Does this work for any orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\)? Let \\(U\\) be the unitary whose columns are the \\(\\ket{u_j}\\), so that \\(U\\ket j=\\ket{u_j}\\). Its inverse \\(U^\\dagger\\) sends \\(\\ket{u_j}\\) to \\(\\ket j\\). Applying \\(U^\\dagger\\) and then measuring gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\[|\\braket{j}{U^\\dagger\\psi}|^2=|\\braket{U j}\\psi|^2=|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2,\\] and applying \\(U\\) afterward turns the resulting state \\(\\ket j\\) into \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Measurement in a basis. Measuring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) in the orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\) gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2\\), and leaves the state \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Geometrically, \\(\\braket{u_j}\\psi\\,\\ket{u_j}\\) is the projection of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) onto the direction \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). With the projector \\(P_j=\\ket{u_j}\\bra{u_j}\\), the probability is \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\). More generally, a projective measurement is given by projectors \\(P_j\\) onto orthogonal subspaces with \\(\\sum_jP_j=I\\): outcome \\(j\\) has probability \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\) and leaves the state \\(P_j\\ket\\psi/\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|\\). When a subspace has more than one dimension, the outcome identifies the subspace, not a single vector. A real-coordinate illustration of the measurement rule. The projections are components used to calculate probabilities, not simultaneous fractional measurement results. Problems Measure \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Give the probability of each outcome. Let \\(\\ket{\\pm i}=(\\ket0\\pm i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Which operations, applied before a computational-basis measurement, measure a qubit in the basis \\(\\{\\ket{+i},\\ket{-i}\\}\\)? Check your answer on \\(\\ket{+i}\\). Let \\(U\\) be the unitary with columns \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Show that applying \\(U^\\dagger\\) and then measuring in the computational basis gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Overlaps under unitary evolution", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-3", "text": "4.3.3 Overlaps under unitary evolution § A unitary preserves more than total probability. It preserves every pairwise overlap: \\[\\braket{U\\phi}{U\\psi}=\\braket\\phi{U^\\dagger U\\psi}=\\braket\\phi\\psi.\\] It can rotate two states together, but cannot turn a nonorthogonal pair into an orthogonal pair. That will limit both discrimination and copying. A useful calculation has revealed a constraint on every possible unitary device. Could we use any matrix and normalize its output afterwards? Generally not. The normalization factor would depend on the input, so the resulting map would no longer be linear. A conditional measurement update does involve normalization, but it is accompanied by a probabilistic outcome. It is not a deterministic gate disguised as a formula. Unitary evolution describes the closed system. Measurement, discarded records, and environmental noise require a broader account. Keeping the scope clear prevents us from attributing all physical processes to one kind of map. An inner product between two inputs equals that between their unitary outputs. Orthogonal inputs stay orthogonal; nonorthogonal inputs do not become perfectly distinguishable. Problems Check that \\(H\\) preserves the overlap of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\). The matrix \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) is not unitary. Find two states whose overlap it changes. Prove that a matrix that preserves the norm of every vector preserves every inner product. Expand \\(\\|\\phi+\\psi\\|^2\\) and \\(\\|\\phi+i\\psi\\|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Eigenvectors and eigenspaces", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-1", "text": "4.4.1 Eigenvectors and eigenspaces § eigenvector · eigenvalue · eigenspace To attach numbers to measurement outcomes, we need the states that an operator only rescales. \\(Z\\) leaves \\(\\ket0\\) alone and multiplies \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(-1\\). A vector \\(\\ket v\\neq0\\) with \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\) is an eigenvector of \\(A\\) with eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\): \\(A\\) keeps its direction and multiplies it by \\(\\lambda\\). For \\(Z\\), the vectors \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) are eigenvectors with eigenvalues \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\). For \\(X\\), which exchanges \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\), we find \\(X\\ket+=\\ket+\\) and \\(X\\ket-=-\\ket-\\), with the same eigenvalues. What can the eigenvalues of a unitary be? A unitary preserves length, so \\(|\\lambda|=1\\). An eigenvector of a unitary only gains a phase. If several independent eigenvectors share an eigenvalue, every combination of them is again an eigenvector with that eigenvalue. Together they form an eigenspace . The outcome subspaces of a projective measurement will be the eigenspaces of the operator we are looking for. \\(Z\\) keeps each configuration and multiplies the amplitude at \\(1\\) by \\(-1\\). So \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) are eigenvectors, with eigenvalues \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\), but a state with \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) both nonzero is not. Problems Show that \\(\\ket{+i}=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket{-i}=(\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) are eigenvectors of \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\), and find their eigenvalues. Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of \\(H\\). Prove that every eigenvalue of a unitary has magnitude \\(1\\), and that eigenvectors of a unitary with different eigenvalues are orthogonal."}, {"title": "Hermitian operators and spectral projections", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-2", "text": "4.4.2 Hermitian operators and spectral projections § observable · Hermitian · spectral projectors A measurement distinguishes outcomes; an observable also assigns them numbers. For orthogonal outcome projectors \\(P_j\\), choose real labels \\(a_j\\) and form \\[A=\\sum_j a_jP_j.\\] This operator is Hermitian : \\(A^\\dagger=A\\). Conversely, a Hermitian matrix has real eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenspaces. Its spectral projectors define a measurement, with probability \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\) for the reported eigenvalue \\(a_j\\). For \\(A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,5)\\), we measure in the computational basis and report 2 or 5. Changing those labels changes the average reported number without changing which basis directions the apparatus distinguishes. There is one qualification: if distinct projectors receive the same number, the operator groups their subspaces into one eigenspace. Recording only that number loses the finer outcome distinction. The standard spectral measurement of \\(A\\) refers to these grouped eigenspaces. For \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(2,5)\\), the projectors select the two computational-basis components. The measurement reports the associated eigenvalue. Problems Is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&i\\\\-i&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) Hermitian? Find its eigenvalues. The observable \\(Z\\) reports \\(+1\\) for \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(-1\\) for \\(\\ket1\\). Find its average value, \\(\\sum_ja_jp_j\\), on \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) and on \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). An observable on three basis states has value \\(0\\) on \\(\\ket0,\\ket1\\) and value \\(1\\) on \\(\\ket2\\). For \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0+\\ket1+\\ket2)/\\sqrt3\\), find the probabilities and the state after obtaining \\(0\\). Why would measuring the full basis and merely hiding the distinction between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) be a different operation?"}, {"title": "Nonorthogonal state discrimination", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-3", "text": "4.4.3 Nonorthogonal state discrimination § Someone hands us one qubit, prepared either in \\(\\ket0\\) or in \\(\\ket+\\). Can we always tell which? Suppose a measurement could. Some outcome would then occur with certainty for \\(\\ket0\\) and never for \\(\\ket+\\). By the measurement postulate, \\(\\ket0\\) would lie in that outcome’s subspace and \\(\\ket+\\) would be orthogonal to it, so \\(\\braket0+=0\\). But \\(\\braket0+=1/\\sqrt2\\). No measurement identifies the two states with certainty. Could a cleverer procedure help, such as an operation before measuring, or an interaction that writes a record into another system? Any such operation is unitary and preserves the overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), so the records cannot become orthogonal either. The limit is structural, not a weakness of detectors. Two pure states can be distinguished perfectly exactly when they are orthogonal. A measurement can trade wrong answers for answers that report “inconclusive,” but it cannot always be right. The real slice shown contains \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) at \\(45^\\circ\\). Their overlap is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), so one copy cannot always reveal which was prepared. Angles here represent state-vector geometry, not physical space. Problems Which of these pairs can be distinguished perfectly by one measurement: \\(\\{\\ket0,\\ket1\\}\\); \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\); \\(\\{\\ket0,\\ket+\\}\\); \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket{+i}\\}\\), where \\(\\ket{+i}=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)? A qubit is \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). We measure in the computational basis and guess \\(\\ket+\\) when the outcome is \\(1\\) and \\(\\ket0\\) when it is \\(0\\). Find the probability of guessing correctly. Prove that two pure states can be distinguished perfectly by a measurement exactly when they are orthogonal: give a measurement when they are, and show that none exists when they are not."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is \\(Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\) Hermitian, unitary, both, or neither? Answer the same for \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). \\(Z\\) is both: \\(Z^\\dagger=Z\\) and \\(Z^\\dagger Z=Z^2=I\\). The second matrix is neither: its adjoint \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) differs from it, and \\(A^\\dagger A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}\\neq I\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-2", "text": "Normalize \\(3\\ket0+4i\\ket1\\). Find the probabilities of a measurement in the computational basis and in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. The normalized state is \\(\\tfrac35\\ket0+\\tfrac{4i}5\\ket1\\). Computational basis: \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\). \\(\\pm\\) basis: \\(\\braket\\pm\\psi=(3\\pm4i)/(5\\sqrt2)\\), each with squared magnitude \\(25/50=1/2\\). The relative phase \\(i\\) makes the \\(\\pm\\) outcomes equally likely, although the computational-basis probabilities are unequal."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-3", "text": "Prove that no unitary maps \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) to two orthogonal states. What does this imply for any procedure that tries to identify which of the two was prepared? \\(\\braket{U0}{U+}=\\braket0+=1/\\sqrt2\\neq0\\), so the outputs cannot be orthogonal. Any procedure built from operations followed by a measurement therefore cannot always identify the state: perfect identification would require orthogonal outputs."}, {"title": "Tensor products and product bases", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-1", "text": "5.1.1 Tensor products and product bases § tensor product · product basis A register of two qubits has four configurations, \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\), so its state has four amplitudes. Suppose we prepare the first qubit in \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\) and, independently, the second in \\(c\\ket0+d\\ket1\\). What are the four joint amplitudes? Reaching the configuration \\(01\\) means that the first qubit is \\(0\\) and the second is \\(1\\). The two preparations are independent, so by the product rule the amplitude is the product of the two local amplitudes, \\(ad\\). Ordering the configurations with the first qubit as the leftmost bit, \\[\\begin{pmatrix}a\\\\b\\end{pmatrix}\\otimes\\begin{pmatrix}c\\\\d\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}ac\\\\ad\\\\bc\\\\bd\\end{pmatrix}.\\] This combination is the tensor product . For basis states it gives \\(\\ket a\\otimes\\ket b=\\ket{ab}\\); for example, \\(\\ket0\\otimes\\ket1=\\ket{01}\\). The four vectors \\(\\ket{ab}\\) form the product basis . Two qubits need four amplitudes and three qubits need eight: dimensions multiply rather than add. How does an operation on the first qubit alone act on the pair? It changes the first label and leaves the second unchanged: each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) of \\(U\\), with weight \\(w\\), becomes the arrows \\(ib\\to jb\\), with the same weight, for each value of \\(b\\). We write this operation \\(U\\otimes I\\). On a product state , \\((U\\otimes I)(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi)=(U\\ket\\psi)\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\). The joint basis has one label for every pair of local labels. This is the Cartesian arrangement of tensor-product basis vectors, not a mixture. Problems Compute \\(\\bigl(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigr)^T\\otimes(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\bigl(\\tfrac35,\\tfrac45\\bigr)^T\\otimes(0,1)^T\\). Compute \\((H\\otimes I)\\ket{01}\\) and \\((I\\otimes H)\\ket{01}\\). Show that \\(\\braket{a\\otimes b}{c\\otimes d}=\\braket ac\\braket bd\\) for vectors of two coordinates, and deduce that the tensor product of two unit vectors is a unit vector."}, {"title": "Product states", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-2", "text": "5.1.2 Product states § product state Which joint states arise from independent preparations? A joint state of the form \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\) is a product state . Its amplitudes are products of local amplitudes, so its probabilities are products of local probabilities: the two qubits behave independently. Does a superposition of several joint configurations signal something more? Not necessarily. The state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) has two nonzero amplitudes, yet \\[\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0:\\] the first qubit is in an equal superposition, and the second is certainly \\(0\\). The real question is whether one local vector for each qubit reproduces every joint amplitude. When it does, the state is a product state. Both terms share the same second-qubit state. Factoring it out shows that this pure state is a product, despite having two nonzero amplitudes. Problems Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\) a product state? If so, factor it. Factor \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})/2\\), and find the probability that the second qubit gives \\(0\\). Show that a two-qubit state with amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{00},\\alpha_{01},\\alpha_{10},\\alpha_{11}\\) is a product state exactly when \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}=\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}\\)."}, {"title": "The composition postulate", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-3", "text": "5.1.3 The composition postulate § composition postulate The product rule tells us how independently prepared systems combine. Must every joint state be a product? Quantum mechanics does not require it. It takes the whole tensor-product space as the state space of the pair. Composition. The state space of a composite system is the tensor product of the state spaces of its parts. Its basis vectors are the products of basis vectors of the parts. A register of \\(n\\) qubits therefore has \\(2^n\\) basis vectors, and a general state needs \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. Does a measurement reveal \\(2^n\\) numbers? No: measuring all \\(n\\) qubits gives a single \\(n\\)-bit label. Storing a general \\(n\\)-qubit state on a classical computer takes up to \\(2^n\\) amplitudes, which quickly becomes impractical. Does that by itself make quantum computers powerful? No. An advantage needs a task, and a procedure that makes its answer observable efficiently. The number of basis coefficients doubles with each added qubit. One computational-basis measurement still returns only one n-bit label. Problems How many amplitudes describe a general state of \\(10\\) qubits? How many bits does a measurement of all \\(10\\) qubits return? A classical simulation stores each amplitude as two \\(8\\)-byte numbers. How much memory does a general \\(40\\)-qubit state need? A product state of \\(n\\) qubits is described by \\(n\\) pairs of amplitudes. Show that applying one one-qubit operation to each qubit of a product state can be simulated with work proportional to \\(n\\). What does this show about the size of the state space as a source of difficulty?"}, {"title": "Bell-state preparation", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-1", "text": "5.2.1 Bell-state preparation § Bell state Start from \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit, then CNOT with the first qubit as control. What happens? \\(H\\) gives \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), still a product state. CNOT acts on each configuration: it leaves \\(00\\) alone and sends \\(10\\) to \\(11\\): \\[\\ket{00}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] The result is a Bell state , written \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). The circuit drawing records this sequence, with time running from left to right. Does the vertical line of CNOT mean that the first qubit is measured and the result used to decide whether to flip the second? No. CNOT acts on both configurations of the superposition at once, without any measurement. That is why the result is a superposition of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), rather than one of them. A Hadamard followed by CNOT prepares a Bell state. Read the circuit from left to right. Predict first. Reset to \\(\\ket{00}\\). After \\(H\\) on the first qubit, which two outcomes are possible? Which outcomes remain after CNOT? Experiment: build a Bell state Follow the joint state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) through a Hadamard on the first qubit and then CNOT. The four bars show computational-basis probabilities. Apply H to the first qubit Reset State: \\(\\ket{00}\\) Both qubits start in 0. The final pure state is entangled: its joint amplitudes do not factor into two single-qubit states. Its probabilities alone would not distinguish it from a classical mixture. After trying it: compare your explanation After \\(H\\), \\(00\\) and \\(10\\) each have probability \\(1/2\\). CNOT changes \\(10\\) to \\(11\\), so the final outcomes are \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). The bars alone do not establish entanglement. Problems Apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit of \\(\\ket{10}\\), then CNOT with the first qubit as control. Which state results? Apply the same circuit to \\(\\ket{01}\\) and to \\(\\ket{11}\\). Show that the four states produced from \\(\\ket{00},\\ket{01},\\ket{10},\\ket{11}\\) form an orthonormal basis, and give a circuit that turns each of them back into its label."}, {"title": "Nonfactorization of amplitudes", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-2", "text": "5.2.2 Nonfactorization of amplitudes § entangled Try to factor the Bell state as \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\otimes(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\). Matching coefficients requires \\[ac=bd=1/\\sqrt2,\\qquad ad=bc=0.\\] The first pair makes all four factors nonzero; the second pair then cannot hold. The state is entangled : this pure joint state is not a product of pure local states. Each qubit still has a local state description, but it is a density matrix rather than a pure-state vector. For this Bell state, each local density matrix is \\(I/2\\). The pair’s vector contains relationships that no pair of local pure vectors can reproduce. Do correlated zero–one results establish entanglement ? No. Preparing \\(00\\) or \\(11\\) according to a fair classical coin gives the same computational-basis counts. We need a measurement that distinguishes that mixture from the coherent Bell superposition. A product \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\) would require \\(ac\\) and \\(bd\\) nonzero. Then all four factors are nonzero, contradicting \\(ad=bc=0\\). Problems Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})/2\\) entangled? For which real numbers \\(\\varepsilon\\) is \\((\\ket{00}+\\varepsilon\\ket{11})/\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon^2}\\) a product state? For a two-qubit state in which both outcomes of the first qubit are possible, measure the first qubit in the computational basis. Show that the state is entangled exactly when the conditional state of the second qubit depends on the outcome."}, {"title": "Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-3", "text": "5.2.3 Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities § joint probability · marginal · conditional probability Although neither qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) has an individual pure-state vector, each still has well-defined local measurement probabilities. How do we read probabilities from a joint state? With amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{ab}\\), the joint probability is \\(p(a,b)=|\\alpha_{ab}|^2\\). The probability that the first qubit gives \\(a\\), whatever the second gives, is the marginal \\(p(a)=\\sum_bp(a,b)\\), and the conditional probability of \\(b\\) given \\(a\\) is \\(p(b\\mid a)=p(a,b)/p(a)\\) when \\(p(a)\\gt 0\\). For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\); each qubit alone gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\); and once the first result is known, the second is certain. Now measure both qubits in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Rewriting \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) in that basis gives \\[\\ket{\\Phi^+}=\\frac{\\ket{++}+\\ket{--}}{\\sqrt2},\\] so the two results again agree with certainty. The classical mixture behaves differently. Each of \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\) is a product of states that give \\(+\\) and \\(-\\) with probability \\(1/2\\), so in the \\(\\pm\\) basis the mixture gives all four pairs of results with probability \\(1/4\\). Correlation in one basis is therefore not enough to establish entanglement; the measurement must be specified. For the Bell state, the row sums give the first-qubit probabilities. Conditional on the first bit being zero, only the \\(00\\) entry remains possible. Problems For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), compute \\(p(\\text{first}=1)\\) and \\(p(\\text{second}=1\\mid\\text{first}=1)\\). For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt3\\), compute the joint probabilities, both marginal distributions, and \\(p(\\text{second}=1\\mid\\text{first}=0)\\). Both qubits of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) are measured in the basis \\(\\ket{u_0}=\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1\\), \\(\\ket{u_1}=-\\sin\\theta\\ket0+\\cos\\theta\\ket1\\). Show that the two results agree with certainty, for every \\(\\theta\\)."}, {"title": "Basis-state copying", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-1", "text": "5.3.1 Basis-state copying § Can a quantum state be copied? CNOT seems to copy. With a blank target, \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\to\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\to\\ket1\\ket1\\). What does it do to a superposition? By linearity, \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\to\\alpha\\ket{00}+\\beta\\ket{11}.\\] Two copies would instead be \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\otimes(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)=\\alpha^2\\ket{00}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{01}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{10}+\\beta^2\\ket{11}.\\] These differ whenever \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are both nonzero. CNOT copies the labels \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), not superpositions of them. With a blank target, CNOT carries the amplitude \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(00\\) to \\(00\\) and the amplitude \\(\\beta\\) of \\(10\\) to \\(11\\). The result \\(\\alpha\\ket{00}+\\beta\\ket{11}\\) is not two copies of \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) unless \\(\\alpha\\) or \\(\\beta\\) is zero. Problems Apply CNOT to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\) and to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket1\\). For which states \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) does CNOT turn \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\) into two copies, \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\otimes(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\)? A unitary \\(U\\) copies both basis states: \\(U\\ket0\\ket0=\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(U\\ket1\\ket0=\\ket1\\ket1\\). Show that, whatever \\(U\\) does on other inputs, it sends \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket0\\) to an entangled state."}, {"title": "The no-cloning theorem", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-2", "text": "5.3.2 The no-cloning theorem § no-cloning theorem Could some cleverer unitary copy every state? Suppose \\(U\\ket\\psi\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\) for every state \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Take two states \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(\\ket\\phi\\). A unitary preserves inner products, and the inner product of two product states is the product of the inner products of their parts, so \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi\\braket00=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2,\\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2.\\] The overlap must therefore be \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). For two different states that are not orthogonal, such as \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) with overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), this fails. No unitary copies every state. This is the no-cloning theorem . What does the theorem not forbid? States known to belong to an orthogonal set, such as basis labels, can be copied, and a state that we know how to prepare can simply be prepared again. What is impossible is a single fixed operation that perfectly copies an arbitrary unknown state. Let \\(c=\\braket\\psi\\phi\\). A unitary cloning map would preserve input overlap \\(c\\), while two copies have overlap \\(c^2\\). The value \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) cannot satisfy that requirement. Problems Compute \\(\\braket0+\\) and \\(\\braket{00}{++}\\). Why do these values rule out a unitary that copies both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\)? Show that a unitary can copy each of the two states \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) into a register prepared in \\(\\ket0\\). Give one. A cloning machine may change its own state: \\(U\\ket\\psi\\ket0\\ket e=\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\ket{e_\\psi}\\), where the final machine state \\(\\ket{e_\\psi}\\) may depend on \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Show that it can still clone two states only if they are orthogonal or equal up to phase."}, {"title": "Local states and no-signalling", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-3", "text": "5.3.3 Local states and no-signalling § density matrix · no-signalling Alice and Bob share \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). Bob’s local state is \\(I/2\\): every orthonormal qubit-basis measurement gives two equally likely outcomes. Alice cannot change this distribution by applying a local unitary or by making a measurement whose result she does not communicate. For the computational-basis example, Alice gets zero or one with equal probability. Conditional on her outcome, Bob has the matching basis state. Without her message, he averages over the alternatives: \\[\\tfrac12\\ket0\\bra0+\\tfrac12\\ket1\\bra1=I/2.\\] Changing Alice’s measurement basis changes the conditional ensemble, but not Bob’s averaged density matrix. Appendix D gives the general local-operation argument. This is why the correlations cannot carry a chosen message by themselves. The outcome is not under Alice’s control, and Bob’s local statistics do not reveal her choice. Learning a remote outcome changes his conditional predictions only after the ordinary message arrives. For the Bell pair, either local computational-basis distribution is fair. Learning the remote outcome permits conditioning; without it, the local distribution remains unchanged. Problems Alice and Bob share \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). Alice measures her qubit and does not report the result. What is the probability that Bob obtains \\(0\\) in a computational-basis measurement? Alice applies \\(X\\) to her qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), without measuring. Find the new joint state and Bob’s computational-basis probabilities. Alice measures her half of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) in either the computational basis or the \\(\\pm\\) basis, without reporting her result. Bob tests for any normalized state \\(\\ket v=a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\). Show that his probability of success is \\(1/2\\) for either choice. Why is this stronger than checking only Bob’s computational-basis outcomes?"}, {"title": "Entanglement resources", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-1", "text": "5.4.1 Entanglement resources § Alice holds a qubit in an unknown state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) and wants Bob to have it. She cannot copy it, and measuring it would reveal only one bit. What can she do if they share a Bell pair? The three qubits start in \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\); Alice holds the first two and Bob the third. Alice applies CNOT from her input qubit to her half of the Bell pair, then \\(H\\) to her input qubit. Nothing quantum is sent to Bob. To see what has happened, we follow the complete three-qubit state. Alice and Bob first share a Bell pair. Alice’s joint measurement and two-bit message allow Bob to select the correction; the shared pair is consumed in transferring the input state. Problems In teleportation, how many qubits does each party hold at the start? Write the initial three-qubit state for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket1\\). For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket1\\), apply Alice’s CNOT and \\(H\\), and group the result by the configuration of Alice’s two qubits. Suppose Alice sends Bob only the bit \\(m\\), and Bob applies \\(Z^m\\). Show that for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\) his qubit is wrong with probability \\(1/2\\)."}, {"title": "Measurement and conditional corrections", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-2", "text": "5.4.2 Measurement and conditional corrections § teleportation Write Alice’s unknown input as \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\). After her CNOT and Hadamard, grouping the three-qubit state by her two basis labels gives \\[\\frac12\\bigl(\\ket{00}\\ket\\psi+\\ket{01}X\\ket\\psi+\\ket{10}Z\\ket\\psi+\\ket{11}XZ\\ket\\psi\\bigr).\\] This identity is the protocol. Every branch has squared norm \\(1/4\\), regardless of \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\). Alice measures her two qubits, sends their values \\((m,n)\\), and Bob receives the conditional state \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\). Alice’s bits Bob’s conditional state Bob applies, in time order 00 \\(\\ket\\psi\\) Nothing 01 \\(X\\ket\\psi\\) \\(X\\) 10 \\(Z\\ket\\psi\\) \\(Z\\) 11 \\(XZ\\ket\\psi\\) \\(X\\), then \\(Z\\) The correction works because \\(Z^mX^nX^nZ^m=I\\). Nothing requires Bob to know the amplitudes. He only needs to know which reversible disturbance to undo. All four outcomes work; teleportation does not wait for a lucky one. To check the identity, expand the initial state \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), apply Alice’s two gates to each term, and collect equal first-two-bit labels. The next problems let you test the same mechanism on specific inputs. Alice’s two measured bits control Bob’s \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) corrections. Dashed lines carry classical information; the Bell pair is prepared at the left. Problems Alice obtains \\((m,n)=(1,0)\\). Which state does Bob hold, and which correction does he apply? For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket+\\) and Alice’s result \\((1,1)\\), write Bob’s state before correction, and check that the correction restores \\(\\ket+\\). Bob applies his corrections in the wrong order: \\(Z^m\\) first, then \\(X^n\\). Show that he still obtains \\(\\ket\\psi\\), up to a global phase."}, {"title": "State transfer and classical communication", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-3", "text": "5.4.3 State transfer and classical communication § Before the classical bits arrive, Bob averages over four possible corrections. The resulting state is \\(I/2\\), independently of \\(\\ket\\psi\\). His qubit is already present, but he cannot use it as the recovered input or learn the input’s amplitudes from it. The useful transfer waits for the message. Nor is there a second copy left with Alice. Her measured qubits are basis states, and the shared Bell pair has been consumed. Conditioned on the communicated outcome and Bob’s correction, the unknown input is now at Bob. Keep three descriptions separate: the full joint state, Bob’s state given Alice’s result, and Bob’s state when that result is unknown. Confusing them makes teleportation look like instantaneous communication or cloning. Tracking them turns it into an explicit calculation with the rules we already have. The two-bit message selects a correction using a previously shared Bell pair. It is not a classical list of the unknown amplitudes. Problems Before the classical bits arrive, what is the probability that Bob obtains \\(0\\) when measuring his qubit, for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\)? Show that, before the bits arrive, Bob obtains \\(0\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) for every input \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\). After Bob’s correction, write the three-qubit state for a particular measurement result \\((m,n)\\). Do Alice’s qubits retain a second copy? Explain the scope of your conclusion."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\) entangled? \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}=0\\) but \\(\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}=1/3\\), so the state is not a product: it is entangled."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-2", "text": "Prepare \\(\\ket{\\Psi^-}=(\\ket{01}-\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) from \\(\\ket{00}\\) using \\(X\\), \\(H\\), and CNOT gates. Show that measuring both qubits in the same basis \\(\\{\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1,\\,-\\sin\\theta\\ket0+\\cos\\theta\\ket1\\}\\) always gives opposite results. \\(X\\otimes X\\) gives \\(\\ket{11}\\); \\(H\\) on the first qubit gives \\((\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\); CNOT gives \\((\\ket{01}-\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). For real basis vectors, \\(\\braket{u_a\\otimes u_b}{\\Psi^-}=\\bigl(u_a(0)u_b(1)-u_a(1)u_b(0)\\bigr)/\\sqrt2\\), which is \\(0\\) when \\(a=b\\). So equal results never occur, in every such basis."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-3", "text": "Teleport \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). For each of Alice’s four outcomes \\((m,n)\\), give Bob’s state before correction and the correction he applies. \\((0,0)\\): \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), no correction. \\((0,1)\\): \\((i\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(X\\). \\((1,0)\\): \\((\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(Z\\). \\((1,1)\\): \\((-i\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(X\\) and then \\(Z\\). In every case the result is \\(\\ket\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Constant and balanced Boolean functions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-1", "text": "6.1.1 Constant and balanced Boolean functions § constant · balanced · promise Here is a small question about a function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\): is \\(f(0)\\) equal to \\(f(1)\\)? If they are equal, \\(f\\) is constant ; if they differ, \\(f\\) is balanced . For a one-bit input, every function is one or the other. What do we actually need to learn? One bit: equal or different. We do not need the values \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f(1)\\) themselves. Can an algorithm learn that one bit without learning both values? The gap between the answer we want and the full data is what the quantum algorithm will exploit. For inputs of more bits, a function need not be constant or balanced. Restricting attention to those two cases is then a promise about the input. Deutsch’s task asks whether the two outputs agree. It does not require identifying which truth table in a class was supplied. Problems List the four functions \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) as pairs \\((f(0),f(1))\\), and classify each as constant or balanced. Among the functions \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^2\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), how many are constant, how many are balanced (output \\(1\\) on exactly half the inputs), and how many are neither? For \\(f\\) on \\(n\\)-bit inputs, promised to be constant or balanced, show that a deterministic classical algorithm can decide which with \\(2^{n-1}+1\\) queries, and that \\(2^{n-1}\\) queries do not always suffice."}, {"title": "Classical query access", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-2", "text": "6.1.2 Classical query access § query Suppose the only access to \\(f\\) is a query : we choose \\(x\\) and receive \\(f(x)\\). How many queries does a classical algorithm need to answer with certainty? After one query, say with the reply \\(f(0)=0\\), both possibilities remain: \\(f(1)=0\\) makes \\(f\\) constant, and \\(f(1)=1\\) makes it balanced. One query cannot settle the question; two clearly suffice. This count belongs to the stated access model. If \\(f\\) were given in another form, for instance as a formula we could inspect, the comparison could change. If a classical query returns \\(f(0)=0\\), both a constant and a balanced truth table remain possible. A second value is needed for an exact classical answer. Problems A classical algorithm queries \\(f(1)\\) and receives \\(1\\). Which of the four functions remain possible? For \\(f\\) on two-bit inputs, promised constant or balanced, an algorithm queries \\(f(00)\\) and \\(f(01)\\). If they differ it answers “balanced”; otherwise it makes one more query. Complete the algorithm, and give its number of queries in the worst case. Show that no one-query classical algorithm for \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), even one that chooses its query at random, is correct with probability greater than \\(1/2\\) on every function."}, {"title": "Reversible quantum oracles", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-3", "text": "6.1.3 Reversible quantum oracles § oracle · black-box How can a quantum computation query \\(f\\)? A query must be a valid operation, hence unitary , while \\(f\\) itself may merge its inputs. The reversible embedding of a function solves this: keep the input and add \\(f(x)\\) to a target bit. \\[U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,\\,y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] On the four configurations, \\(U_f\\) is a rearrangement: it exchanges \\((x,0)\\) and \\((x,1)\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\) and leaves them alone otherwise. Its matrix is a permutation matrix, so it is unitary, and applying it twice restores every configuration. What counts as one query? One use of \\(U_f\\), whatever state it is applied to, including a superposition . This is the black-box , or oracle , model: it counts the uses of \\(U_f\\) and ignores how \\(U_f\\) is built. The construction costs return when circuits are analysed in detail. Configurations are written \\(xy\\), here with \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\). Where \\(f(x)=1\\) the two target values exchange; elsewhere nothing moves. One arrow enters each configuration, so \\(U_f\\) is reversible even when \\(f\\) is not one-to-one. Problems Write the action of \\(U_f\\) on the four basis states for \\(f=(1,0)\\), that is, \\(f(0)=1\\) and \\(f(1)=0\\). Write the matrix of \\(U_f\\) for \\(f=(0,1)\\) in the order \\(00,01,10,11\\), and check that it is unitary. Show that the map \\(\\ket{x,y}\\mapsto\\ket{x,f(x)}\\), which overwrites the target, is not unitary for any \\(f\\), and that \\(\\ket x\\mapsto\\ket{f(x)}\\) is not unitary when \\(f\\) is constant."}, {"title": "Oracle action on superpositions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-1", "text": "6.2.1 Oracle action on superpositions § What happens if we query \\(f\\) on both inputs at once? Prepare the input in \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and the target in \\(\\ket0\\). By linearity, \\(U_f\\) acts on each configuration separately: \\[U_f\\left(\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\right)=\\frac{\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Have we computed both values? Both appear in the state, but nothing has read them. Whether this helps depends on what we do next. With \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), each amplitude follows its own arrow. The input superposition with a blank target becomes \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\): the target is now correlated with the input. Problems Apply \\(U_f\\) for \\(f=(1,1)\\) to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\). For each of the four functions, write \\(U_f\\bigl(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\bigr)\\), and say which results are entangled. With \\(n\\) input qubits in the uniform superposition and a clean target, \\(U_f\\) produces \\(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\). Measuring both registers gives a uniformly random \\(x\\) with \\(f(x)\\). About how many runs are needed, on average, before every value \\(f(x)\\) has been seen?"}, {"title": "Register measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-2", "text": "6.2.2 Register measurement § Measure the joint state immediately after the query and you obtain one pair \\((x,f(x))\\), chosen at random. The other function value does not appear as a second answer in the detector. Repeating the preparation obtains more samples, but a classical method can query randomly chosen inputs too. Merely placing both function values in a state has not produced an advantage. The useful target is narrower: make “equal” and “different” lead to distinguishable final states. The remaining gates must act before measurement, while there are still coherent amplitudes to combine. For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), a computational-basis measurement samples one of these four labels. It does not output the full list of amplitudes. Problems Measure both registers of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). What are the possible results and their probabilities? How many function values does one run reveal? To decide constant or balanced, we repeatedly prepare \\((\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)})/\\sqrt2\\) and measure both registers until both inputs have been seen. How many runs does this take on average? Show that measuring only the input register of \\((\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)})/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) each for all four functions."}, {"title": "Workspace entanglement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-3", "text": "6.2.3 Workspace entanglement § The blank target creates a problem. If \\(f(0)\\ne f(1)\\), it holds an orthogonal record of which input branch occurred. Applying \\(H\\) to the input alone gives a random computational-basis outcome; the two contributions now end with different target labels and cannot cancel each other. This is the path-record example from Chapter 3 in a new setting. The question is no longer whether we can evaluate the function. It is whether we can use the evaluation without keeping a record that spoils the readout. We need a target preparation that remains the same physical state under either possible update, while still allowing the updates to contribute different phases. Eigenstates of the target flip provide exactly that possibility. Different workspace records can prevent the desired input interference. The shared-workspace example factors; the branch-record example does not. The common normalization factor \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) is omitted in the labels. Problems For \\(f=(0,1)\\), apply \\(H\\) to the input of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), and find the probability of each input outcome. For the constant function \\(f=(0,0)\\), repeat the calculation: start from \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\), apply \\(U_f\\), then \\(H\\) to the input. Combining the two previous problems, design a one-query procedure with a clean target that never wrongly answers “balanced.” With what probability does it detect a balanced function?"}, {"title": "Bit-flip eigenstates", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-1", "text": "6.3.1 Bit-flip eigenstates § The oracle either flips the target or leaves it alone. Which target states does a flip leave unchanged, apart from a factor? The eigenvectors of \\(X\\): \\[X\\ket+=\\ket+,\\qquad X\\ket-=-\\ket-,\\] since \\(X(\\ket0-\\ket1)=\\ket1-\\ket0\\). A flip therefore multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\), and no flip leaves it with the factor \\(+1\\): the target state stays the same while the factor records whether a flip occurred. The state \\(\\ket+\\) is unchanged in both cases and cannot tell them apart. The target we want is \\(\\ket-\\). A bit flip moves each amplitude to the other configuration. For \\(\\ket-\\) the result is \\(-\\ket-\\): the target is unchanged apart from a sign, which the oracle can pass to the input. Problems Compute \\(X(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Is \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) an eigenvector of \\(X\\)? Find every state that \\(X\\) leaves unchanged apart from a factor, by solving \\(X(a,b)^T=\\lambda(a,b)^T\\). The target is prepared in \\(\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1\\), with real \\(\\theta\\), and the oracle flips it. Show that its overlap with the original target is \\(\\sin2\\theta\\), and that the target keeps no record of the flip only when it is \\(\\ket+\\) or \\(\\ket-\\)."}, {"title": "Phase-oracle construction", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-2", "text": "6.3.2 Phase-oracle construction § phase kickback Apply \\(U_f\\) to \\(\\ket x\\ket-\\). If \\(f(x)=0\\), nothing happens; if \\(f(x)=1\\), the target flips, which multiplies the state by \\(-1\\): \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-.\\] With the input in \\(\\ket+\\), \\[U_f\\ket+\\ket-=\\frac{(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket0+(-1)^{f(1)}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket-.\\] The target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) whatever \\(x\\) is, so it holds no record of the input. The function value has moved into the sign of the input amplitude . This is phase kickback . What do the signs mean? If \\(f\\) is constant, the two signs are equal and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket+\\); the common sign is a global phase . If \\(f\\) is balanced, the signs differ and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket-\\). The answer has become a relative phase . The target returns to the same minus state, while the input receives a function-dependent phase. Problems For \\(f=(1,0)\\), compute \\(U_f\\ket+\\ket-\\). Prepare the input in \\(\\ket+\\), but mistakenly prepare the target in \\(\\ket+\\) too. Find the output of \\(U_f\\) for each of the four functions. Does leaving the target unentangled suffice to obtain useful phase kickback? With \\(n\\) input qubits and the target in \\(\\ket-\\), show that \\(U_f\\bigl(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x\\ket x\\bigr)\\ket-=\\bigl(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\bigr)\\ket-\\), and that the target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) for every \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Coherent uncomputation", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-3", "text": "6.3.3 Coherent uncomputation § compute, phase, uncompute What if \\(f\\) is not given as an oracle but computed by a circuit \\(A\\) that uses workspace, \\(A\\ket{x,0,0}=\\ket{x,f(x),g(x)}\\)? Apply \\(Z\\) to the answer bit, which multiplies the state by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\), then run \\(A\\) backward: \\[A^\\dagger Z_{\\mathrm{answer}}A\\ket{x,0,0}=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x,0,0}.\\] The workspace returns to \\(0\\), and the sign remains. This is compute, phase, uncompute . It is not free: it uses \\(A\\) and its inverse, and an honest count of the cost must include both. For a basis input \\(x\\), compute \\(f(x)\\) into clean workspace, apply \\(Z\\) to that bit, and reverse the computation. The function-dependent sign remains while the workspace returns to zero. Problems For \\(f(x)=x\\), computed into a clean answer bit by CNOT, compute \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes Z)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\) on \\(\\ket{0,0}\\) and \\(\\ket{1,0}\\). Compute \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes Z)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\) on all four basis states, and identify the operation. Why does compute, phase, uncompute need the workspace to start at \\(0\\)? Omit the uncomputation: compute \\(f(x)=x\\) into the answer bit with CNOT and apply \\(Z\\) to the answer bit, but do not run CNOT again. Starting from \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\), apply \\(H\\) to the input at the end. Show that the outcome is random, whereas with uncomputation it is certain."}, {"title": "Interference and readout", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-1", "text": "6.4.1 Interference and readout § After the query, the input is \\((s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), with \\(s_x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\). How do we read the relative sign? Apply \\(H\\), which sends \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\): \\[H\\,\\frac{s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{s_0+s_1}2\\ket0+\\frac{s_0-s_1}2\\ket1.\\] Equal signs give \\(0\\) with certainty; opposite signs give \\(1\\) with certainty. In terms of paths, the two contributions to the wrong answer cancel. The outcome depends only on whether \\(s_0\\) and \\(s_1\\) agree, never on \\(f(0)\\) or \\(f(1)\\) separately. We learn the one bit we wanted, and nothing more. The final \\(H\\) sends each signed amplitude along two arrows. At \\(0\\) the contributions add and at \\(1\\) they subtract, so equal signs give \\(0\\) with certainty and opposite signs give \\(1\\). Problems Compute \\(H\\bigl((-\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\bigr)\\). For each of the four functions, compute the final input amplitudes \\((s_0+s_1)/2\\) and \\((s_0-s_1)/2\\). Show that if the final \\(H\\) is omitted, or replaced by \\(X\\), the measurement gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) each, for every function."}, {"title": "Quantum and classical query counts", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-2", "text": "6.4.2 Quantum and classical query counts § Deutsch’s algorithm · query separation Prepare \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\), apply \\(H\\) to each qubit , query \\(U_f\\) once, apply \\(H\\) to the input, and measure that input. Outcome zero means constant; outcome one means balanced. This is Deutsch’s algorithm, with exact success in the ideal model. An exact classical value-query method needs two queries in the worst case. The comparison uses the same underlying function but different allowed access: classical queries supply a basis input and read a value; the quantum algorithm applies its specified reversible oracle coherently to a superposition. That access assumption is part of the model. The result is a one-versus-two query separation, not a claim that this tiny circuit runs faster on actual hardware. It matters because the entire mechanism is visible: choose a property, encode it in phase, and turn that phase into an observable answer. Deutsch’s algorithm: prepare, query, recombine, and measure. Only the joint U_f box counts as an oracle query. Predict first. Compare a constant function with a balanced function. Before running the circuit, predict whether the final input bit is 0 or 1. Would complementing every function value change that bit? Experiment: an oracle changes relative phase Prepare the input in \\(\\ket{+}\\) and the target in \\(\\ket{-}\\). The oracle leaves the target unchanged and multiplies each input amplitude by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\). A final Hadamard makes the relative sign observable. Function f(0) = 0, f(1) = 0 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 0 Apply oracle Reset |0⟩ +1/√2 |1⟩ +1/√2 Both input amplitudes have the same sign. Choose a function, then apply its oracle. A common sign changes no probabilities. Opposite signs change the result after the final Hadamard. After trying it: compare your explanation A constant function gives 0; a balanced function gives 1. Complementing the function multiplies the state after the phase oracle by a common minus sign, so it does not change the final measurement. Problems Count the queries and the other operations in Deutsch’s algorithm, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\). Run Deutsch’s algorithm for \\(f=(1,0)\\), giving the state after each step. With \\(n\\) input qubits, after the query and \\(H\\) applied to each input qubit, show that the amplitude of the input configuration \\(0^n\\) is \\(2^{-n}\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\). Deduce that one query distinguishes constant from balanced functions with certainty."}, {"title": "Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-3", "text": "6.4.3 Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement § The circuit has four jobs: prepare coherent alternatives, encode the desired property, recombine the amplitudes, and read the result. A missing job gives a specific failure. A target starting in \\(\\ket0\\) can keep a branch record. A target starting in \\(\\ket+\\) keeps no such record, but contributes no distinguishing phase either. The choice \\(\\ket-\\) avoids both problems: it remains factored from the input while supplying the required signs. Without the final \\(H\\), those signs still give the same zero–one probabilities. Correctness therefore belongs to the whole preparation–query–readout chain, not to the oracle alone. Search will use the same phase-marking idea, but its readout needs repeated reflections to make a marked label likely. The target \\(\\ket-\\) lets the oracle encode a phase without keeping an input-dependent target record. The final \\(H\\) converts that phase into the answer. Problems Name the four stages of Deutsch’s algorithm and the operations used in each. Replace the target \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(\\ket+\\), and run the algorithm for \\(f=(0,1)\\). What does the measurement give? Leave out the first \\(H\\) on the input, so that the input stays \\(\\ket0\\). Show that the algorithm then gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\) for every function."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(f=(0,1)\\), what does \\(U_f\\) do to \\(\\ket1\\ket-\\)? The target flips, which multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\): \\(U_f\\ket1\\ket-=-\\ket1\\ket-\\). The function value appears as a sign, and the target is unchanged."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-2", "text": "Run the \\(n\\)-qubit version of Deutsch’s algorithm for \\(n=2\\) and the balanced function \\(f(x_1x_2)=x_1\\). Show that the outcome \\(00\\) never occurs. After the query, the input is \\(\\tfrac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})=\\ket-\\ket+\\). Applying \\(H\\otimes H\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\), so the outcome is \\(10\\), never \\(00\\). The function is identified as balanced with one query."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-3", "text": "Show that Deutsch’s algorithm gives the same outcome for \\(f\\) and for its complement \\(1-f\\), and that the outcome depends only on \\(f(0)\\oplus f(1)\\). For \\(1-f\\), each sign \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\) is multiplied by \\(-1\\), a global phase that changes no probability. The outcome is \\(1\\) exactly when \\(s_0\\neq s_1\\), that is, when \\(f(0)\\oplus f(1)=1\\). The algorithm extracts this one bit and nothing more about \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Search predicates and classical queries", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-1", "text": "7.1.1 Search predicates and classical queries § marked · predicate A predicate \\(f\\) checks a candidate: \\(f(x)=1\\) means marked, and zero means unmarked. We want to find a marked label among \\(N\\) possibilities. With exactly one mark promised, testing \\(N-1\\) distinct labels classically suffices in the worst case: if every test fails, infer the remaining label. Even allowing a fixed nonzero error probability, an unstructured classical method still needs order \\(N\\) queries. The promise explains the exact count; the scaling is what we will compare. Begin with four labels and mark \\(11\\). We will first calculate every amplitude , then find the pattern that survives when four becomes \\(N\\). The predicate is given through an oracle , so marking 11 in the worked example is not advance knowledge supplied to the search algorithm . In this four-item example only \\(11\\) is marked. Testing a supplied candidate is different from locating the successful one. Problems A predicate on three-bit labels marks the labels whose bits add to \\(2\\). How many labels are marked, and what is the probability that a uniformly random label is marked? One label among \\(N=8\\) is marked. A classical search queries labels in a uniformly random order, without repetition, until it finds the marked one. What is the expected number of queries? One label among \\(N\\) is marked, in a uniformly random position. Show that any classical algorithm making \\(k\\) queries finds it with probability at most \\(k/N\\)."}, {"title": "State preparation and phase marking", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-2", "text": "7.1.2 State preparation and phase marking § uniform superposition Can a quantum search do better than testing candidates one at a time? It can begin with all candidates at once. Two Hadamards prepare the uniform superposition \\[\\ket s=\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11}}2.\\] How should a query act? Phase kickback turns a query into a sign: \\[O_f\\ket x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x.\\] With \\(11\\) marked, the amplitudes become \\((1,1,1,-1)/2\\). Has this helped? Not yet. Every probability is still \\(1/4\\), and measuring now finds \\(11\\) with probability \\(1/4\\), as a guess would. The query has created a relative sign, and a further operation must turn that sign into probability. The phase oracle \\(O_f\\) may be supplied directly, or built by phase kickback or by compute, phase, uncompute. Its cost in queries must be stated accordingly. The oracle keeps every configuration and multiplies the amplitude of the marked item \\(11\\) by \\(-1\\). Every immediate measurement probability stays \\(1/4\\), so another operation is needed to turn the sign into an advantage. Problems For \\(N=4\\) with the label \\(01\\) marked, write the amplitudes after preparing the uniform superposition and applying the phase oracle. For \\(N=8\\) with one marked label, what are the amplitudes after preparation and marking, and what is the probability of measuring the marked label? Show that applying the phase oracle any number of times, with nothing in between, never changes any measurement probability."}, {"title": "Reflection and final amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-3", "text": "7.1.3 Reflection and final amplitudes § reflection about the mean Which operation turns a negative sign into a larger amplitude? Look at the amplitudes \\((1/2,1/2,1/2,-1/2)\\). Their mean is \\(\\mu=1/4\\). The marked amplitude lies far below the mean, and the others slightly above it. Reflect every amplitude about the mean, \\(a_x\\mapsto2\\mu-a_x\\): \\[\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14-\\tfrac12=0,\\qquad-\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14+\\tfrac12=1.\\] One marking and one reflection find \\(11\\) with certainty. Is reflection about the mean a valid quantum operation? It is the matrix \\[D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I.\\] Indeed, \\(\\braket s a=\\sum_xa_x/\\sqrt N\\), and every entry of \\(\\ket s\\) is \\(1/\\sqrt N\\), so the entries of \\(2\\ket s\\braket sa\\) are \\(2\\mu\\), and \\(D\\ket a\\) has entries \\(2\\mu-a_x\\). Since \\(D^2=4\\ket s\\bra s-4\\ket s\\bra s+I=I\\) and \\(D^\\dagger=D\\), it is unitary . It does not estimate the mean from samples; it acts on the amplitudes directly. A complete two-qubit search for 11. The first controlled-Z marks 11. The remaining coherent gates implement diffusion up to global phase. Both measured bits are one. Problems Reflect the amplitudes \\(\\tfrac12(1,-1,1,1)\\) about their mean. For \\(N=8\\) with one marked label, compute the amplitudes after one marking and one reflection about the mean, and the success probability. Show directly from \\(a_x\\mapsto2\\mu-a_x\\) that reflection about the mean preserves the sum of squared amplitudes, for real amplitudes."}, {"title": "Good and bad subspaces", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-1", "text": "7.2.1 Good and bad subspaces § good and bad subspaces The four-item calculation looks expensive to repeat for millions of labels. Its symmetry saves us. Starting uniformly, all marked labels always share one amplitude and all unmarked labels share another. Group the \\(M\\) marked labels into the normalized vector \\(\\ket G\\), and the \\(N-M\\) unmarked labels into \\(\\ket B\\). Their supports are disjoint, so they are orthogonal. For \\(0 \\[\\ket s=\\sqrt{M/N}\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-M/N}\\ket B.\\] Why the square roots? The good group contains \\(M\\) terms of squared magnitude \\(1/N\\), so its total weight is \\(M/N\\). Its coefficient on a normalized good vector must have that squared magnitude. These vectors are tools for analyzing the algorithm. Constructing \\(\\ket G\\) explicitly would require knowing the marked set, which is precisely what we do not know. The available oracle and uniform-state reflection preserve their plane without requiring that knowledge. For one marked item among four, \\(\\ket{G}=\\ket{11}\\) and \\(\\ket{B}=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\). These orthogonal directions contain the initial state and the entire ideal search trajectory. Problems For \\(N=8\\) with the marked labels \\(011\\) and \\(101\\), write \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). For \\(N=8\\) and \\(M=2\\), write \\(\\ket s\\) as a combination of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). Show that the oracle \\(O_f\\) and the reflection \\(D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\) both map the plane of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\) into itself, by computing their action on \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\)."}, {"title": "Initial success amplitude", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-2", "text": "7.2.2 Initial success amplitude § success probability Where does \\(\\ket s\\) lie in this plane? Write \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{M/N}\\), with \\(0\\lt\\theta\\lt\\pi/2\\). Then \\[\\ket s=\\sin\\theta\\,\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\,\\ket B:\\] the state makes the angle \\(\\theta\\) with the bad direction, and its success probability is \\(\\sin^2\\theta=M/N\\). For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), or \\(30^\\circ\\). Why is one angle enough, however many labels the register has? Both the oracle and the reflection map the plane to itself, so the state never leaves it. With one marked item among four, \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\). The good amplitude is the vertical component; the success probability is its square. Problems Find the angle \\(\\theta\\) for \\(N=16\\) with \\(M=1\\), and for \\(N=8\\) with \\(M=2\\). For \\(N=8\\) and \\(M=2\\), how many iterations give success with certainty? Show that, for \\(0\\lt M\\lt N\\), exactly one Grover iteration gives certain success precisely when \\(M/N=1/4\\)."}, {"title": "Reflections and rotations", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-3", "text": "7.2.3 Reflections and rotations § Grover iteration What does the oracle do in this plane? It negates \\(\\ket G\\) and keeps \\(\\ket B\\): a reflection across the bad axis. The operation \\(D\\) is a reflection across \\(\\ket s\\). Two reflections in a plane make a rotation, by twice the angle between their axes. Here the axes are the bad direction and \\(\\ket s\\), at the angle \\(\\theta\\), so each Grover iteration \\(DO_f\\) rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\) toward the good direction. For \\(N=4\\), the state starts at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\), and the reflection across \\(\\ket s\\) sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\), the good direction: the certain success found above. The picture also predicts what happens if we continue. The rotation does not stop at the good direction; it carries the state past it. For \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\), the initial state is at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\) about the bad axis; reflection about the initial direction then sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\). These are real coordinates in the good/bad plane. Problems For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), by how many degrees does each Grover iteration rotate the state? For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), give the angle of the state and the success probability after \\(1\\), \\(2\\), \\(3\\), and \\(4\\) iterations. In the plane with the bad direction as the first axis and the good direction as the second, reflection across the line at angle \\(\\alpha\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\alpha&\\sin2\\alpha\\\\\\sin2\\alpha&-\\cos2\\alpha\\end{pmatrix}\\). Verify that reflecting across the bad axis and then across \\(\\ket s\\) is a rotation by \\(2\\theta\\)."}, {"title": "Amplitude evolution", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-1", "text": "7.3.1 Amplitude evolution § Where is the state after \\(k\\) iterations, and how likely is success? It starts at the angle \\(\\theta\\) and turns by \\(2\\theta\\) each time, so it reaches \\((2k+1)\\theta\\): \\[\\ket{\\psi_k}=\\sin\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket G+\\cos\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket B,\\qquad p_k=\\sin^2\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr).\\] Which marked label do we find? All marked labels share the amplitude of \\(\\ket G\\), so given success, the result is uniform over the marked set. The success probability depends on the marked fraction \\(M/N\\), not on which labels are marked. For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(p_k=\\sin^2((2k+1)\\pi/6)\\). One iteration is ideal, but the next two return to probability \\(1/4\\). Stopping is part of the algorithm. Problems For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), compute \\(p_k=\\sin^2\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\) for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=3\\). For \\(N=64\\) and \\(M=1\\), which number of iterations maximizes the success probability, and what is that probability? Show that, given success, the measured label is uniformly distributed over the \\(M\\) marked labels, and that, given failure, it is uniform over the unmarked labels."}, {"title": "Iteration count and overshooting", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-2", "text": "7.3.2 Iteration count and overshooting § overshooting · stopping rule Stop near the first time the rotating state reaches the good axis: \\[(2k+1)\\theta\\approx\\pi/2,\\qquad k\\approx\\frac{\\pi}{4\\theta}-\\frac12.\\] Choose a nearby nonnegative integer. For a small marked fraction, \\(\\theta\\approx\\sqrt{M/N}\\), giving order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\) iterations rather than the classical order \\(N/M\\) tests. The four-item case happens to land exactly on the axis after one iteration. Most cases do not. With \\(N=16,M=1\\), three iterations give success about 0.961. The point is a high success probability with fewer queries, not a universal promise of certainty. Continuing is not automatically helpful. For four items the probabilities at \\(k=0,1,2\\) are \\(1/4,1,1/4\\). The same reversible rotation that increases the good component eventually decreases it. A stopping rule is therefore part of the algorithm’s proof, not an afterthought about implementation. Predict first. Set Search space to 4 and reset. There is one marked item; start from equal amplitudes. Predict the success probability after one iteration and after a second. Does another iteration always help? Experiment: rotate toward the marked item There is one marked item. A full Grover iteration rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\), where \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt N\\). Keep stepping after the first peak to see why overshooting matters. Search space 4 16 64 256 One iteration Play Reset Iterations 0 unmarked component → marked component ↑ Probability of finding the marked item: 6.25%. The initial state spreads probability uniformly over all items. After trying it: compare your explanation One iteration gives success probability 1. A second gives \\(1/4\\): continued rotation can move the state away from the marked direction. Compare this with the initial probability \\(1/4\\). Problems For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), compute the success probability after three iterations. A search has \\(N=16\\) items. You plan three Grover iterations assuming one marked item, but there are actually four. What success probability do three iterations give? How many iterations would work if you knew there were four? Show that the sequence of success probabilities \\(p_0,p_1,p_2,\\dots\\) is periodic when \\(\\theta\\) is a rational multiple of \\(\\pi\\), and find the period for \\(N=4\\), \\(M=1\\)."}, {"title": "Unknown marked fraction and verification", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-3", "text": "7.3.3 Unknown marked fraction and verification § verification What if we do not know \\(M\\), and so cannot compute the stopping point? A fixed guess may overshoot. A standard approach chooses a random number of iterations from a range that grows gradually, measures, and checks the candidate with one query to \\(f\\). When \\(M\\gt 0\\), such schedules still need an expected number of queries of order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\). What if nothing is marked? Then no check ever succeeds, and an unbounded search never ends; it cannot prove that nothing is marked. A bounded version stops after a fixed budget and reports failure, with a stated probability of being wrong when marked labels do exist. That is a statistical guarantee, not a proof of absence. If every label is marked, the very first measurement succeeds. Further detail: Boyer, Brassard, Høyer, and Tapp: Tight bounds on quantum searching . A failed verification rejects that candidate, not every possible candidate. With an unknown marked fraction, the schedule must choose how long to search before measuring again. Problems An algorithm succeeds with probability \\(p\\) per attempt and checks its candidate with one query. How many attempts does it need on average to find a marked label? For \\(N=1024\\), the iteration count tuned for \\(M=1\\) is \\(k=25\\). What is the success probability if in fact \\(M=4\\)? Each verified attempt succeeds with probability at least \\(1/4\\), independently. Show that \\(t\\) attempts all fail with probability at most \\((3/4)^t\\), and find the smallest \\(t\\) for which this is below \\(10^{-6}\\)."}, {"title": "Arbitrary state preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-1", "text": "7.4.1 Arbitrary state preparation § Was the uniform start essential? Suppose a unitary \\(A\\) prepares \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and \\(P_G\\) projects onto the outcomes we count as successes, with success probability \\(p=\\|P_G\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\), where \\(0\\lt p\\lt 1\\). Normalizing the good and bad parts writes \\[\\ket\\psi=\\sqrt p\\,\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-p}\\,\\ket B.\\] This is the same plane as before, with \\(\\sin^2\\theta=p\\). Where might \\(A\\) come from? A classical randomized procedure can sometimes be made coherent and serve as \\(A\\), but only through an explicit reversible implementation, including its random bits and its workspace. Access to a routine that produces samples is not enough. A nonuniform preparation can still be resolved into normalized good and bad components. This example has \\(p=1/4\\), so its amplitudes are \\(1/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt3/2\\) regardless of how many underlying labels contribute. Problems A preparation succeeds with probability \\(p=1/9\\). Find \\(\\theta\\) and the amplitudes of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). For \\(p=1/9\\), how many amplification iterations maximize success, and what is the success probability? A one-qubit preparation \\(A\\) gives \\(A\\ket0=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\ket0+\\tfrac12\\ket1\\), and success means outcome \\(1\\). Show that one amplification iteration succeeds with certainty, even though this preparation is not uniform."}, {"title": "Reflection using preparation and its inverse", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-2", "text": "7.4.2 Reflection using preparation and its inverse § coherent access The search reflected about \\(\\ket s\\). How do we reflect about \\(\\ket\\psi\\) without knowing it? Reflection about \\(\\ket0\\), \\(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\), is easy: it negates every configuration except \\(0\\). Bring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) with \\(A^\\dagger\\), reflect there, and return with \\(A\\): \\[R_\\psi=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I=A\\bigl(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\bigr)A^\\dagger.\\] Applied to a state, the rightmost factor \\(A^\\dagger\\) acts first. Together with \\(I-2P_G\\), which negates the successful part, \\(R_\\psi\\) rotates the state toward the good direction exactly as in search. What does this require? Coherent access to \\(A\\), to its inverse, and to the success test. Measuring whether a sample succeeded is no substitute: it destroys the superposition instead of reflecting it. The inverse preparation acts first. Conjugating the zero-state reflection produces reflection about the prepared state. Problems For \\(A=H\\) on one qubit, write \\(R_\\psi=A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger\\) as a matrix. Show that \\(A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I\\) when \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and that it is unitary. Why can the reflection \\(R_\\psi\\) not be replaced by measuring whether the prepared state succeeded? Show that measuring after each oracle step makes the success probability stay at \\(p\\)."}, {"title": "Success probability and resource cost", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-3", "text": "7.4.3 Success probability and resource cost § amplitude amplification Independent trials with success probability \\(p\\) need order \\(1/p\\) repetitions for constant success. Coherent amplitude amplification needs order \\(1/\\sqrt p\\) rounds when \\(p\\) is small and the required reflections are available. Each round contains the preparation \\(A\\), its inverse, and the success reflection. If preparation costs \\(C_A\\) and the reflection costs \\(C_G\\), a useful estimate is \\[\\text{cost}\\sim\\frac{2C_A+C_G}{\\sqrt p},\\] up to constants, the initial preparation, and verification. Chapter 9 gives a precise notation for such scaling estimates. The denominator is the benefit; the numerator is the work that must be paid for each time. A more successful but much more expensive preparation need not win overall. Chapter 8 opens these boxes into gates so that the comparison can be made honestly. For small \\(p\\), the improvement concerns repeated use of coherent preparation and the success test. Their implementation costs must be included. Problems For \\(p=0.01\\), compare the number of independent trials needed for a reasonable chance of success with the number of amplification iterations. Preparing \\(A\\) costs \\(100\\) gates and the success test \\(20\\) gates. For \\(p=10^{-4}\\), compare the total cost of about \\(1/p\\) independent trials, each a preparation and a test, with about \\(\\tfrac\\pi4/\\sqrt p\\) amplification iterations, each using \\(A\\), \\(A^\\dagger\\), and the test. Preparing an arbitrary list of \\(N\\) amplitudes costs about \\(N\\) gates. For a search among \\(N\\) items with one marked item, so that \\(p=1/N\\), compare the cost of amplitude amplification with a classical scan of the list."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(N=32\\) and \\(M=2\\), find \\(\\sin\\theta\\) and the initial success probability. \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{2/32}=1/4\\), and the initial success probability is \\(1/16\\), the same as for one marked item among sixteen."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-2", "text": "For \\(N=32\\) and \\(M=2\\), find the number of Grover iterations that maximizes success, and the success probability. With \\(\\theta\\approx14.48^\\circ\\), the success probabilities for \\(k=2,3,4\\) are about \\(0.91\\), \\(0.96\\), and \\(0.58\\). The best is \\(k=3\\), with success about \\(0.96\\). Only the marked fraction matters, not \\(N\\) or \\(M\\) separately."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-3", "text": "For \\(N=4\\) with two marked items, show that Grover iterations never change the success probability. \\(\\theta=45^\\circ\\), so the angles \\((2k+1)45^\\circ\\) are odd multiples of \\(45^\\circ\\), and \\(\\sin^2\\) of every one is \\(1/2\\). Iterating never helps: when half the items are marked, a random guess is already as good as the algorithm can do."}, {"title": "Wires and gate ordering", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-1", "text": "8.1.1 Wires and gate ordering § circuit · wire A circuit draws one wire for each qubit and a box for each operation, with time running left to right. A wire identifies a subsystem; it need not carry a definite zero or one. In an entangled computation, follow the joint state of all wires. If \\(U\\) acts first and \\(V\\) second, the state becomes \\(VU\\ket\\psi\\). The drawing’s time order and the matrix product’s right-to-left action describe the same sequence. Gates on disjoint wires commute: \\((U\\otimes I)(I\\otimes V)=U\\otimes V\\). Gates sharing a wire generally do not. This lets us recognize possible parallel work in the diagram, while leaving physical scheduling to the hardware model. The circuit applies \\(H\\) first and \\(Z\\) second, as the columns run from left to right. The combined operator is written \\(ZH\\): on a column vector the rightmost matrix acts first. Problems A circuit applies \\(H\\) to qubit 1, then \\(X\\) to qubit 2, then CNOT with qubit 1 as control and qubit 2 as target, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\). Write the state after each step. Apply, to \\(\\ket{00}\\), first \\(H\\) on qubit 1 and then CNOT, and in the other order. Do the two operations commute? Prove that \\((U\\otimes I)(I\\otimes V)=(I\\otimes V)(U\\otimes I)=U\\otimes V\\): gates on different wires commute."}, {"title": "Circuits for query and search algorithms", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-2", "text": "8.1.2 Circuits for query and search algorithms § Read the algorithms we have met as circuits. In Deutsch’s circuit, the first pair of \\(H\\) gates prepares \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), the oracle box puts the function into signs, and the last \\(H\\) exposes their relation. In search, a block consisting of the phase oracle and the reflection \\(D\\) is repeated. Does a box labelled \\(O_f\\) tell us how to build \\(O_f\\)? No. Drawing an operation as a single box does not make it a single elementary gate; its cost is unknown until we say how it is implemented. A query diagram counts uses of an oracle. Replacing each box by a circuit exposes the gate cost, plus any operations between calls. Problems In the four-item search circuit, how many oracle uses and how many Hadamard gates does one Grover iteration contain, and how many Hadamard gates prepare the start? List the gates of Deutsch’s algorithm in time order, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\), with the qubits each acts on. An oracle box \\(O_f\\) on \\(n\\) qubits marks one fixed string. It is built from \\(X\\) gates and a phase flip controlled on all \\(n\\) qubits, which in turn uses Toffoli gates and \\(n-2\\) workspace qubits. Count the gates in such a construction."}, {"title": "Measurement and classical control", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-3", "text": "8.1.3 Measurement and classical control § classically controlled · coherent controlled gate A measurement symbol produces a classical outcome. The qubit itself may remain available in its conditional post-measurement state; the symbol does not mean that the physical system ceases to exist. In these drawings, solid lines carry quantum systems and dashed lines carry classical records. A classically controlled correction chooses a gate using a recorded bit. A coherent controlled gate instead acts on an unmeasured control and can preserve a superposition of branches. Teleportation uses both kinds of control. Alice’s CNOT is coherent. Bob’s correction is selected after receiving her classical outcomes. Replacing the first by a measurement-controlled flip would change the protocol; replacing the second requires retaining and handling the outcomes coherently, rather than simply deleting the message. Classical control uses a recorded outcome. It selects a branch after measurement rather than preserving a coherent superposition of both control alternatives. Problems Bob receives a bit \\(b\\) and applies \\(X\\) only if \\(b=1\\). Write his operation for each value of \\(b\\). For the input \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\), compare (a) CNOT followed by measuring the control with (b) measuring the control first and then applying \\(X\\) to the target if the result is \\(1\\). Show that the outcomes and final states agree. Now the control is not measured at the end. Compare CNOT with “measure the control, then apply \\(X\\) if \\(1\\)” on the input \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), by applying \\(H\\) to the control afterward and measuring it."}, {"title": "Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-1", "text": "8.2.1 Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates § Pauli matrices · rotation gate · phase gates Which one-qubit gates do we need? We have met three: \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] A fourth, \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\), completes the Pauli matrices \\(I\\), \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\). Fixed gates turn a qubit by fixed amounts. A general operation, such as the half of a NOT met earlier, needs turns of any size. For a Pauli matrix \\(P\\), define the rotation gate \\[R_P(\\theta)=\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I-i\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P.\\] Is it unitary ? Since \\(P^\\dagger=P\\) and \\(P^2=I\\), we get \\(R_P(\\theta)^\\dagger R_P(\\theta)=\\cos^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I+\\sin^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P^2=I\\). It is. Two fixed rotations will matter when gates must come from a finite set: the phase gates \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and \\(T=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\pi/4})\\). Here \\(S\\) names the conventional phase gate, not the square root of NOT from Chapter 3. These gates change the relative phase of \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(\\pi/2\\) and by \\(\\pi/4\\). Predict first. Starting from \\(\\ket0\\), compare applying \\(H\\) twice with applying \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Predict both final measurement distributions before trying the gates. Experiment: a gate changes amplitudes Start in \\(\\ket{0}\\). Apply \\(H\\) twice, then compare \\(HZH\\) with a single \\(X\\). Orange marks a negative amplitude; the bar length shows its squared magnitude. Apply H Apply X Apply Z Reset to \\(\\ket0\\) State: \\(1\\ket0+0\\ket1\\) Choose a gate to change the state. This experiment uses real amplitudes. It illustrates X, Z, and H, not every possible qubit state. After trying it: compare your explanation \\(H\\) followed by \\(H\\) returns \\(\\ket0\\), so 0 is certain. \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\) produces \\(\\ket1\\), so 1 is certain. \\(Z\\) changes a relative sign that the final \\(H\\) converts into a different outcome. \\(X\\) exchanges basis labels; \\(Z\\) changes one sign; \\(H\\) changes basis. Their action on arbitrary superpositions follows by linearity. Problems Compute \\(R_X(\\pi)\\) and \\(R_Z(\\pi)\\), and compare them with \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\). Verify by multiplying matrices that \\(HZH=X\\), \\(HXH=Z\\), \\(S^2=Z\\), and \\(T^2=S\\). Show that \\(R_Z(\\theta)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-i\\theta/2},e^{i\\theta/2})\\), and that up to a global phase it is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})\\). Which values of \\(\\theta\\) give \\(S\\) and \\(T\\)?"}, {"title": "Controlled and entangling gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-2", "text": "8.2.2 Controlled and entangling gates § controlled-\\(Z\\) How do we make a gate act on a target only when a control qubit is \\(1\\)? Use the projectors onto the two values of the control: \\[\\text{controlled-}U=\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes U.\\] It keeps the control and applies \\(U\\) to the target in the branch where the control is \\(1\\). CNOT is controlled-\\(X\\). Controlled-\\(Z\\) multiplies only \\(\\ket{11}\\) by \\(-1\\). Can one be made from the other? Since \\(HXH=Z\\), applying \\(H\\) to the target before and after a CNOT gives controlled-\\(Z\\). Do controlled gates always entangle? Not on every input: CNOT leaves \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) a product state . On \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\), however, it produces a Bell state . Hadamards on the target convert controlled-X to controlled-Z. Problems Apply controlled-\\(Z\\) to the four basis states. Show that exchanging the roles of control and target gives the same operation. Verify that \\((I\\otimes H)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes H)\\) acts as controlled-\\(Z\\) on \\(\\ket{10}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\). Let \\(\\ket v\\) be an eigenvector of \\(U\\) with \\(U\\ket v=e^{i\\phi}\\ket v\\). Show that controlled-\\(U\\) sends \\(\\ket+\\ket v\\) to \\(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)\\otimes\\ket v\\). Is the result entangled?"}, {"title": "Oracle and reflection circuits", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-3", "text": "8.2.3 Oracle and reflection circuits § How is a phase oracle built? To mark one bit string, flip with \\(X\\) each of its positions that is \\(0\\), apply a phase flip controlled on all qubits being \\(1\\), and undo the flips. For a predicate computed by a circuit, use compute, phase, uncompute. And the reflection \\(D\\)? Since \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) maps \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) to \\(\\ket s\\), we can reflect about \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) between two layers of Hadamards: \\[D=H^{\\otimes n}\\bigl(2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I\\bigr)H^{\\otimes n}.\\] The two-qubit drawing implements the reflection about \\(\\ket{00}\\) with \\(X\\) gates and a controlled-\\(Z\\), and so produces \\(-D\\). The sign is a global phase and changes nothing in an ordinary Grover iteration ; it would matter if the iteration were itself controlled. The oracle and reflection are decomposed here for one explicit predicate; a general predicate needs its own circuit. Problems Which gates mark the string \\(01\\) on two qubits, that is, multiply \\(\\ket{01}\\) by \\(-1\\) and leave the other basis states alone? For two qubits, verify that \\((H\\otimes H)(2\\ket{00}\\bra{00}-I)(H\\otimes H)\\) and \\(2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\) agree on \\(\\ket{00}\\). Show that \\((X\\otimes X)\\,CZ\\,(X\\otimes X)=-(2\\ket{00}\\bra{00}-I)\\). Why does this sign not matter in an ordinary Grover iteration, and when would it matter?"}, {"title": "Limits of local operations", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-1", "text": "8.3.1 Limits of local operations § Can one-qubit gates alone produce every operation? A circuit of one-qubit gates acts as \\(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\). On a product state it gives another product state: \\[(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n)(\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\ket{\\psi_n})=U_1\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\ket{\\psi_n}.\\] It can never prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\), however many gates it uses. The argument settles the question without counting anything: a universal set needs a gate that lets qubits affect one another. Applying \\(A\\) to one qubit and \\(B\\) to the other gives \\((A\\ket\\psi)\\otimes(B\\ket\\phi)\\). Starting with a product, local unitary gates alone cannot create entanglement. Problems Apply \\(H\\otimes H\\) to \\(\\ket{00}\\), and check that the result is a product state. A circuit applies \\(H\\) to qubit 1, then \\(X\\) to qubit 2, then \\(H\\) to qubit 1 again. Find the result on \\(\\ket{00}\\), and explain why it had to be a product state. Show that one-qubit operations cannot turn an entangled two-qubit state into a product state."}, {"title": "Entangling gates and universality", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-2", "text": "8.3.2 Entangling gates and universality § universal CNOT together with arbitrary one-qubit unitaries can construct every finite-register unitary. This is a universality theorem about the whole set ; possessing one entangling gate with no adequate local controls is not enough. One proof first decomposes a unitary into transformations acting on pairs of basis states, then builds those transformations from controlled operations and one-qubit gates. We state the theorem here; its full synthesis proof is beyond the algorithms we need. Universality settles possibility, not cost. A generic \\(n\\)-qubit unitary has exponentially many parameters and typically needs an exponentially long circuit. Useful algorithms work because their operations have additional structure. Our oracle and reflection constructions are examples of that structure made explicit. Further detail: Barenco et al.: Elementary gates for quantum computation . Arbitrary one-qubit unitaries together with CNOT suffice to construct arbitrary finite-register unitaries. A chosen finite gate alphabet instead generally requires approximation. Problems Which of these gate sets can prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\): \\(\\{H,X,Z\\}\\); \\(\\{H,\\text{CNOT}\\}\\); \\(\\{\\text{CNOT},X\\}\\)? Show that circuits of CNOT and \\(X\\) gates only permute computational basis states. A reversible circuit on \\(m\\) wires uses \\(g\\) gates, each an \\(X\\), CNOT, or Toffoli on chosen wires. Show that there are at most \\((3m^3)^g\\) such circuits, and deduce that most Boolean functions of \\(n\\) bits need at least about \\(2^n/\\log_2(3m^3)\\) gates."}, {"title": "Exact synthesis and approximation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-3", "text": "8.3.3 Exact synthesis and approximation § Clifford+T · operator-norm error A finite gate alphabet produces only countably many finite circuits, while unitaries vary continuously. Exact synthesis of every unitary is therefore impossible. Approximation is the useful goal: a suitable set, such as Clifford+T, approximates arbitrary quantum operations up to a global phase. Choose consistent phase representatives and ask for \\(\\|U-V\\|\\leq\\epsilon\\). This operator-norm bound guarantees that the two output vectors differ by at most \\(\\epsilon\\) on every unit input. If the operation will itself be controlled, its relative phase must be specified; it cannot simply be discarded as a global phase of the larger circuit. A circuit with \\(L\\) approximated gates has total operator-norm error at most \\(\\sum_i\\epsilon_i\\). To meet a budget \\(\\epsilon\\), one simple allocation gives each gate tolerance \\(\\epsilon/L\\). The triangle-inequality proof appears in the problems. An approximation claim therefore has two parts: can we reach the requested tolerance, and how many gates does doing so require? Universality answers only the first. The task is to find a finite circuit \\(V\\) close enough to a target \\(U\\). The tolerance controls acceptable error and affects the required circuit length. Problems Each of \\(1000\\) gates is approximated within \\(10^{-6}\\). What error bound holds for the whole circuit? A circuit has \\(10^6\\) gates and its total error must be at most \\(10^{-2}\\). What accuracy must each gate have if the error is split equally? Prove that \\(\\|U_2U_1-V_2V_1\\|\\leq\\|U_2-V_2\\|+\\|U_1-V_1\\|\\) for unitaries \\(U_1,U_2,V_1,V_2\\)."}, {"title": "Gate count, depth, and workspace", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-1", "text": "8.4.1 Gate count, depth, and workspace § gate count · depth · width · ancilla · uniform What does a circuit cost? Three measures matter: the gate count , the total number of elementary gates; the depth , the number of layers of gates that must run one after another; and the width , the number of qubits, including extra workspace qubits called ancillas . These trade off. More workspace can reduce depth, while recomputing an intermediate value instead of storing it can save qubits. Ancillas must start in a known state and are usually returned to it by uncomputation , so that they can be reused. There is a subtler cost. An algorithm needs a circuit for every input length \\(n\\). A circuit family is uniform if a classical procedure produces the circuit for each \\(n\\), and the family counts as efficient only if that procedure is efficient too. Otherwise a short circuit could hide hard work in its design. Four independent gates on four wires can occupy one layer. Four dependent gates on one wire require four layers. Parallelism may use more workspace. Problems A circuit on three qubits applies \\(H\\) to each qubit, then CNOT from qubit 1 to qubit 2, then CNOT from qubit 2 to qubit 3. Give its gate count, depth, and width. Compute the AND of \\(n=8\\) bits with a chain of Toffoli gates: \\(w_1=x_1x_2\\), \\(w_2=w_1x_3\\), and so on, then the output, then uncompute the workspace. Give the gate count, depth, and width. Compute the AND of the same \\(8\\) bits with a balanced tree instead: combine bits in pairs, then pairs of results, and so on. Compare gate count, depth, and workspace with the chain."}, {"title": "Connectivity and data preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-2", "text": "8.4.2 Connectivity and data preparation § connectivity · SWAP gate Can any two qubits interact? In a circuit diagram, yes; in a device, often only neighbouring qubits can. Gates between distant qubits then need SWAP gates to bring them together, which adds to the count and the depth. Is loading data free? Preparing an arbitrary list of amplitudes can take work proportional to the length of the list, even though the register holding it is small. Likewise, the arithmetic that implements a predicate can cost far more than the number of oracle calls suggests. A resource estimate must therefore state the gate set, the connectivity, how inputs are prepared, and how outputs are read, before an asymptotic advantage can become a claim about real machines. On a three-site line only neighboring sites interact directly. To act on logical qubits A and C, swap A next to C, apply the gate, and restore the placement if needed. Problems Three qubits sit on a line, \\(1\\)–\\(2\\)–\\(3\\), and only neighbours can interact. How many SWAP gates are needed to apply a CNOT between qubits 1 and 3 and restore the original placement? A SWAP gate costs three CNOT gates. Count the CNOT gates in the construction above, and for a CNOT between the two ends of a line of \\(n\\) qubits. A data set of \\(N=2^n\\) numbers is loaded into amplitudes using one gate per number, and then searched with about \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries. When does the quadratic query speedup survive in the total cost?"}, {"title": "Error accumulation and reliability", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-3", "text": "8.4.3 Error accumulation and reliability § quantum error correction · syndrome A gate can be synthesized accurately in mathematics and still be executed imperfectly. Approximation error concerns the chosen ideal circuit; physical noise concerns how the device actually realizes it. Both matter, but their models and remedies differ. Quantum error correction encodes logical information across several physical qubits. Syndrome measurements reveal information about errors without revealing the encoded logical amplitudes. A syndrome need not identify a unique microscopic error; it must support a correction that preserves the logical state for the errors the code handles. This does not clone the unknown input. The encoding creates a joint state, not independent copies available on separate wires. The no-cloning theorem remains intact. We stop short of constructing a fault-tolerant computer. The foundation for that subject is now visible: joint states, subspaces, measurements, classical corrections, and a resource budget. Proving an ideal algorithm correct is necessary, but reliability is a further requirement. For ideal and implemented unitary gates with operator-norm error at most ε per gate, a telescoping bound gives total error at most mε. The bound is not a claim that all errors add with the same sign in a particular run. Problems Each gate fails independently with probability \\(10^{-3}\\). What is the probability that a circuit of \\(1000\\) gates runs without any failure? How small must the failure probability per gate be for a circuit of \\(10^9\\) gates to run without failure with probability at least \\(0.9\\)? A repetition code stores a classical bit as three identical bits and decodes by majority. With independent flip probability \\(0<q<1\\), derive the failure probability and find when encoding helps. Now consider \\(\\alpha\\ket{000}+\\beta\\ket{111}\\): what does a phase flip \\(Z\\) on its first qubit do, and why does majority voting miss it?"}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-1", "text": "Do \\(H\\) followed by \\(Z\\), and \\(Z\\) followed by \\(H\\), have the same action on \\(\\ket0\\)? No. \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) gives \\(\\ket-\\); \\(Z\\) then \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket+\\). The order of the matrices must follow the order in time."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-2", "text": "Build the complete two-qubit Grover circuit for the marked item \\(10\\): the oracle from an \\(X\\) gate and a controlled-\\(Z\\), and the reflection from \\(H\\), \\(X\\), and controlled-\\(Z\\) gates. Verify that one iteration from \\(\\ket s\\) gives \\(10\\). Oracle: \\(X\\) on qubit 2, controlled-\\(Z\\), \\(X\\) on qubit 2. It sends \\(\\ket{10}\\) to \\(\\ket{11}\\), applies the sign, and restores \\(\\ket{10}\\). Starting from \\(\\ket s=\\tfrac12(1,1,1,1)\\), the oracle gives \\(\\tfrac12(1,1,-1,1)\\). The reflection circuit implements \\(-D\\), and \\(D\\) maps this state to \\(\\ket{10}\\), so the result is \\(-\\ket{10}\\): the outcome is \\(10\\) with certainty."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-3", "text": "A search over \\(N=2^n\\) items uses \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\) iterations. The oracle is compiled into \\(20n\\) gates and the reflection into \\(10n\\) gates. Give the total gate count as a function of \\(n\\), and evaluate it for \\(n=30\\). The total is about \\((\\pi/4)\\,2^{n/2}\\cdot30n\\). For \\(n=30\\): \\((\\pi/4)\\times32{,}768\\times900\\approx2.3\\times10^7\\) gates. The query count, about \\(2.6\\times10^4\\), hides a factor of \\(30n=900\\) gates per query."}, {"title": "Input size and success criteria", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-1", "text": "9.1.1 Input size and success criteria § worst case When can we say that one method is faster than another? Only when both solve the same task on the same terms: the same encoding of inputs, the same measure of input size, the same access to data, and the same standard of success. Why does the encoding matter? Size is measured by the length of the input. An integer \\(N\\) written in binary has about \\(\\log_2N\\) bits, so a procedure that takes \\(N\\) steps takes a number of steps exponential in the input length. Which cost do we report? The worst case over all inputs, the average over a stated distribution of inputs, or the expected cost of an algorithm that uses randomness. Each is legitimate, but they must not be mixed. In a query model, we must also say what one query returns. Changing the interface changes the problem, not merely the method used to solve it. Binary encoding uses logarithmically many bits. A cost proportional to the integer’s value can therefore be exponential in the number of input bits. Problems How many bits does \\(N=10^9\\) have in binary? A procedure takes about \\(\\sqrt N\\) steps. Is that polynomial in the input length? An algorithm takes \\(5\\) steps on half of the \\(n\\)-bit inputs and \\(2^n\\) steps on the other half. Give its worst-case cost and its average cost over uniformly random inputs. Trial division finds a factor of \\(N\\) by testing the divisors \\(2,3,\\dots,\\lfloor\\sqrt N\\rfloor\\). Express its worst-case number of divisions in terms of the input length \\(n=\\log_2N\\). Is it polynomial-time?"}, {"title": "Asymptotic notation", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-2", "text": "9.1.2 Asymptotic notation § asymptotic notation · \\(O(g(n))\\) · \\(\\Omega\\) · \\(\\Theta\\) To compare growth, write \\(T(n)=O(g(n))\\) when some constant \\(C>0\\) bounds \\(T(n)\\leq Cg(n)\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\). Write \\(\\Omega\\) for the corresponding lower bound and \\(\\Theta\\) when both hold. For \\(n\\geq1\\), \\[3n^2\\leq3n^2+7n+2\\leq12n^2.\\] The cost is therefore \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\). This describes the growth of that cost expression; it does not prove that every algorithm for the task needs quadratic time. Asymptotic notation suppresses constant factors, lower-order detail, and finite-size crossover behavior. It does not supply the input encoding or computational model: those must be stated separately. Every fixed polynomial eventually grows more slowly than an exponential with base greater than one. “Eventually” matters. A large exponent or constant can make a polynomial method impractical over the entire range of inputs we can use. Scaling and actual costs answer related but different questions. The linear algorithm with a large constant eventually wins, but not for every input size. Big-O notation describes growth, not a complete timing prediction. Problems Is \\(100n=O(n^2)\\)? Is \\(n^2=O(100n)\\)? Give constants, or explain why none exist. Two algorithms take exactly \\(1000n\\) and \\(n^2\\) steps. Which is faster at \\(n=100\\), \\(n=1000\\), and \\(n=10{,}000\\)? Which scales better? Explain why these are different questions. For which input lengths \\(n\\geq2\\) is an algorithm costing \\(n^{10}\\) steps faster than one costing \\(2^n\\) steps? What does this show about asymptotic comparisons?"}, {"title": "Query, gate, and space complexity", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-3", "text": "9.1.3 Query, gate, and space complexity § query complexity · gate complexity · space complexity Which resource are we counting? Query complexity counts oracle uses, gate complexity counts elementary operations, and space complexity counts memory. Physical running time depends further on scheduling, error correction, and the device. Does an advantage in one resource carry over to another? Not automatically. A quantum query advantage may survive, or disappear, once the cost of implementing each query and of preparing the input is counted. For unstructured search, the quadratic reduction in queries is a theorem. It is not a theorem that searching any stored dataset on any quantum device is quadratically faster overall. These totals count only the gates inside query calls. Preparation and other gates must still be added for a full comparison. Problems A Grover search over \\(N=10^6\\) items uses about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\) queries. If each query costs \\(1000\\) gates and each reflection \\(50\\) gates, estimate the total gate count. A classical scan costs one operation per item. For \\(N=10^6\\), below which cost \\(c\\) per query does a Grover search, costing about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\cdot c\\) gates, beat the scan? Algorithm A uses \\(n^2\\) queries and \\(n^3\\) other gates; algorithm B uses \\(n\\) queries and \\(n^4\\) other gates. If each query costs \\(n^k\\) gates, which algorithm has the smaller total gate count, depending on \\(k\\)?"}, {"title": "P, NP, and reductions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-1", "text": "9.2.1 P, NP, and reductions § decision problem · P · NP · certificate · polynomial-time reduction Return to the distinction between finding an argument and checking one. P consists of decision problems solvable in polynomial time by a deterministic algorithm. NP consists of decision problems whose yes-instances have polynomial-size certificates checkable in polynomial time. A proposed nontrivial factor of an integer illustrates the difference: division checks the candidate, but the checking procedure does not tell us how to find it. Equivalently, a nondeterministic machine can guess a certificate and accept if some branch verifies it. This says nothing about the probability that a random guess succeeds. A polynomial-time reduction from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) converts an instance of \\(A\\) into one of \\(B\\) while preserving the answer. An efficient solver for \\(B\\) would then give one for \\(A\\). Notice the direction: the solver for the target problem helps solve the source problem. Whether every efficiently verifiable decision problem is efficiently solvable—whether P equals NP—remains open. We need these definitions to state computational claims precisely, not to suggest that quantum computation has already settled them. If task \\(A\\) reduces efficiently to task \\(B\\), an efficient solver for \\(B\\) would yield one for \\(A\\). The arrow describes converting instances, not proving that such a solver exists. Problems Which of these are decision problems: (a) is \\(N\\) prime? (b) find a factor of \\(N\\); (c) does a graph \\(G\\) contain a path from \\(u\\) to \\(v\\)? For the problem “does \\(N\\) have a factor \\(d\\) with \\(1\\lt d\\lt k\\)?”, give a witness for \\(N=91\\) and \\(k=10\\), and explain how a witness is checked in polynomial time. Prove that if \\(A\\) reduces to \\(B\\) in polynomial time and \\(B\\) is in P, then \\(A\\) is in P. Why must the reduction itself run in polynomial time?"}, {"title": "BPP and BQP", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-2", "text": "9.2.2 BPP and BQP § BPP · BQP What if an algorithm may use randomness and occasionally err? BPP is the class of decision problems solved in polynomial time by a randomized classical algorithm whose probability of error is at most \\(1/3\\) on every input. Why \\(1/3\\)? Any constant below \\(1/2\\) would do: repeating the algorithm and taking the majority answer drives the error down quickly. BQP is the quantum counterpart: decision problems solved, with error at most \\(1/3\\), by a uniform family of polynomial-size quantum circuits. How do the classes compare? A quantum circuit can simulate a randomized classical computation, so \\(\\mathrm{BPP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\). Is BQP larger? This is not known, and neither is whether BQP contains NP. These are classes of decision problems; factoring is usually stated as a task whose answer is a number. Both classes concern polynomial-time decision procedures with a bounded error on every allowed input. The computational model differs. Problems An algorithm errs with probability at most \\(1/3\\). Run it three times independently and take the majority answer. What is the error probability? How many independent runs, with a majority vote, bring the error below \\(0.01\\) when each run errs with probability \\(1/3\\)? Show that an algorithm that ignores its input and answers by flipping a fair coin has error \\(1/2\\) on every input, and that majority voting over repeated runs never improves it. Why is the condition “error at most \\(1/3\\)” essential?"}, {"title": "Factoring and search", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-3", "text": "9.2.3 Factoring and search § period finding What evidence is there that quantum computers are more powerful? There are two kinds, of different strength. Factoring: quantum period finding gives a factoring algorithm whose running time is polynomial in the number of digits; its derivation lies beyond this book. No polynomial-time classical algorithm is known. No one has proved that none exists, however, so this is an advantage over known methods, not a proven separation. Search: Grover’s algorithm has a proved quadratic advantage, but only in the unstructured query model. Neither kind of evidence suggests that quantum computers speed up every task. Factoring uses arithmetic structure; unstructured search assumes only oracle access to a predicate. A lower bound for the latter is not a lower bound for every structured problem. Problems Trial division of a \\(2048\\)-bit number may test up to \\(\\sqrt N\\approx2^{1024}\\) divisors. Write this roughly as a power of ten. A rough estimate for Shor’s factoring algorithm is \\(n^3\\) operations for an \\(n\\)-bit number. Compare \\(n^3\\) with \\(2^{n/2}\\) for \\(n=2048\\). The best known classical factoring method, the number field sieve, takes time roughly \\(\\exp\\bigl(1.92(\\ln N)^{1/3}(\\ln\\ln N)^{2/3}\\bigr)\\). Evaluate it for a \\(2048\\)-bit \\(N\\), and explain why this comparison does not prove that quantum computers are exponentially more powerful."}, {"title": "Upper and lower bounds", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-1", "text": "9.3.1 Upper and lower bounds § upper bound · lower bound An upper bound is constructive: exhibit one algorithm and bound its cost. A lower bound is universal: show that every algorithm allowed by the model needs at least a certain resource. An unsuccessful search for a faster method is neither proof nor evidence sufficient for a lower bound. Query models isolate the obstacle. The hidden input can affect the computation only through oracle calls; the intervening operations must be independent of that hidden input except through what the calls make available. This restriction can make a lower-bound proof possible even when gate-by-gate analysis of every imaginable algorithm would be hopeless. Its conclusion is strong within the model. It does not determine the physical cost of a query, or automatically apply when a different interface reveals more structure. A construction proves that a task can be solved within a cost. A lower bound must cover every permitted algorithm in the stated model. Problems Which of these establishes a lower bound on query complexity: (a) an algorithm using \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries; (b) a proof that every algorithm needs at least \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries; (c) many failed attempts to find an algorithm with fewer queries? Using the fact that \\(k\\) classical queries find a single marked item among \\(N\\) with probability at most \\(k/N\\), show that success probability \\(1/2\\) needs at least \\(N/2\\) queries. In the query model, an algorithm may compute without limit between queries. Explain why a query lower bound still applies to algorithms with limited computation, but a query upper bound need not give a fast algorithm."}, {"title": "Unstructured search optimality", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-2", "text": "9.3.2 Unstructured search optimality § hybrid argument Can a quantum algorithm search faster than Grover’s? No. Finding a unique marked item among \\(N\\) with constant success probability requires \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) queries. Grover’s algorithm uses \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\), so the query complexity of the task is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). Why can it not be faster? A single query can change the state substantially only through the amplitude on the marked label, and averaged over all possible positions of the mark, that amplitude is small. The hybrid argument makes this precise; we state its conclusion as a theorem. The geometry of the Grover iteration proves an upper bound, not optimality. For one marked item and constant success in the unstructured quantum query model, Grover’s \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) upper bound and the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound give \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) query complexity. Problems For \\(N=10^6\\), compare Grover’s query count, about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\), with the classical average \\(N/2\\). If some quantum algorithm searched with only \\(N^{1/3}\\) queries, how many would it use for \\(N=10^6\\)? Which result rules this out? Use the lower bound to show that no quantum algorithm finds a marked item among \\(N=2^n\\) with a number of queries polynomial in \\(n\\). What does this imply for solving NP problems by unstructured search?"}, {"title": "Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional)", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-3", "text": "9.3.3 Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional) § approximate degree Optional derivation · polynomial lower bounds Is there another route to lower bounds? Write the hidden input of the oracle as bits \\(z_1,\\dots,z_N\\). After \\(T\\) queries, every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in these bits, and every acceptance probability is a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\). An algorithm that is correct with probability at least \\(2/3\\) therefore yields a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\) that approximates the function it computes on every Boolean input. If every such approximating polynomial needs degree at least \\(d\\), the approximate degree , then \\(2T\\geq d\\), so \\(T\\geq d/2\\). For OR on \\(N\\) bits, which detects whether any item is marked, the approximate degree is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). This gives a second proof of the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound. Appendix E explains why the degree grows by at most one per query; the approximate degree of OR is a separate theorem. Further detail: Beals et al.: Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials . The query model limits acceptance degree to \\(2T\\). If approximating the requested function requires degree \\(d\\), then \\(2T\\ge d\\). The approximate-degree theorem is a separate ingredient. Problems After \\(T=3\\) queries, what is the largest possible degree of an amplitude, and of an acceptance probability? The approximate degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits grows like \\(\\sqrt N\\). For \\(N=10^4\\), what lower bound on queries does \\(T\\geq d/2\\) give, up to a constant factor? Show that the exact degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits is \\(N\\), by writing \\(\\mathrm{OR}(z)=1-\\prod_i(1-z_i)\\). What does this imply for algorithms that must be correct with certainty?"}, {"title": "Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-1", "text": "9.4.1 Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions § What do the algorithms of this book have in common? Each uses coherent operations to make the property we want affect the probabilities of the final outcome. Deutsch’s algorithm turns the equality of two function values into a relative phase , and then into a definite bit. Amplitude amplification rotates a prepared state toward the successful outcomes. What do their advantages depend on? Specified oracles, coherent access to inverses where they are used, and a measurement chosen to expose the answer. The large state space gives room for these constructions, but size alone explains nothing: correctness is established by calculating the evolution and the readout. An advantage claim must include the access assumptions and success criterion. Expensive preparation or readout can dominate a reduction in oracle calls. Problems For Deutsch’s algorithm and for Grover search, name the property that is encoded in phases, and the operation that turns it into probability. For each of the two algorithms, list costs that its query count does not include. An algorithm prepares the uniform superposition of \\(n\\) qubits, applies an oracle once, and measures immediately. Show that it does no better than one classical query, whether the oracle is a phase oracle or the reversible \\(U_f\\)."}, {"title": "Computability and readout limitations", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-2", "text": "9.4.2 Computability and readout limitations § Quantum computation changes some resource costs without removing all limits. In the standard effective circuit model, the gates and their required approximations have computable descriptions. A classical computer can simulate the resulting finite computations, generally at enormous cost; bounded-error decisions can be resolved by sufficiently accurate simulation. This does not furnish a general halting decider. Other restrictions have different origins. Linearity prevents universal cloning. Measurement returns a sampled outcome rather than a readable list of all amplitudes. Input preparation, oracle construction, and protection from noise consume resources even when an ideal query count is small. These are not reasons to dismiss an algorithm. They tell us what a convincing claim must contain: a specified task, an allowed access model, an observable answer with a success guarantee, and the costs of producing it. A faster implementation does not turn an undecidable task into a decidable one, nor make all amplitudes available from a single measurement. Problems Can a quantum computer decide the halting problem? Why not? A classical simulation of an \\(n\\)-qubit circuit stores all \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. How does its memory grow, and why does the simulation nevertheless show that quantum computers compute nothing beyond Turing machines? A source promises to prepare either \\(\\ket0\\) on every run or \\(\\ket+\\) on every run. After obtaining \\(k\\) copies, the alternatives are \\(\\ket0^{\\otimes k}\\) and \\(\\ket+^{\\otimes k}\\). Compute their overlap. Can any finite number of copies guarantee perfect discrimination? How does that differ from making the error very small?"}, {"title": "Period finding and error correction: further topics", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-3", "text": "9.4.3 Period finding and error correction: further topics § quantum Fourier transform Two natural continuations now have a clear starting point. Period finding uses controlled operations, phase, and a quantum Fourier transform to make periodic structure observable. Error correction uses joint states, subspaces, and measurements to protect information while computations proceed. Neither requires abandoning the rules developed here. Each requires new constructions and a proof that its measurements reveal the intended information without losing what is still needed. When you meet such a construction, ask three questions: What property has been encoded in the state? Which operation makes it visible to measurement? What did preparing and protecting that process cost? If you can answer those, a large circuit becomes a chain of understandable claims. Period finding concerns making structure observable; error correction concerns protecting a computation from faults. A useful large computation needs both suitable algorithms and sufficiently reliable operations. Problems What is the period of \\(f(x)=2^x\\bmod15\\)? Use the period \\(r=4\\) of \\(2^x\\bmod15\\) to factor \\(15\\), by computing \\(\\gcd(2^{r/2}-1,15)\\) and \\(\\gcd(2^{r/2}+1,15)\\). Let \\(a^x\\bmod N\\) have even period \\(r\\), with \\(a^{r/2}\\not\\equiv-1\\pmod N\\). Show that \\(\\gcd(a^{r/2}-1,N)\\) is a factor of \\(N\\) other than \\(1\\) and \\(N\\)."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-1", "text": "An algorithm uses \\(O(N)\\) steps on an integer \\(N\\) written in binary. Is that polynomial in the input length? Not in general. The input length is about \\(\\log_2N\\), so \\(N\\) steps can be exponential in that length."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-2", "text": "One algorithm uses at most \\(3n^2+7n+2\\) operations, and at least \\(n^2\\). Give constants showing that its cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\). Does this prove that every algorithm for the task needs quadratic time? For \\(n\\geq1\\), \\(n^2\\leq3n^2+7n+2\\leq3n^2+7n^2+2n^2=12n^2\\), so the cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\) with constants \\(1\\) and \\(12\\). This describes one algorithm. A lower bound for the task would have to hold for every algorithm, which needs a separate argument."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-3", "text": "A quantum search uses \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) queries, but each query requires \\(\\Theta(N)\\) gates in a chosen implementation. What do the query and gate counts say about this implementation, and about the task? The queries alone contribute \\(\\Theta(N^{3/2})\\) gates, worse than a classical scan of \\(N\\) items. The quadratic query advantage is still a true statement about oracle access. It does not show that this implementation beats a classical method, and a cheaper oracle implementation could change the comparison."}, {"title": "Automata and Memory", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-a", "text": "A. Automata and Memory finite automaton · pushdown automaton · linear-bounded automaton How much memory does a computation need? Start with the least: a finite automaton , which reads its input once while keeping one of finitely many control states. Two states suffice to track parity, whether the number of ones read so far is even or odd: reading a one swaps the states, and reading a zero leaves them unchanged. Can a finite automaton check that a string consists of some number of \\(a\\)’s followed by the same number of \\(b\\)’s, the language \\(\\{a^nb^n:n\\geq0\\}\\)? No. A fixed automaton has finitely many states, so two different long prefixes \\(a^i\\) and \\(a^j\\) must reach the same state. Appending \\(b^i\\) then forces the same decision on \\(a^ib^i\\), which is in the language, and on \\(a^jb^i\\), which is not. This state-collision argument proves the limitation. What extra memory is enough? A stack , which can hold arbitrarily many pending symbols: push once for each \\(a\\), then pop once for each \\(b\\). Reject an \\(a\\) after the first \\(b\\), or a \\(b\\) with nothing left to pop. Accept when the input and stack run out together. A pushdown automaton formalizes this memory. A linear-bounded automaton has a writable tape no longer than a constant multiple of the input, and a Turing machine removes even that bound. Do these models form a sequence of ever better machines? Not in that sense. More memory changes which configurations are available, not what a single step means. The models describe different classes of languages; they are not successive generations of hardware. Reading \\(aabb\\) pushes for each \\(a\\) and pops for each \\(b\\). The empty stack at the end certifies matching counts in this example; checking order is also required. Problems Design a two-state finite automaton that accepts the binary strings with an even number of \\(1\\)s, and trace it on \\(1011\\). Run the stack procedure on \\(aaabbb\\) and on \\(aabbb\\), giving the stack height after each symbol. Show that no finite automaton recognizes the strings over \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) with equal numbers of \\(a\\)’s and \\(b\\)’s, in any order."}, {"title": "Grammars and Lambda Calculus", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-b", "text": "B. Grammars and Lambda Calculus grammar · lambda calculus · Church numeral Instead of recognizing strings, can we describe a set of strings by how its members are produced? A grammar does this. It specifies a start symbol, terminal symbols, nonterminal symbols, and replacement rules. The rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\), where \\(\\epsilon\\) is the empty string, generate exactly the strings of matching \\(a\\)’s and \\(b\\)’s: applying the first rule twice and then the second gives \\(aabb\\). How do grammars relate to machines? Generation asks what can be produced; recognition asks whether a given string belongs to the generated set. Regular grammars correspond to finite automata, context-free grammars to pushdown automata, context-sensitive grammars to linear-bounded automata, and unrestricted grammars to the languages a Turing machine recognizes, with the usual conventions about the empty string. Can computation be described without any machine at all? Lambda calculus uses only functions, application, and substitution. The reduction \\((\\lambda x.t)u\\to t[x:=u]\\) replaces the free occurrences of \\(x\\) in \\(t\\) by \\(u\\), renaming bound variables where necessary to avoid capture; for example, \\((\\lambda x.x)u\\to u\\). How can functions alone represent numbers? A Church numeral \\(n\\) is the function that applies a given function \\(n\\) times to a given starting value, so three is \\(\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f(f(x)))\\). Functions alone can encode data and iteration. The functions computable in lambda calculus are exactly the Turing-computable ones. That equivalence between formal models supports the broader Church–Turing thesis; it does not prove it. Apply \\(S\\to aSb\\) twice, then \\(S\\to\\) empty string. This demonstrates generation of \\(aabb\\); it does not yet give a procedure for rejecting arbitrary strings. Problems Derive \\(aaabbb\\) from \\(S\\) with the rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\), writing each step. Reduce \\((\\lambda x.\\lambda y.x)\\,a\\,b\\) step by step. With Church numerals, show that \\(\\mathrm{SUCC}=\\lambda n.\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(n\\,f\\,x)\\) applied to \\(2=\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f\\,x)\\) gives \\(3\\)."}, {"title": "Linear Algebra Reference", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-c", "text": "C. Linear Algebra Reference operator norm This appendix collects the linear algebra used in the book; each identity is explained where it is first needed. How are complex vectors compared? Through the inner product \\(\\braket uv=u^\\dagger v\\), where \\(u^\\dagger=(u^*)^T\\) is the conjugate transpose. The norm is \\(\\sqrt{u^\\dagger u}\\). An orthonormal basis matrix \\(B\\) satisfies \\(B^\\dagger B=I\\), and \\(B^\\dagger\\) converts old coordinates to new ones. In which order do matrices act? From right to left on column vectors, and the adjoint reverses the order of a product: \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). Three properties of a matrix are easily confused. A unitary preserves overlaps; a Hermitian matrix equals its adjoint; an orthogonal projector satisfies \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\). How do tensor products behave? \\((A\\otimes B)(u\\otimes v)=Au\\otimes Bv\\) and \\(\\braket{u\\otimes v}{x\\otimes y}=\\braket ux\\braket vy\\). In the binary ordering used in this book, the leftmost qubit is the most significant label. How large can a matrix make a vector? The operator norm \\(\\|A\\|=\\max_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Av\\|\\) answers this. It gives uniform error bounds, because \\(\\|(U-V)v\\|\\leq\\|U-V\\|\\) for every unit vector \\(v\\). For the complex vector \\((3,4i)\\), the squared norm is \\(9+16=25\\). Dividing by five gives a unit vector; the imaginary coordinate contributes positively to its norm. Problems What is the operator norm of \\(\\operatorname{diag}(3,1)\\), and of a unitary \\(U\\)? Verify \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\) for \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&i\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\). Show that a projector \\(P\\), with \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\), has only the eigenvalues \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), and that \\(I-P\\) is also a projector."}, {"title": "Mixed States and General Measurements", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-d", "text": "D. Mixed States and General Measurements density matrix · reduced state · effects What if we know only that a system was prepared in one of several states, \\(\\ket{\\psi_r}\\) with probability \\(q_r\\)? In general, no single state vector describes that preparation. The density matrix \\[\\rho=\\sum_rq_r\\ket{\\psi_r}\\bra{\\psi_r}\\] does. Its trace—the sum of its diagonal entries—is one. It is positive semidefinite, meaning \\(\\bra v\\rho\\ket v\\geq0\\) for every vector \\(\\ket v\\); this ensures nonnegative measurement probabilities. A pure state has \\(\\rho=\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi\\). Different ensembles can give the same density matrix, and then they give the same predictions for every measurement. The measurement rule becomes \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\), and unitary evolution sends \\(\\rho\\) to \\(U\\rho U^\\dagger\\). What is the state of one part of an entangled pair? Its reduced state \\(\\rho_A=\\operatorname{tr}_B\\rho_{AB}\\), computed by summing over the unobserved basis label: \\((\\rho_A)_{a,a^\\prime}=\\sum_b(\\rho_{AB})_{ab,a^\\prime b}\\). The same \\(b\\) appears in the row and column because we ignore which outcome occurred in \\(B\\). For either half of a Bell state , it is \\(I/2\\). Can an operation on \\(B\\) change \\(\\rho_A\\)? Not when its outcome is ignored. A trace-preserving operation on \\(B\\), with operators \\(M_r\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_rM_r^\\dagger M_r=I\\), leaves \\(\\rho_A\\) unchanged; inserting the sum into the partial trace shows this. Conditioning on the outcome \\(r\\) changes the description only for someone who knows \\(r\\). Are projective measurements the only ones? No. A general measurement has positive operators \\(E_j\\), called effects , that sum to the identity, with probabilities \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(E_j\\rho)\\). The effects fix the probabilities but not the state after the measurement; measurement operators supply that further information. Can we sometimes identify either of two nonorthogonal states with certainty, provided we allow an inconclusive result? A measurement with three outcomes makes this possible. Suppose the state is either \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\). With \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\), take \\[E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-,\\qquad E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1,\\qquad E_?=I-E_0-E_+.\\] The outcome \\(0\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket+\\), the outcome \\(+\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket0\\), and the outcome \\(?\\) is inconclusive. \\(E_?\\) is positive because the largest eigenvalue of \\(\\ket-\\bra-+\\ket1\\bra1\\) is \\(1+1/\\sqrt2\\). Whenever this measurement gives an answer, the answer is certain; the price is that it sometimes declines to answer. An equal mixture of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) and an equal mixture of \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) both yield \\(I/2\\). No measurement on the qubit alone identifies which ensemble was used. Problems Write the density matrix of the ensemble that prepares \\(\\ket0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\). What is the probability of outcome \\(0\\)? Show that the ensembles \\(\\{\\ket0,\\ket1\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\), each with equal probabilities, have the same density matrix. For the measurement with effects \\(E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-\\), \\(E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1\\), and \\(E_?=I-E_0-E_+\\), where \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\), compute the probability of the inconclusive outcome for \\(\\ket0\\) and for \\(\\ket+\\)."}, {"title": "Polynomial Lower Bounds", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-e", "text": "E. Polynomial Lower Bounds approximate degree Why must a search algorithm make many queries? In the Boolean query model, the hidden input is a string \\(z\\in\\{0,1\\}^N\\), and a query transforms \\(\\ket{i,b}\\) into \\(\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\). How does each query change the amplitudes? Every new amplitude is a combination of previous amplitudes multiplied by \\(z_i\\) or by \\(1-z_i\\). The amplitudes therefore start as constants and gain degree at most one per query. Operations that do not depend on the input only form linear combinations, which do not raise the degree. By induction, after \\(T\\) queries every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in the input bits. What about the probability of accepting? It is a sum of squared magnitudes of amplitudes, so its degree is at most \\(2T\\). On Boolean inputs, any power \\(z_i^k\\) with \\(k\\geq1\\) equals \\(z_i\\), so the polynomial can be taken to be multilinear. The approximate degree of a Boolean function is the least degree of a real polynomial that approximates it, within a fixed error below one half, on every Boolean input. An algorithm with bounded error yields such a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\), so its number of queries is at least half the approximate degree. Proving that OR has approximate degree of order \\(\\sqrt N\\) needs an argument from approximation theory, beyond this short derivation. Together with the polynomial representation, that theorem gives the lower bound for detecting a marked item. Neither follows from the observation that known algorithms are slow. Starting from amplitudes independent of hidden input bits, each Boolean query raises their degree by at most one. Squaring magnitudes gives the factor of two for acceptance probabilities. Problems With two hidden bits and the query \\(\\ket{i,b}\\mapsto\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\), start from \\(\\ket{1,0}\\). Write the amplitudes after one query as polynomials in \\(z_1,z_2\\). Use \\(z_i^2=z_i\\) on Boolean inputs to reduce \\(z_1^2z_2+z_1z_2^2\\) to a multilinear polynomial. Write the parity of two bits, \\(z_1\\oplus z_2\\), as a real polynomial, and show that its degree is \\(2\\). What lower bound on queries does this give for an exact algorithm, and is it achieved?"}, {"title": "Adjoint", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adjoint", "text": "The conjugate transpose of a matrix, written with a dagger. It is not generally the inverse."}, {"title": "Algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#algorithm", "text": "A finite set of precise instructions for carrying out a task. Whether it terminates on every allowed input must be established."}, {"title": "Amplitude", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude", "text": "A complex coefficient used to describe a quantum state or transition. The squared magnitude gives the corresponding probability in the specified measurement basis."}, {"title": "Amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude-amplification", "text": "A sequence of reflections that increases the amplitude in a designated successful subspace, given suitable preparation and inverse operations."}, {"title": "Bell state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bell-state", "text": "One of four maximally entangled two-qubit basis states. The book begins with the equal superposition of 00 and 11."}, {"title": "Boolean function", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#boolean-function", "text": "A function whose inputs and outputs are bits. A truth table specifies its value for every input."}, {"title": "BQP", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bqp", "text": "The class of decision problems solvable by uniform polynomial-size quantum circuits with bounded error."}, {"title": "Circuit depth", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#circuit-depth", "text": "The number of sequential layers of operations when compatible operations can run in parallel."}, {"title": "Computability", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#computability", "text": "Whether a task can be performed by an effective procedure, without imposing an efficiency requirement."}, {"title": "Consistency", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#consistency", "text": "A formal theory is consistent if its rules cannot prove both a statement and its negation."}, {"title": "Decision problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#decision-problem", "text": "A problem whose required answer is yes or no for each allowed input."}, {"title": "Entanglement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#entanglement", "text": "For a pure joint state, the inability to write it as a product of subsystem states. Mixed-state entanglement requires a broader definition."}, {"title": "Global phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#global-phase", "text": "A common phase factor multiplying an entire state vector. It leaves all measurement probabilities unchanged."}, {"title": "Grover iteration", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#grover-iteration", "text": "A phase reversal on marked items followed by a reflection about the prepared uniform state in the standard search algorithm."}, {"title": "Halting problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#halting-problem", "text": "The task of deciding, for every program and input, whether the program eventually stops. No algorithm solves it in full generality."}, {"title": "Hermitian", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hermitian", "text": "An operator equal to its adjoint. Its eigenvalues are real and its eigenspaces can describe a projective measurement."}, {"title": "Inner product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#inner-product", "text": "A scalar obtained from two vectors by conjugating the coordinates of the first and summing their products with the second. It measures their overlap."}, {"title": "Interference", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#interference", "text": "The addition of amplitudes for indistinguishable alternatives, which can increase or decrease an outcome probability."}, {"title": "Lower bound", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#lower-bound", "text": "A limit on the resources required by every algorithm within a specified model, rather than the cost of one construction."}, {"title": "NAND", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#nand", "text": "The Boolean operation that returns zero exactly when both input bits are one. NAND gates suffice to build every Boolean function."}, {"title": "Normalization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#normalization", "text": "The requirement that a state vector has squared norm one, so the probabilities of a complete basis measurement sum to one."}, {"title": "Oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#oracle", "text": "A specified operation that provides access to a function. Counting its uses does not by itself account for the cost of building it."}, {"title": "Phase kickback", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-kickback", "text": "When a controlled operation acts on a target eigenstate, its eigenvalue appears as a phase on the corresponding control component."}, {"title": "Product state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-state", "text": "A pure joint state that can be written as a tensor product of one state for each subsystem."}, {"title": "Projective measurement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#projective-measurement", "text": "A measurement described by mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity. Each outcome probability is the squared norm of the projected state."}, {"title": "Quantum advantage", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-advantage", "text": "An improvement over classical computation for a specified task, resource measure, and success guarantee. Its assumptions must be stated."}, {"title": "Quantum circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-circuit", "text": "An ordered description of operations on quantum registers, with any measurements and classical control specified."}, {"title": "Qubit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#qubit", "text": "A two-state quantum system. Its state is described by two amplitudes, one for each of the labels zero and one."}, {"title": "Query complexity", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query-complexity", "text": "The number of oracle calls required for a task under a stated success criterion and oracle model."}, {"title": "Relative phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#relative-phase", "text": "The phase difference between two components of a state. It can affect probabilities after further operations."}, {"title": "Reversible", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#reversible", "text": "A transformation is reversible when its output determines its input uniquely on the allowed state space."}, {"title": "Superposition", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#superposition", "text": "A linear combination of basis states. Its components depend on the chosen basis; measurement does not reveal all coefficients at once."}, {"title": "Tensor product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#tensor-product", "text": "The construction of a joint vector space from component spaces. Basis labels combine in pairs and dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Transition weight", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#transition-weight", "text": "The number attached to an arrow from one configuration to another: 1 for a deterministic or allowed arrow, a probability for a probabilistic rule, a complex amplitude for a quantum operation."}, {"title": "Turing machine", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#turing-machine", "text": "A mathematical model with a tape, a read/write head, and finitely many control states. A transition rule specifies each step."}, {"title": "Uncomputation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#uncomputation", "text": "Applying the inverse of a computation to clear temporary workspace while retaining the needed result or phase."}, {"title": "Unitary", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#unitary", "text": "An operator whose adjoint is its inverse. Unitary transformations preserve inner products and describe closed-system quantum evolution."}, {"title": "Universal gate set", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#universal-gate-set", "text": "A set of gates sufficient to construct arbitrary quantum operations in the stated model, exactly or to a specified approximation."}, {"title": "Notation: not P", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-not-p", "text": "¬ P The negation of the sentence \\(P\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: f from n-bit strings to bits", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-f-from-n-bit-strings-to-bits", "text": "f:{0,1}ⁿ→{0,1} A Boolean function: one yes/no answer for each \\(n\\)-bit input string."}, {"title": "Notation: a XOR b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-xor-b", "text": "a⊕ b Exclusive OR: one when exactly one input is one. It is addition modulo two."}, {"title": "Notation: a and b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-and-b", "text": "a∧ b AND of two bits: one only when both are one."}, {"title": "Notation: k B T log two", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-k-b-t-log-two", "text": "k_BTln 2 Landauer’s minimum heat for erasing one equally likely bit at temperature \\(T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: x maps to y", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-maps-to-y", "text": "x↦ y The rule that sends each input \\(x\\) to the output \\(y\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: U sub f", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-u-sub-f", "text": "U_f The reversible oracle for \\(f\\): it keeps \\(x\\) and XORs \\(f(x)\\) into a target, \\(U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,y\\oplus f(x)}\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sum over j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sum-over-j", "text": "Σⱼ Add the following term for every value of \\(j\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: probability of j given i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-probability-of-j-given-i", "text": "P(j| i) Conditional probability: the chance of next state \\(j\\) when the current state is \\(i\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: r e to the i phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-r-e-to-the-i-phi", "text": "re^(iφ) An amplitude with magnitude \\(r\\ge0\\) and phase \\(\\phi\\). Its outcome probability is \\(r^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-i", "text": "i The imaginary unit, with \\(i^2=-1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: z star", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-z-star", "text": "z* The complex conjugate: for \\(z=a+ib\\), \\(z^*=a-ib\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: magnitude of z", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-magnitude-of-z", "text": "|z| The size of a complex number. \\(|z|^2=z^*z\\) turns an amplitude into a probability weight."}, {"title": "Notation: v transpose", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-v-transpose", "text": "vᵀ Turns a row into a column or a column into a row; \\((a,b)^T\\) is a column vector."}, {"title": "Notation: ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-psi", "text": "|ψ⟩ The column vector of a state, named \\(\\psi\\). The brackets name a vector; they are not an operation."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero, ket one", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-ket-one", "text": "|0⟩, |1⟩ The computational basis: \\(\\ket0=(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\ket1=(0,1)^T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi", "text": "⟨φ| The conjugate-transpose row of \\(\\ket\\phi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi-ket-psi", "text": "⟨φ|ψ⟩ The complex inner product, or overlap, \\(\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket plus, ket minus", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-plus-ket-minus", "text": "|±⟩ The plus/minus basis, \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: norm of v", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-norm-of-v", "text": "|v| The length of a vector, \\(\\sqrt{\\braket vv}\\). A state vector has norm one."}, {"title": "Notation: ket a tensor ket b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-a-tensor-ket-b", "text": "|a⟩⊗|b⟩ The joint state of two systems, also written \\(\\ket{ab}\\). Dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Notation: Bell state phi plus", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bell-state-phi-plus", "text": "|Φ⁺⟩ The entangled state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero to the n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-to-the-n", "text": "|0ⁿ⟩ All \\(n\\) qubits in state zero."}, {"title": "Notation: identity", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-identity", "text": "I The matrix that leaves every vector unchanged."}, {"title": "Notation: A dagger", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-dagger", "text": "A† The adjoint: the conjugate transpose \\((A^*)^T\\), defined by \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: diagonal a, b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-diagonal-a-b", "text": "diag(a,b) The diagonal matrix with entries \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket e bra e", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-e-bra-e", "text": "|e⟩⟨e| The projector onto the unit vector \\(\\ket e\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: P sub j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-p-sub-j", "text": "Pⱼ The projector for measurement outcome \\(j\\). The Born rule gives \\(p(j)=\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: lambda", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-lambda", "text": "λ An eigenvalue: \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: X, Z, H", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-z-h", "text": "X, Z, H The bit flip, phase flip, and Hadamard gates."}, {"title": "Notation: A tensor n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-tensor-n", "text": "A^(⊗ n) \\(n\\) copies combined by the tensor product. \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) applies \\(H\\) to each of \\(n\\) qubits; \\(\\ket\\psi^{\\otimes2}\\) is two copies of \\(\\ket\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: rho", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-rho", "text": "ρ A density matrix, describing a preparation that need not be pure."}, {"title": "Notation: trace", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-trace", "text": "tr The sum of diagonal entries. With \\(\\rho\\), the Born rule is \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: minus one to the f of x", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-minus-one-to-the-f-of-x", "text": "(−1)^(f(x)) The sign a phase oracle attaches to input \\(x\\): \\(+1\\) when \\(f(x)=0\\), \\(-1\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sine squared theta equals M over N", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sine-squared-theta-equals-m-over-n", "text": "sin²θ=M/N Defines the search angle \\(\\theta\\) for \\(M\\) marked items among \\(N\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: big O of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-o-of-g-of-n", "text": "O(g(n)) An eventual upper bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big omega of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-omega-of-g-of-n", "text": "Ω(g(n)) An eventual lower bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big theta of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-theta-of-g-of-n", "text": "Θ(g(n)) Both an upper and a lower bound: growth exactly like \\(g(n)\\) up to constants."}, {"title": "Notation: BPP is contained in BQP", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bpp-is-contained-in-bqp", "text": "BPP⊆BQP Every problem in the first class is also in the second."}, {"title": "Notation: lambda x dot t, applied to u", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-lambda-x-dot-t-applied-to-u", "text": "(λ x.t)u In lambda calculus, a function with parameter \\(x\\) and body \\(t\\), applied to \\(u\\)."}];
